2200:
** ζεστός**, ή, όν,
(ζέω) **seethed, boiled**, κρέα ζ. καὶ ὀπτά {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Ἰβηρική” 85.}}
**II** **hot**, ὕδωρ {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “fragment” 70.11, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.33, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.50}}; ὕδατα ζ., of hot springs, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 12.8.17}}; opposed to χλιαρός, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 1.101; ψάμμος 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 6.23}}; λίθος Ps.-{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Fluv.” 1.2}}; εἰ δὲ -οτέρας κράσεως δέοιντο if they want the oil-bath **hotter**, {{NT+1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Herodotus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 10.37.12: _metaphorically_, χλιαρὸς εἶ καὶ οὔτε ζ. οὔτε ψυχρός NT.Rev.3.15.}}

2201:
** ζεῦγος**, εος, τό,
(ζεύγνυμι) **yoke of beasts, pair** or **team of mules, oxen** or **horses**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.543, “IG” 5(2).3.1 (4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus, 4th c.BC), etc.}}; ζ. ἵππων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.26.}}
**2** **carriage drawn by a yoke of beasts, a chariot**, ζεύγεϊ κομίζεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.31}}; ἐπὶ ζευγέων ἐλάσασαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 199; ἐπὶ ζεύγους ἄγειν 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.45; τῷ ζεύγει ὃ ἦγεν τὴν γυναῖκα 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “pro Lycophrone” 5}}; **wagons**, ζεύγεσι τοὺς λίθους ἄγουσι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 124 (4th c.BC)}}; βοεικά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.128}}; **racing-car**,= τέθριππον, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 5.50; ζ. τέθριππον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 346; ἢ συνωρίδι ἢ ζεύγει νενίκηκεν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 36d (but ={συνωρίς}, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.146d)}}]; ζ. μίσθιον a hired **chariot**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ant.” 6}}; λευκὸν ζ. **with** white **horses**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.158.}}
**II** **pair** or **couple** of any things, ἰρήκων, αἰγυπιῶν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.76}}; πεδέων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 130; Ἀτρειδᾶν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 44 (_anapaest meter_); ἐμβάδων 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 872, compare 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 7.51; ἐνωτίων “BGU” 1050.9 (Aug.), etc.; κεράμου “IG” 12.313.23, +others}}; [κεράμων] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 245 G i36 (Delph., 4th c.BC)}}; θυρῶν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 12.313.110; ταὧν 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 205; καλλιπύγων 3rd c.BC: Cercidas Iambographus 14}}: _absolutely_, **married couple**, τὸ ζ., ὃ καλεῖται θῆλυ καὶ ἄρρεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 7.18}}; τὸ ἐρωτικὸν ζ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 11}}; κατὰ ζεῦγος in **pairs**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.93d; ἐς ζεύγεα 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Syr.D.” 12.}}
**II.2** **mouthpiece of a double flute**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.11.4, 6.}}
**II.3** ζ. ἱματίων **suit** of clothes, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“BGU” 814.9 (3rd c.AD).}}
**III** incorrectly also of **more than two things** or **persons joined together**, ζ. τριπάρθενον three maiden sisters, of the Graces, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 357; so ζ. τρίδουλον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 576}}; ζ. νεκρῶν, where parents and children are spoken of, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 454.}}
**IV** = Latin _jugerum,_ {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-6th c.AD: Codex Justinianus 10.27.2.8.}}

2202:
Related to: ** ζεύγλ-η**, ἡ,
**loop attached to the yoke** (ζυγόν), through which the beasts' heads were put, χαίτη ζεύγλης ἐξεριποῦσα παρὰ ζυγόν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.440; ἔζευξα.. ἐν ζυγοῖσι κνώδαλα ζεύγλαισι δουλεύοντα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 463}}; ὑποδύντες ὑπὸ τὴν ζ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.31; βόας ζεύγλᾳ πέλασσεν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.227}}; ὑπάγειν τοὺς ἵππους τῇ ζ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMar.” 6.2.}}
**2** ={ζεῦγος}{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|1.1, “BGU” 1507 (3rd c.BC).}} —Not found in good _Attic dialect_ Prose.
**II** **cross-bar** of the double rudder, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1536.}}

2203:
** Ζεύς**, ὁ,
_nominative_ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.175, +others}}, once written Ζηύς {{Refs|“IG” 12(3).1313 (from Thera), but Ζεύς [prev. work]1316, +others}}; _Boeotian dialect_ Δεύς (which see); _vocative_ Ζεῦ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.503, etc.; _genitive_ Διϝός “BMus.Inscription” 952 (Cephallenia, 6th c.BC), Διός 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.63, etc.; _dative_ Διϝί “Schwyzer” 80 (Argive, from Olympia, 5th c.BC), Διί 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.578, +others, “IG” 12.80.12 (5th c.BC), etc., _contraction_ Δί [ῑ] 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.106, “SIG” 9,35 (Elis, 6th c.BC, Syrac., 5th c.BC, from Olympia)}}; late Δεΐ {{Refs|“JHS” 32.167 (from Pisidia), etc.}}; _accusative_ Δία, rarer than Διός, Διί in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.394, +others)}}, frequently later (cf. Sanskrit _dyaús_, _genitive_ **divás**, _locative_ **diví** 'sky', 'heaven', 'day', _locative_ also **dyávi**,= Latin “Jove”, _accusative_ **dyā´m**,= Latin _diem_,= Gr. Ζῆν (see. below)): also _nominative_ **Ζήν** probably in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 162 (Lyric poetry)}}; _genitive_ _dative_ _accusative_ Ζηνός, Ζηνί, Ζῆνα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.408, 2.49, 14.157, +others, frequently in Trag. (Comedy texts only in Trag. phrases)}}; Coan Ζηνί {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 1025.24 (4th-3rd c.BC)}}; _accusative_ Ζῆν (Ζῆν᾽ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Aristarchus Grammaticus) 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.206, 14.265, 24.331, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 884, at end of verse, before vowel in next verse (stem Ζην- probably originated in _accusative_ _sing._)}}; Cretan dialect Ττηνός, Ττηνί, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“GDI” 5024.23, 77, Τῆνα, Τηνί, [prev. work]5039.11, 5145.12, Δῆνα “SIG” 527.17 (3rd c.BC)}}; _nominative_ Δήν Hdn.Gr.2.911:—_Doric dialect_ and _Attic dialect_-_Ionic dialect_ forms with α (of doubtful origin), _nominative_ **Ζάν** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus cited in 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “Vita Pythagorae - Porphyrii Opuscula” 17, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 570; _genitive_ Ζανός “Schwyzer” 696 (Chios, 4th c.BC), 3rd c.BC: Cercidas Iambographus 1.7, 3rd c.BC: Philoxenus Epigrammaticus 3.10, “IG” 5(1).407 (Sparta, 2nd c.AD)}}; Ζανός and Ζανί, {{Refs|“Lyrica Adespota” 82A, B (from Ionic)}}; _accusative_ Ζᾶνα {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 10.6P., cf. Euhem. 24J. (“FGrH” 63)}}; _nominative_ **Ζάς** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Pherecydes Syrius Philosophus 1, 2 (Ζής cited in Hdn.Gr. [same place]), Ζάς Ζαντός 4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus “Scholia in Theodosii Canones” 1.116}}; **Δάν** (which see); **Τάν** Head {{Refs|“Hist.Num.” 2469 (from Crete)}}; _nominative_ **Δίς** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Rhinthon Comicus 14, Hdn.Gr. [same place]:—oblative cases Ζεός, Ζεΐ, Ζέα, cited by 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 1.177, 195}}; Ζεῦν _falsa lectio_ for Ζῆν᾽ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschrio Lyricus 8.5: the _plural_ Δίες, Δίας, Διῶν, Δισί, 2nd c.AD: Aelius Dionysius Grammaticus “fragment” 127; τοὺς κτησίους Δίας 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 11.473b; Δίες καὶ Ζῆνες “Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 2.191; Elean Ζᾶνες 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 5.21.2:— “Zeus”, the sky-god, ὔει μὲν ὀ Z. 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 34, compare “SIG” 93.34 (5th c.BC), 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 14.12, etc.; Ζεῦ ἄλλοι τε θεοί 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.476}}; ὦ Ζεῦ καὶ πάντες θεοί, ὦ Ζεῦ καὶ θεοί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.2.10, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1, etc.; Ζεῦ Ζεῦ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 246, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 323 (Lyric poetry, probable reading); ὦ Ζεῦ βασιλεῦ, τῆς λεπτότητος τῶν φρενῶν [prev. author] “Nu.” 153}}; in oaths, οὐ μὰ Ζῆνα, twice in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.43, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.339: frequently in Comedy texts and Prose, οὐ μὰ Δία 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 193, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 426b (with _Article_, μὰ τὸν Δί᾽, οὐ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 169, +others); ναὶ μὰ Δία [prev. author] “Ach.” 88, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.7.14}}; νὴ τὸν Δία or νὴ Δία, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 217, “Eq.” 319, etc.; compare νηδ; πρὸς τοῦ Διός [prev. author] “Av.” 130; πρὸς Διός 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.7.32}}; οὐ τὸν Δία alone, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 986}}: proverbial of enormous wealth, τῷ Διὶ πλούτου πέρι ἐρίζειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.49.}}
**II** of other deities, Ζ. καταχθόνιος,= Πλούτων, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.457; Ζ. χθόνιος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1606, “SIG” 1024.25 (Myconos, 3rd-2nd c.BC)}}; of non-Greek divinities, Ζ. Ἄμμων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.16, etc.}}; frequently of Semitic Baalim, Z. Βεελβώσωρος, etc., {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“OGI” 620 (Gerasa, 1st c.AD)), etc.}}; Z. Ὠρομάσδης,= {{Refs 1st c.BC+|4th c.AD: Perses Epigrammaticus “Ahuramazda”, [prev. work]383.41 (Nemrud Dagh, 1st c.BC).}}
**III** of persons, ὁ σχινοκέφαλος Z., iron. of Pericles, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 71}}; in flattery of kings, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.56 (of Xerxes)}}; Ξέρξης ὁ τῶν Περσῶν Z. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Gorgias Rhetor et Sophista “fragment” 5a 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator; [ἱερεὺς] Σελεύκου Διὸς Νικάτορος “OGI” 245.10 (2nd c.BC)}}; of the Roman emperors, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 1.3}}; Νέρων Z. Ἐλευθέριος {{Refs|“IG” 7.2713.41 (from Acraephiae), etc.}}; Ζῆνα τὸν Αἰνεάδην {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.307 (1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus).}}
**IV** Διὸς ἀστήρ the **planet Jupiter**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epinomis” 987c, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 343b30, etc.; so Ζεύς “Placita Philosophorum” 2.32.1, 2nd c.AD: Cleomedes Astronomus 2.7}}; Διὸς ἡμέρα a day of the week, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 37.19.}}
**V** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus}} name for the **monad,** {{Refs|Theol.Ar. 12.}}

2204:
** ζέω**,
_contraction_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ζεῖ even in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.362}}; later _Epic dialect_ **ζείω** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Dianam” 60}}_subjunctive_ ζείῃσι Epic. in {{Refs|“Arch.Papyrus” 7p.7}}; in late Prose **ζέννυμι** (which see): _imperfect_ ζέε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.365, ἔζεε 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 695, ἔζει 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 434:}}_future_ ζέσω (ἐξανα-) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 372}}: _aorist_ ἔζεσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.188, compare ἐπιζέω}}; _Epic dialect_ ζέσσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.349:}} —_passive_, _aorist_ ἐζέσθην (ἀπ-) {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.3, (ἐν-) 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “ὀξέων νούσων θεραπευτικόν” 1.2}}: _perfect_ ἔζεσμαι {{Refs|“Geoponica” 10.54.3}}:—**boil, seethe**, of water, ἐπεὶ δὴ ζέσσεν ὕδωρ ἐνὶ ἤνοπι χαλκῷ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.349, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.360}}; ὡς δὲ λέβης ζεῖ ἔνδον as the kettle **boils**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.362, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 343}}; rarely of solids, **to be fiery hot**, χθὼν ἔζεε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 695, 847; χαλκός Callias Epigrammaticus [prev. cited]}}
**2** **ferment**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 11; γλεύκους ζέοντος 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 5.8.}}
**3** _metaphorically_, **boil** or **bubble up**, τῆς θαλάσσης ζεσάσης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.188; αἷμα διὰ χρωτὸς ζέσσ᾽ “Anthologia Graeca” 7.208 (from Anyte)}}; οἶνος ζεῖ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 773d.}}
**b** of passion, ὁπηνίκ᾽ ἔζει θυμός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 434, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 440c, etc.; τὸ ζέον τῆς μάχης 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 1.33.}}
**4** with _genitive_, **boil up** or **over with** a thing, λίμνη ζέουσα ὕδατος καὶ πηλοῦ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 113a}}; πίθος ζ. [οἴνου] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 9.17.3}}; πεδία ζείοντ᾽ Ἀγαρηνῶν **boiling, teeming with**.., {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“APl.” 4.39 (6th c.AD: Arabius Epigrammaticus)}}; of persons, ζ. σκωλήκων {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 59: with _dative_, ζ. φθειρί [prev. author] “Sat.” 26; ζ. φλογμῷ 3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 690; θάλαττα αἵυατι καὶ ῥοθίῳ ζέουσα 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 1.142J.}}
**II** causal, **make to boil, boil**, τοὶ δὲ λοετρὰ πυρὶ ζέον {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.273}}; θυμὸν ἐπὶ Τροίῃ πόσον ἔζεσα; {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 7.385 (1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus).}}
**II.2** **exhale**, ἀϋτμήν (variant{-μῇ}) {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.734. (ζέ(ς) -ω, compare ζεσ-τός}}, Sanskrit _yásati_ 'boil', OHG.**jësan** 'ferment', 'foam', Engl. **yeast.**)

2205:
** ζῆλος**, ου, ὁ,
later εος, τό, {{NT|NT.Phil.3.6}} codices _optative_; _Doric dialect_ **ζᾶλος** {{Refs|“IG” 12 (5).891, etc.}}:— **jealousy** (= φθόνος), {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 195, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1526: coupled with φθόνος by 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 191, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.48, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 47e, 50c, “Lg.” 679c (_plural_); εἰς ζῆλον ἰέναι [prev. author] “R.” 550e}}: more usually in good sense, **eager rivalry, emulation**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Mx.” 242a, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1388a30.}}
**2** with _genitive_ _person_, **zeal for** one, ξυναίμων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 943}}; κατὰ ζῆλον Ἡρακλέους in **emulation** of him, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Theseus” 25; ζ. πρός τινα 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Dem.Enc.” 57}}: _absolutely_, **passion,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|PGrenf. 1.1.13 (2nd c.BC).}}
**3** with _genitive_ _of things_, ζῆλον.. γάμων ἔχουσα causing **rivalry** for my hand, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 352; ζ. ἀζήλων καὶ φόβον ἀφόβων 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ οἰκονομίας” p.66J.}}; ζ. τῶν ἀρίστων **emulous desire** for.., opposed to φυγὴ τῶν χειρόνων, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Ind.” 17; ἀνδραγαθίας 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Coriolanus” 4; so ζ. πρός τι 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.53S., 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 2; ζ. περὶ τὰ στρατιωτικά 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 14.2.27}}: _plural_, **ambitions**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.54S.}}
**4** **fervour, zeal**, {{LXX+NT|LXX.2Ki.19.31, +others, NT.1Cor.14.1, +others}}; **indignation**, ζ. πυρός {{NT|NT.Heb.10.27.}}
**5** personified as son of Styx, brother of Βία, Κράτος, Νίκη, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 384.}}
**II** **pride, honour, glory**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 503; ζ. καὶ χαρά 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.217; τὸν αὐτὸν ἔχει ζῆλον ὁ στέφανος, [prev. work]120; ζῆλον καὶ τιμὴν φέρει τῇ πόλει [prev. author] 23.64, compare 18.273, 60.33.}}
**III** **spirit**, τῆς πολιτείας {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.27.8}}: _plural_, **tastes, interests**, τοῖς ἀπὸ διαφόρων ἐπιτηδευμάτων, βίων, ζήλων, ἡλικιῶν, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 7.4.}}
**III.2** especially in Lit. Crit., **style**, τοῦ Ἀσιανοῦ λεγομένου ζήλου {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 14.1.41, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 2.}}

2206:
** ζηλ-όω**,
(ζῆλος):
**I** with _accusative_ _person_, **vie with, emulate**, τινα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.37, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 553a, “Michel” 1007.29 (Teos, 2nd c.BC)}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.125S., etc.: c._accusative_ _of things_, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.64}}; in bad sense, **to be jealous of, envy**, ζηλοῖ δέ τε γείτονα γείτων {{LXX+8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 23, compare “hymnus ad Cererem” 168, 223, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 6.27; τὴν αὑτοῦ γυναῖκα LXX.Sir.9.1}}: _absolutely_, **to be jealous**, {{NT|NT.1Cor.13.4; ζηλώσαντες}}**through jealousy,** {{NT|NT.Acts.7.9.}}
**b** with _accusative_ _person_, **to be jealous for**, {{LXX|LXX.Num.11.29.}}
**2** **esteem** or **pronounce happy, admire, praise**, τινά τινος one for a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1027, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.91; ζηλῶ σε τῆς εὐβουλίας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1008 (Lyric poetry); τῆς εὐγλωττίας [prev. author] “Eq.” 837; τῆς εὐτυχίας τὸν πρέσβυν [prev. author] “V.” 1450 (Lyric poetry); τοῦ πλούτου 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.45; τινὰ ἐπί τισι “IG” 12(5).860.47 (Tenos, 1st c.BC)}}: more rarely, ζ. τινά τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 552}}; ζ. σε ὁθούνεκα.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 332}}; τὴν πόλιν, ὅτι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.5.45}}; πολλά σε ζηλῶ βίου, μάλιστα δ᾽ εἰ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 584}}: with _participle_, σε ζ. θανόντα πρὶν κακῶν ἰδεῖν βάθος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 712 (troch.), compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 521: iron., ζηλῶ σε}} happy in your ignorance! {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Med.” 60; ὑμῶν οὐ ζηλοῦμεν τὸ ἄφρον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.105}}:—_passive_, **to be deemed fortunate**, ὑπό τινων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 232a.}}
**II** with _accusative_ _of things_, **desire emulously, strive after, affect**, ὁ μὲν δόξης ἐπιθυμεῖ καὶ τοῦτ᾽ ἐζήλωκε {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.15; ἀρετήν [prev. author] 20.141; ἀστρολογίαν 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 2p.53U.; μάθησιν “PSI” 1.94.9 (2nd c.AD)}}; πίστιν {{Refs|“Codex Justinianus [4th-6th c.AD]” 1.1.3.2}}:—_passive_, ἡ ἀρετὴ ὑπὸ πάντων τῶν ἀνθρώπων ζηλοῦται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.26; τὰ ζηλούμενα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1360b34.}}
**III** also of persons, **pay zealous court to,** {{NT|NT.Gal.4.17:—}}_passive_ {{Refs|[prev. work]18.}}

2207:
** ζηλ-ωτής**, οῦ, ὁ,
**emulator, zealous admirer** or **follower**, μιμητὴς καὶ ζ. τῆς πατρῴας ἀρετῆς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.11; ζ. καὶ ἐρασταὶ τῆς Λακεδαιμονίων παιδείας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 343a; τῆς ἡλικίας τοῦ μειρακίου 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.166; τῶν καλῶν βουλευμάτων [prev. work]171; τῆς αὐτῆς αἱρέσεως “SIG” 675.27 (Oropus, 2nd c.BC)}}; μαθήσεως {{NT+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.262S.; πνευμάτων NT.1Cor.14.12; τῶν ἀγαθῶν τῶν εἰς τὴν πόλιν μαρτυρουμένων “IG” 7.2712.99 (from Acraephiae): with _genitive_ _person_, τοῦ Διός 1st c.AD: Musonius Philosophus “fragment” 8p.37H.; τῶ πράτω θεῶ Sthenidas Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.7.63 (_nominative_ _sing._ ζηλωτάς codices)}}; Θουκυδίδου, Ἀντισθένους, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 15, “Herm.” 14}}; perhaps **champion**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 70G.}}
**2** **jealous**, θεὸς ζ. {{LXX|LXX.Exo.20.5.}}
**II** **zealot**, used to translate Κανανίτης or Καναναῖος, {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Luke.6.15, NT.Act.1.13, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 4.3.9; τῶν πατρίων ἐθῶν [prev. author] “AJ” 12.6.2; τῶν νόμων LXX.2Mac.4.2.}}

2208:
** ζηλ-ωτής**, οῦ, ὁ,
**emulator, zealous admirer** or **follower**, μιμητὴς καὶ ζ. τῆς πατρῴας ἀρετῆς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.11; ζ. καὶ ἐρασταὶ τῆς Λακεδαιμονίων παιδείας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 343a; τῆς ἡλικίας τοῦ μειρακίου 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.166; τῶν καλῶν βουλευμάτων [prev. work]171; τῆς αὐτῆς αἱρέσεως “SIG” 675.27 (Oropus, 2nd c.BC)}}; μαθήσεως {{NT+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.262S.; πνευμάτων NT.1Cor.14.12; τῶν ἀγαθῶν τῶν εἰς τὴν πόλιν μαρτυρουμένων “IG” 7.2712.99 (from Acraephiae): with _genitive_ _person_, τοῦ Διός 1st c.AD: Musonius Philosophus “fragment” 8p.37H.; τῶ πράτω θεῶ Sthenidas Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.7.63 (_nominative_ _sing._ ζηλωτάς codices)}}; Θουκυδίδου, Ἀντισθένους, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 15, “Herm.” 14}}; perhaps **champion**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 70G.}}
**2** **jealous**, θεὸς ζ. {{LXX|LXX.Exo.20.5.}}
**II** **zealot**, used to translate Κανανίτης or Καναναῖος, {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Luke.6.15, NT.Act.1.13, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 4.3.9; τῶν πατρίων ἐθῶν [prev. author] “AJ” 12.6.2; τῶν νόμων LXX.2Mac.4.2.}}

2209:
** ζημία**,
_Doric dialect_ **ζᾱμία** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|(“SIG” 239 D iii5 (Delph., 4th c.BC)}}, etc., later **σαμία** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Delph.” 3(1).342 (2nd c.BC), compare ταμία, ἀττάμιος)}}, ἡ, **loss, damage**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 148}}; opposed to κέρδος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.12, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 835b, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1132b12}}; ζημίαν or -ίας λαβεῖν to sustain **loss**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 807, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 11.11; ζ. ποιεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1124; ζ. ἐργάζεσθαι 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 6.20 (unless in signification 1.2); ζ. φέρειντῇ πόλει 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lg.”}}[same place]; ζ. εἶναι νομίζειν consider **as loss**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.50, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 7.23; ζ. πλείονα ὑπομένειν τῆς τιμῆς “PFlor.” 142.8 (3rd c.AD).}}
**2** ζ. ἐργάζεσθαι, of a slave, be guilty of a **delict**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 6.20 (see. above), 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “adv. Athenogenem” 22.}}
**II** **penalty** in money, **fine**, ζημίην ἀποτίνειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.65, compare “PHal.” 1.195 (3rd c.BC)}}; ἐκτίνειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 774e; ἱρὴν ζ. ὀφείλειν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.52; ζ. καταβάλλειν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.83, compare “SIG” [same place]; μετὰ.. χρημάτων ζημίας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 862d; ζ. ἐπέκειτο στατήρ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.70; ζημίαν ὀφείλειν τάλαντον 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lysander” 27; τῆς ζ. ἀφεθῆναι [prev. author] “Arist.” 4.}}
**II.2** generally, **penalty**, ζ. ἐπιτιθέναι τινί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.144; ζ. ἔπεστί τινι [prev. author] 2.136; πρόσκειταί τινι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Vectigalibus” 4.21; γλώσσῃ ζ. προστρίβεται 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 331, compare 384}}; with the penalty added, θάνατον ζ. ἐπιθέσθαι, προθεῖναι, τάξαι, to make death **the penalty**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.24, 3.44, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.135; θάνατος ἡ ζ. ἐπίκειται 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.38, compare 65}}; but ἐφ᾽ οἷς.. θάνατος ἡ ζ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 325b: in _plural_, θανάτου ζημίαι πρόκεινται 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.45 (variant): with _genitive_ criminis, ζ. ἀδικίας}} **penalty for**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 176d, compare “Lg.” 860e (_plural_).}}
**b** simply, **expense**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 717.81 (2nd-1st c.BC), “PLond.” 5.1660.10, 1674.23 (_plural_, 6th c.AD).}}
**III** of what is bought too dearly, a **bad bargain**, a **dead loss**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.3.2}}: usually with _adjective_, φανερὰ ζᾱμία {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 737}}; καθαρὰ ζ., λαμπρὰ ζ., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.21, 38, compare 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 56.6.}}

2210:
** ζημι-όω**,
_future_ **-ώσω**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.48}}: _aorist 1_ ἐζημίωσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 578, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.65, etc.}}: _perfect_ ἐζημίωκα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.49}}:—_passive_, _future_ ζημιωθήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 29.4, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 10.16, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.9.12}}; more frequently _middle_ ζημιώσομαι in _passive_ sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.39, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.72, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.40, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 18.37, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.27, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1320a10}}: _aorist_ ἐζημιώθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 855b, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.160}}: _perfect_ ἐζημίωμαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 3.16, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1372b8}}:—**cause loss** or **do damage to, penalize**, πόλιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 30.25}}; τοσαύτας ἡμέρας ζημιοῦν τινα **to cause** one **the loss of**.., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 3.23}}: with _adjective_ _neuter_, μηδὲν μηδένα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 846a; οὐδὲν ζ. τὸ κοινόν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.5}}; πλείω ζ. σαυτὸν ἤ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.30:}} —_passive_, μεγάλα ζημιώσεται **will suffer** great **losses**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.40; πολλά 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 916e: _absolutely_, [prev. author] “Grg.” 490c.}}
**II** **fine, mulct in** a sum of money, with _dative_ _of things_, ζ. τινὰ χιλίῃσι δραχμῇσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.21, compare 136; χρήμασιν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.65; μναῖς τρισί 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 936a; also ζ. τινὰ ἕως τριάκοντα μνᾶς 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator “fragment” 40 (uncertain reading)}}; τινά τινος **deprive** of.., {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Lydus, Joannes Laurentius Historicus “Mag.” 2.19}}:—_passive_, **to be fined** or **amerced in** a thing, with _dative_ _of things_, χρήμασι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.4.7; δραχμῇ τῆς ἡμέρας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lg.” 766d; μέχρι τοσούτου [prev. work] 855b: also, c._accusative_ _of things_, ταῦτα [prev. work] 774b; ζ. τριάκοντα λίτρας 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 476}}; **suffer financial loss,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|PFlor. 142.8 (3rd c.AD): hence _metaphorically_, τοῦ ἑνὸς τοῦ περιέχεαι μάλιστα τῇ ψυχῇ ζημιώς εαι}} **wilt lose**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.39; τὴν ψυχὴν αὐτοῦ NT.Matt.16.26; ἑαυτόν NT.Luke.9.25; τὰ κέρατα 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 10.1.}}
**II.2** generally, **punish**, τὴν θάλασσαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.35; σφέας [prev. author] 9.77; τινὰ θανάτῳ [prev. author] 3.27}}; τινὰ φυγῇ, πληγαῖς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.65, 8.74}}:—_passive_, ζημιοῦσθαι ζημίαις ἐσχάταις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 31.26; θανάτῳ 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.3.9, “PTeb.” 5.92 (2nd c.BC)}}; θανάτῳ καὶ πᾶσι τοῖς ἐσχάτοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 297e; χρήμασιν καὶ ἀτιμία [prev. author] “Lg.” 721b.}}

2212:
** ζητ-έω**,
_Doric dialect_ _participle_ **ζάτεισα** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.85:}}_imperfect_ ἐζήτουν, _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ζήτει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.258 (nowhere else in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus)}}: _aorist 1_ ἐζήτησα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 16.14}}: _perfect_ ἐζήτηκα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 2.19:}} —_middle_, _aorist 1_ ἐζητησάμην (ἀν-) {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Longus Scriptor Eroticus “Prooem.” 2}}:—_passive_, _future_ ζητηθήσομαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 1.60, “M.” 8.16}}; but ζητήσομαι in _passive_ sense,{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 1.28, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 1.649}}:—**seek, seek for**, ἐμὲ δ᾽ ἔξοχα πάντων ζήτει Il.[same place]; ζ. πημάτων ἀπαλλαγάς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 318, compare 264; εὑρήσεις ζητῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 105}}; μὴ ζητῶν without **seeking**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 8.1}}; τὸ ζητούμενον ἁλωτόν **what is sought for** may be found, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 110.}}
**2** **mquire for**, τὰ πινάκια καὶ τὰ γραμματεῖα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12.91.11; τοὺς ἄρχοντας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.3.2: with _relative_ clause, ὅτου δέοιτο ὁ Κῦρος [prev. author] “Cyr.” 8.5.13.}}
**3** **search after, search out**, τὸν αὐτόχειρα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 266; μεγάλοις μηνύτροις ἐζητοῦντο οἱ δράσαντες 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.27}}; of huntsmen, ζ. τὸν λαγώ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 6.25.}}
**4** **search** or **inquire into, investigate, examine**, of philosophical investigation, ζ. τὰ θεῖα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.1.15; ζ. καὶ ἐρευνῶ κατὰ τὸν θεόν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 23b; ζητουμένης ἀρετῆς ὅ τι ἐστίν [prev. author] “Men.” 79d}}; τὸ ζητούμενον the **matter of inquiry**, the **question**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Tht.” 201a, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 110a7, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 2.1.18, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Adverbiis;” 188.13}}; also of judicial **inquiry**, ζ. περὶ ἀδικημάτων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.8; ζήτησιν τὴν ὑπέρ τινος ζ. [prev. work] 10; ἔνοχος εἶναι τοῖς ζητουμένοις [prev. work] 55}}: generally, ζ. πότερον.. ἤ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 27c; ζ. πρὸς ἐμαυτόν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Lex.” 17.}}
**5** **require, demand**, τῶν πράξεων παρὰ τοῦ στρατηγοῦ τὸν λόγον ζητοῦντες {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.33: _metaphorically_, ὁ περικράνιος ὑμὴν.. τὴν ἐπιδιαίρεσιν ζ}}. **requires** the opening up of the wound, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Heliodorus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 46.7.3.}}
**II** **seek after, desire**, ἀμήχανα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 203; ἐμοὶ ζητῶν ὄλεθρον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 659}}; of natural tendencies, ὁ θερμὸς ὕφαμμον χώραν ζητεῖ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 8.11.8}}:—_passive_, ζητούμενος **sought after, in great demand,** {{Refs|PMag.Par. 1.3086,3114.}}
**II.2** with _infinitive_, **seek to** do, ἐκμαθεῖν τι ζ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.137, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 776; μεταλαβεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 370. compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 322b, “Men.” 90e, “SIG” 372.7 (Samothrace, 3rd c.BC)}}: with _future_ _infinitive_, ζητεῖς ἀναπείσειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 573 codices (sed to be read -πείθειν)}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, **seek** or **desire that**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 443b, “Chrm.” 172c.}}
**III** **have to seek, feel the want of**, ἵνα μὴ ζητέοιεν σιτία {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.94; Νέρωνα 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Galba” 8}}:—_passive_, ζητούμενος οἷς ἀπέλειπες {{Refs|“epigram Gr.” 215.3 (from Rhenea).}}

2213:
** ζήτ-ημα**, ατος, τό,
**that which is sought**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 3}}; οὐ ῥάδιον ζ. **a thing** not easy **to find**, of Pentheus' mutilated limbs, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1139}}; δυσνοούμενον ζ., of God, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Secundus Gnomologus “Sententiae - FPG” 3.}}
**II** **inquiry, question**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 278, NT.Act.15.2, +others}}; especially of a philosophic nature, τὸ περὶ νόμους ζ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 631a}}; τὰ περὶ φύσεως ζ{{Refs 4th c.AD+|. [prev. work] 891c; ποιητικῶν ζ. λύσεις 4th c.AD: Metrodorus Epigrammaticus “Herc.” 831.13}}; also τοῦτ᾽.. οὗ τυγχάνει ζ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 421a; ἐκεῖνό γ᾽ ἦν τὸ ζ. πρῶτον, πότερον.. [prev. author] “Sph.” 221c}}; **search**, σῶμα μυρίοις ζητήμασιν εὑρών {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1218}}; μητρός **after** her, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ion” 1352.}}
**II.2** **official** or **judicial inquiry,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|POxy. 97.14 (2nd c.AD).}}
**III** in _plural_, **claims,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|PRyl. 117.14 (3rd c.AD)}}; **subjects of dispute**, {{NT+1st c.AD+|“SIG” 785.8 (1st c.AD), NT.Act.25.19.}}

2214:
** ζήτ-ησις**, εως, ἡ,
**seeking, search for**, κατ᾽ Εὐρώπης ζήτησιν ἐκπλῶσαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.44}}; κατὰ βίου τε καὶ γῆς ζ. {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.94, compare 2.54}}; ἀνδρὸς κατὰ ζήτησιν in **quest** of him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 55; ἡ ζ. τῶν δρασάντων 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.66; ζ. ἐπιστήμης 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 196d, etc.; τῆς τροφῆς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.57; τῆς ἀληθείας [prev. author] 1.20.}}
**2** **searching, examining**, ζήτησιν ἐποιέετο τῶν νεῶν **searched** the ships, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.118, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.30, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.43.}}
**3** **inquiry, investigation**, especially of a philosophic nature, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 406a, “Ap.” 29c, +others; περὶ τῆς τοῦ παντὸς φύσεως [prev. author] “Ti.” 47a}}; ζ. τοῦ μέλλοντος διὰ ὀρνίθων ποιεῖσθαι **inquire into** the future by augury, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. author] “Phdr.” 244c: in _plural_, [prev. author] “Phd.” 66d, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.276S., 2.185S.}}
**4** **judicial inquiry**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.10, “POxy.” 237 vi7 (2nd c.AD)}}, etc.: _plural_, **suits, controversies**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“OGI” 629.9 (Palmyra, 2nd c.AD).}}

2217:
** ζόφος**, ὁ,
**nether darkness**, ἱεμένων Ἔρεβόσδε ὑπὸ ζόφον {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.356, compare 11.155, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.56, NT.Jude.6}}; Ἀΐδης δ᾽ ἔλαχε ζόφον ἠερόεντα obtained **the realms of gloom** for his share, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.191, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.57, “hymnus ad Cererem” 402, 446, etc.; γῆς ὑπὸ ζόφον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 839.}}
**2** generally, **gloom, darkness**, {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 227, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.20.7, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 400a8, NT.Heb.12.18, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alcibiades” 28, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMort.” 15.2}}; χειμέριος ζ. **the gloom** of winter, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 4(3).18: _metaphorically_, τῆς ψυχῆς.. ζ. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.48c.}}
**II** **the dark quarter**, i.e. **the West**, ἤδη γὰρ φάος οἴχεθ᾽ ὑπὸ ζόφον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.335; οὐ γὰρ ἴδμεν ὅπῃ ζ. οὐδ᾽ ὅπῃ ἠώς 10.190}}; ποτὶ ζόφον, opposed to πρὸς ἠῶ τ᾽ ἠέλιόν τε, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.240, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.241, 9.26}} (**the North** _accusative_ to {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 10.2.12)}}; Γαδείρων τὸ πρὸς ζόφον to **the west** of.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 4.69.}} —poetical and later Prose. (Prob. _cognate_ with ζέφυρος.)

2218:
** ζῠγόν**, τό,
also **ζυγός**, ὁ, (in various senses), {{NT+5th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Cererem” 217, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 63b, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 30.29, LXX.Gen.27.40, +others, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.82.2, NT.Matt.11.29, 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 5.173a, etc.}}: rarely in _plural_, ζυγοί {{LXX+5th c.BC+|LXX.Pro.11.1, Scholia 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.29}}: Delph. **δυγός** (which see).
**I** **yoke** of a plough or carriage, ζ. ἵππειον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.799, 23.392; ὑπὸ ζυγὸν ἤγαγεν ἵππους 5.731, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.383; ἐπὶ ζυγὸν αὐχένι θεῖναι βουσί 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 815, compare 581}}; ὑπὸ ζυγόφιν (i.e. ζυγοῦ) λύον ἵππους {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.576: proverbial, τὸν αὐτὸν ἕλκειν ζ}}. 'to be in the same boat', {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|5th c.AD: Aristaenetus Rhetor 2.7, 2nd c.AD: Zenobius Paroemiographus 3.43; ταὔτ᾽ ἐμοὶ ζ. τρίβεις 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 6.12.}}
**I.2** _metaphorically_, ἐπὶ ζυγὸς αὐχένι κεῖται {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Cererem” 217; ἐχθροῖσιν ὑπὸ ζυγὸν αὐχένα θήσω 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1023}}; ἐπαυχένιον λαβεῖν ζ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.93}}; δούλιον ζ. **the yoke** of slavery, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.8.γ, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 75 (_plural_), 471, etc.}}; δουλείας, ἀνάγκης ζ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 944, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1330; ὑπὸ ζυγῷ λόφον δικαίως εἶχον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 291}}; ἐπιτιθέναι τινὶ ζυγὰ τοῦ μὴ.. so as to prevent.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.27; ζυγῷ ζυγῆναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 508a; ἄγειν ὑπὸ τὸν ζ. τινάς 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.82.2, compare 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 3.22; ὑπὸ τὸν ζ. ὑπαγαγεῖν 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus “fragment” 36.10; ζυγὸν ὑποστῆναι 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 10.20.}}
**II** **crossbar** of the φόρμιγξ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.187.}}
**II.2** ζυγὸς ἡ τῆς ἀμπέλου πρὸς τὴν χάρακα συζυγία {{Refs|“Geoponica” 5.29.6.}}
**III** in _plural_, **thwarts** or **benches joining** the opposite sides of a ship, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.99, 13.21, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.96}}: rarely in _singular_, θοὸν εἰρεσίας ζυγόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 249 (Lyric poetry)}}: _metaphorically_, of the seat of authority compared to the helmsman's seat, ἐς τὸ πρῶτον πόλεος ζ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 595; ἐπεὶ δ᾽ ἐπὶ ζυγοῖς καθέζετ᾽ ἀρχῆς [prev. author] “Ph.” 74}}; σὺ ταῦτα φωνεῖς νερτέρᾳ προσήμενος κώπῃ, κρατούντων τῶν ἐπὶ ζυγῷ δορό; while on the **main thwart** sits authority, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1618}}; also of a coachman's **seat, box,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|PMasp. 303.15 (6th c.AD).}}
**III.2** in _plural_, **panels** of a door, {{Refs|“IG” 12.372.199,22.1457.14,1672.155}}; compare ζευγίον.
**IV** **beam of the balance**, ζυγὸν ταλάντου {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 822 (Lyric poetry), compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mechanica” 850a4}}: hence, **the balance itself** (compare πῆχυς IV), αἴρειν τὸν ζυγόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 63b}}; ἐν πλάστιγγι ζυγοῦ κεῖσθαι {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 55o e}}; ζυγῷ or ἐν τῷ ζ. ἱστάναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 10.18, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 356b; ζυγὸν ἱστάναι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Prooemia” 55: in _plural_, [prev. author] 25.46, “SIG” 975.39 (Delos, 3rd c.BC): proverbial, ζ. μὴ ὑπερβαίνειν 4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus cited in 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 8.18.}}
**IV.b** the constellation {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Libra”, 2nd c.BC: Hipparchus Astronomicus 3.1.5, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.28, 4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 2.137, etc.; ζ. Ἀφροδίτης 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Antro Nympharum - Porphyrii Opuscula” 22.}}
**V** καρχασίου the **yard-arm** at the mast head, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 5.51, compare 3rd c.AD(?): Achilles Tatius Astronomus 5.16.}}
**VI** **cross-strap** of a sandal, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 417, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 7.81}}; ζυγός, ὁ, {{Refs 9th c.AD+|9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc.}}
**VII** **pair**, κλεινὸν ζυγόν, of persons, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 392}}; κατὰ ζυγά in **pairs**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 544a5, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 13.32.}}
**VIII** **rank** or **line** of soldiers, opposed to **file** (στοῖχος), ἐν τῷ πρώτῳ ζ. ἐμάχοντο τέσσαρες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.68; ὁ ζυγός 2nd c.AD: Polyaenus Historicus 4.4.3 (τὰ ζυγά 2.10.4)}}; κατὰ ζυγόν **line with line**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.45.9; κατ᾽ ἄνδρα καὶ ζ. [prev. author] 3.81.2}}; especially **front rank**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Tactica - Griechische Kriegsschriftsteller” 7.1, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Tactica” 8.1}}; also of the Chorus, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 4.108.}}
**IX** ζυγὰ ἢ ἄζυγα **even** or odd, a game, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 817.}}
**X** measure of land, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 963.13 (Amorgos, 4th c.BC).}}

2220:
** ζῡμ-όω**, ζύμη
) **leaven**, μικρὰ ζύμη ὅλον τὸ φύραμα ζ. {{NT|NT.1Cor.5.6}}:—_passive_, **to be leavened, ferment**, {{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.Exo.12.34, 39, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.659b, etc.}}; of digestion, τὰ μέλανα -οῦται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 61}}; [κοιλίη] ἐζυμωμένη **in a ferment**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “VM” 11.}}
**2** **cause to effervesce**, γῆν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 10.964, 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 1 “Praef.”:}} —_passive_, ζυμουμένη [χύτρα] {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 124.8.}}

2221:
** ζωγρ-έω**,
(ζωός, ἀγρέω) **take, save alive, take captive** instead of killing, ζώγρει, Ἀτρέος υἱέ, σὺ δ᾽ ἄξια δέξαι ἄποινα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.46, compare 10.378, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.86, etc.; εἷλε.. καὶ ἐζώγρησε [prev. author] 3.52; τοὺς μὲν ἀπέκτειναν, τινὰς δὲ καὶ ἐζώγρησαν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.92; πλὴν ὅσον ἐκ τριῶν νεῶν οὓς ἐζώγρησαν [prev. author] 7.23}}; πλὴν μηδαμῇ μηδαμῶς ζωγροῦντας provided that they do not **spare** him **alive**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 868c}}; opposed to διαφθείρειν, ἀποκτεῖναι, {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.84.10, LXX.Num.31.18: _metaphorically_, ἀνθρώπους ἔσῃ ζωγρῶν NT.Luke.5.10}}; of ships, ἃς ἐζώγρησεν αὐτάνδρους {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Charito Scriptor Eroticus 7.6}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.66, 5.77.}}
**II** **restore to life and strength, revive**, περὶ δὲ πνοιὴ Βορέαο ζώγρει ἐπιπνείουσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.698 (quoted by 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “ὀξέων νούσων θεραπευτικόν” 2.3)}}; **preserve alive**, ζώγρει, δέσποτ᾽ ἄναξ, τὸν σὸν ναετῆρα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“epigram Gr.” 841.7 (Thrace, 2nd c.AD).}}

2222:
** ζωή **
(**ζωιή** (probably an error) {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 577.34 (Milet., 3rd-2nd c.BC))}}, _Doric dialect_ **ζωά**: _Ionic dialect_ and _poetry_ **ζόη**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 10.4, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 556, etc. (see. below), compare “IG” 9(1).86 (from Hyampolis)}}, _Doric dialect_ **ζόα**: _Aeolic dialect_ **ζοΐα** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 29.5}}: ἡ:— **living**, i.e. one's **substance, property**, ἦ γάρ οἱ ζ. γ᾽ ἦν ἄσπετος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.96}}; τοὶ δὲ ζωὴν ἐδάσαντο{{Refs|[prev. work] 208; κατὰ ζωὴν φαγέειν 16.429}}; τὴν ζόην ποιέεσθαι or καταστήσασθαι ἀπὸ or ἐκ.. to get one's **living** by.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.105, compare 2.36, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 608b21; ἐξ ἁλός 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus “Coma Berenices” 2.}}
**2** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, **life, existence**, opposed to death, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Tyrtaeus Elegiacus 15.5, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 8.36, etc.}}; θανάτου πέρι καὶ ζωᾶς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 9.29; οὐδὲν γὰρ ἄλγος οἷον ἡ πολλὴ ζόη 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 556}}; ζόας (ζωᾶς codices) βιοτά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 664 (Lyric poetry); τοῦ βίου ζωή 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 44c; ὁ τῆς ζ. χρόνος “SIG” 1210 (from Calymna), etc.: as a term of affection, ζωή}} **mylife**! {{LXX+4th c.BC+|1st c.AD: Juvenalis, D. Junius Poeta Latinus 6.195: _plural_, ζόαι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 99.13; ζωαί LXX.Psa.62(63).3(4)}}; μετὰ τὴν μίαν ζ. πολλαὶ ζ. {{Refs 4th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 100; αἱ τῆς ψυχῆς ζ. καὶ δυνάμεις 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de communi mathematica scientia” 3.}}
**3** **way of life**, ζόην ἔζωον τὴν αὐτήν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.112, compare 114.}}
**II** ζωή,= γραῦς {{Refs|11}}, **the scum on milk**, {{Refs|12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 906.52}}; ζόη· τὸ ἐπάνω τοῦ μέλιτος, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus [The form ζόη (paroxyt.)}} is required by the metre in trimeters in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 556, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1108, and in lyrics 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 592, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 976, +others, ζωή never: ζόη in other Poets, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 114, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus “Epigrammata” 18.9, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 10.4.] (For the root, compare ζῶ: fancifully connected with ζέω and ζητέω, 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 81: in signification 11 ζόη probably from ζέω.)}}

2223:
** ζών-η**, ἡ,
(ζώννυμι) **belt, girdle**:
**I** properly **the lower girdle worn by women** just **above the hips**, περὶ δὲ ζώνην βάλετ᾽ ἰξυῖ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.231, 10.544, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.181, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.51, etc.}} —Phrases:
**I.1** λῦσε δὲ παρθενίην ζ. unloosed her maiden **girdle**, of the bridegroom, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.245, compare “Lyric poetry” “Alex.Adesp.” 11.18, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lycurgus” 15}}:—_middle_, of the bride, μούνῳ ἑνὶ ζώναν ἀνέρι λυσαμένα {{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 7.324 (hence ζώνη}}, _absolutely_, of **marriage**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 204 (Lyric poetry)}}; of **sexual intercourse**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 7.6): but also}}
**I.1.b** ζ. λῦσαι to loose **the girdle** for childbirth, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “Fragmenta” 67}}; later λύσασθαι or ἀπολύσασθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Delum” 209, 3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 3.56; so ζώναν κατατίθεσθαι 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.39.}}
**I.1.c** of men on a march, ζ. λύσασθαι to slacken one's **belt**, i.e. rest oneself, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.120; ζ. ἀναλύεσθαι 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Delum” 237.}}
**I.2** of pregnant women, τέκνων ἤνεγχ᾽ ὑπὸ ζώνην βάρος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 1000}}; πῶς γάρ σ᾽ ἔθρεψεν ἐντὸς.. ζώνη; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Eu.” 608; τοῦτον.. ἔφερον ζώνης ὕπο 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 762}}; also ὑπὸ ζώνῃ θέσθαι to **conceive,** {{Refs|hymnus ad Venerem 255.}}
**I.3** proverbial, εἰς ζώνην δεδόσθαι to be given **for girdle-money** (as we should say, **pinmoney**), of Oriental queens who had cities given them for their small expenses, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.4.9; ἣν [χώραν] καλεῖν.. ζ. τῆς βασιλέως γυναικός 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.123b.}}
**II** man's **belt** (more frequently ζωστήρ), ἡ ζ. τοῦ Ὠρίωνος the three stars that form the **belt** of Orion. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 343b24}}; the **belt** of barbarians, in which they wore the dagger, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.6.10, 4.7.16, 4th c.BC: Theopompus Historicus 39a, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Anach.” 33, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Mi.” 368c.}}
**II.b** belt used as a **purse,** {{NT+1st c.AD+|PRyl. 127.32(1st c.AD), NT.Matt.10.9, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.665b; ζ. χρυσίον 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Fug.” 31.}}
**II.2** **part round which the girdle passed, waist**, Ἄρεϊ ζώνην ἴκελος [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.479 (misunderstood by 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 9.17.3), compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.234, 5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Fragmenta” 168.28, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus cited in 1st c.AD: Erotianus Grammaticus (also explained as ={ὀσφύς}).}}]
**II.3** = Latin _cingulum_, **belt** worn by Roman civil and military officers, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Demodocus Lyricus] 5}}; ={ἀξίωμα}, {{Refs|10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}}; οἱ ὑπὸ ζώνην **soldiers**, [{{Refs 6th c.AD+|Anonymus cited in [same work]see {αὐθεντήσαντα},compare “Codex Justinianus [4th-6th c.AD]” 1.5.12.6, 11, 6th c.AD: Justinianus Imperator “Edict.” 13.26, “PLond.” 5.1680.21 (6th c.AD).}}]
**III** **anything that goes round like a belt**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.935a, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Musc.Enc.” 3}}; of the **girdle** of ocean, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “adversus Christianos - Abh. Berl. Akad.” 69.}}
**III.2** **one of the zones of the terrestrial sphere,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|Stoic. 2.195, 2nd-1st c.BC: Posidonius Historicus cited in 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 2.2.2, “Placita Philosophorum” 2.12.1 (_plural_), etc.}}; ζ. διακεκαυμένη, εὔκρατος, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 1.2.24, 1.4.6.}}
**III.2.b** **one of the planetary spheres**, οἱ μὲν [τῶν πλανητῶν] ὑψηλὴν ζ. φέρονται οἱ δὲ ταπεινήν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus 8, compare 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 26.18, “Corpus Hermeticum” 1.25.}}
**III.2.c** Astrology texts,= ζῴδιον, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “in Ptolemaei Tetrabiblon” 186.}}
**III.3** in Architecture texts,= διάζωμα, **frieze**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 5.10.5.}}
**III.4** Latin _zona_, in in Medicine texts, **shingles**, Scrib.Larg.{{Refs|63, 247; compare ζωστήρ 111.3.}}
**III.5** **stripes** on fish, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 3.28, +others}}
**IV** _plural_, an order of divine beings presiding over, or **engirdled with** cosmic **zones**, opposed to ἄζωνοι, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 96, 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in Platonis Parmenidem commentarii” p.494S.}}

2224:
** ζώννῡμι**
(ὑπο-ζωνύναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12.73.9), (παρα-) 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 553c}}; **ζωννύω** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 1.68}}: _imperfect_ ἐζώννυον {{NT|NT.John.21.18}}: _future_ ζώσω {{LXX+NT|LXX.Exo.29.9, NT.John.21.18}}: _aorist 1_ ἔζωσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.76, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 14}}: _perfect_ ἔζωκα {{Refs 1st c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 8.40.2, (δι-) 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 2.5}}:—_middle_ (see. below {{Refs|11)}}:—_passive_, _aorist 1_ ἐζώσθην {{Refs 4th c.BC+|(δι-) 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Signis Tempestatum” 22:}}_perfect_ ἔζωμαι (δι-) [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.6 cited in 9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc., 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus see at {σέσωται}, _3rd.pers._ _sing._ ἔζωται (δι-) “IG” 22.1491.36, (ὑπ-) [prev. work]1621.68, ἔζωσται 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Art.”}}][same place]; also in _middle_ sense (see. below): rare in _Attic dialect_, even in compounds:—**gird**, especially **gird round the loins** for a pugilistic conflict (see. below), ἄγον ζώσαντες ἀνάγκῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.76}} (here only _active_ in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus); ζῶσέ [μιν].. Ἀθήνη 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 72}}; ζ. τινά **hug** him in wrestling, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 8.40.2}}; ζ. γαῖαν, of Ocean, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.778 (1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus)}}; ζ. νῆα ὅπλῳ,= ὑποζώννυμι {{LXX+3rd c.BC+|11, 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.368: with double _accusative_, ζ. τινὰ ζώνην LXX.Lev.8.7, cf. LXX.1Ki.17.39.}}
**II** _middle_, **ζώννῠμαι**, **gird oneself**, especially of athletes. γυμνός, ζωννυμένων τῶν πρὶν ἐνὶ σταδίῳ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 7.52.6 (Megara, 4th c.BC)}}; τὼ δὲ ζωσαμένω βήτην ἐς μέσσον ἀγῶνα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.685, compare 710; ζώννυνταί τε νέοι καὶ ἐπεντύνονται ἄεθλα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.89; Ὀδυσσεὺς ζώσατο μὲν ῥάκεσιν περὶ μήδεα 18.67, compare 1st c.BC: Parthenius Mythographus 10.2.}}
**II.2** generally, **gird up one's loins** for battle, ζώννυσθαι ἄνωγεν Ἀργείους {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.15; ζώννυσθαι [ζωστῆρι] 10.78}}: with _accusative_, ὅθι ζωννύσκετο μίτρην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5.857 (vulgar form); ζώσατο δὲ ζώνην 14.181 (vulgar form); χαλκὸν ζώννυσθαι 23.130; ἐς γόνυ μέχρι χιτῶνα ζώννυσθαι 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Dianam” 12; χιτῶνα εἰς μηρὸν ἔζωστο 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 4; for labour, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 345; ἐπὶ βουσίν 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.426, etc.; ζώννυσθαι τὰς κοιλίας ζώναις 4th c.BC: Theopompus Historicus 39a.}}
**III** _passive_, **to be fixed by means of girths**, {{LXX|LXX.1Mac.6.37.}}
**III.2** **to be formed in belts** or **seams**, καδμεία ἐζωσμένη (ἐξωσμ- codices) probably in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Pseudo-Democritus Alchemista Alch. p.45B. (compare ζωνῖτις). (ζω(ς)- from I.- 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **yōs-**, cf. Lithuanian _júosti_ 'to gird', **júostas**, Avest. **yāsta-**,= ζωστός 'girt'.)

2225:
** ζωογον-έω**,
**propagate** or **engender living creatures**, of inanimate substances, ἡ φύσις ζ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 3.22.3, compare “HP” 8.11.2}} (so in _middle_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “CP” 3.24.3), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mirabilia” 835b26}}; of animals, **breed**, like{ζωοτοκέω}, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.88, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.494c}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mirabilia” 832a14}}: generally, **engender** [στοιχεῖον] ἕκαστον ἀπὸ ἑτέρου -εῖται {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 162.17.}}
**II** **to be viviparous**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 7.14.3}}; **produce alive**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 19}}; ζ. παρθένον, of Zeus, **producing** Pallas **alive** from his head, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. author] “DDeor.” 8, compare 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.23.}}
**II.2** **make alive, quicken**, τι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 4.15.2}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 311, 6th-7th c.AD: Isidorus Hispalensis Grammaticus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 2.93f: _metaphorically_, σωφροσύνη ζ. τὸ φρονοῦν 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.378, compare 435.}}
**II.3** ={ζωγρέω}, **preserve alive**, {{LXX|LXX.Exo.1.17, “1 Ki.” 27.9}}; κύριος θανατοῖ καὶ ζ{{NT|. [prev. work] 2.6, cf. NT.Luke.17.33}}:—_passive_, {{NT|NT.Act.7.19.}}

2226:
** ζῷον**, τό,
**living being, animal**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.10 (of bees), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 551, “Pl.” 443, etc.; πᾶν ὅ τι περ ἂν μετάσχῃ τοῦ ζῆν ζῷον ἂν λέγοιτο 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 77b}}; ζῷα, opposed to φυτά, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phd.” 70d, 110e, etc.}}; ζ. θαλάττιον, χερσαῖον, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.98 S}}.; contemptuously, ὅπως ἡ χώρα τοῦ τοιούτου ζῴου καθαρὰ γίγνηται may be free from this kind of **animal** (i.e. beggars), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 936c}}; ζ. πονηρόν, of women, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Secundus Gnomologus “Sententiae - FPG” 8.}}
**II** in art, **figure, image**, not necessarily of animals (compare ζῴδιον), ζῷον δέ οἱ ἐνῆν, ἀνὴρ ἱππεύς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.88}}: mostly in _plural_, ζῷα ἐς τὴν ἐσθῆτα ἐγγράφειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.203, compare 2.4, 124, 148, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 515a, etc.}}; ζῷα γράψασθαι τὴν ζεῦξιν τοῦ Βοσπόρου to have **pictures** of the bridging of the Bosporus painted, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.88}}; compare ζωγραφέω: ζῷα ποιεῖν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 13.}}
**III** **sign** of the Zodiac, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 2.166.—The word is post- 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, no generic word used for **animal** being found till after the middle of the fifth cent. BC {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC(ζώϊον 7th-6th c.BC(?): Semonides Iambographus 13, whence _Attic dialect_ ζῷον}} by contraction: ι is found in {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 12.372.42, +others,11(2).161 B 76 (Delos, 3rd c.BC), 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.166 S}}., and in codices _optative_ in the Noun; the _adjective_ ζωός (which see) had no ι: for the compounds (except ζωγλύφος, ζωγράφος) decisive evidence is lacking: ζῳάγρια with ι was read by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Aristarchus Grammaticus in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.407.)}}

2227:
** ζωοποι-έω**,
= ζωογονέω {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 555b9;= ζωογονέω 11.2, [prev. author] “GA” 730a2, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 3.22.4: _metaphorically_, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 2.52.}}
**II** **make alive**, θανατῶς α καὶ ζ. {{LXX+NT|LXX.4Ki.5.7, cf. NT.John.5.21}}; **bring to life**, νεκρά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “VH” 1.22, cf. Scholia 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 122}}; **endow with life**, ἑαυτό {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 78, +others, compare 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Inst.” 25:}} —_passive_ {{Refs|[prev. work] 209.}}
**II.2** **preserve alive**, {{LXX|LXX.Judg.21.14.}}

2228:
** ἤ**,
_Epic dialect_ also **ἠέ** (in _signification_ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 11 ἤ (or ἠέ) followed by ἦ (or ἦε), see below)}}, _conjunction_ with two chief senses, Disj. (**or**) and _comparative_ (**than**).
**A** DISJUNCTIVE, **or**, ἐγὼ.. ἢ ἄλλος Ἀχαιῶν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.231, compare 397, 800, 4.142, 7.236, +others}}; θεόσυτος ἢ βρότειος ἢ κεκραμένη {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 116.}}
**A.2** ἢ.. ἤ **either.. or**, ἢ νῦν δηθύνοντ᾽ ἢ ὕστερον αὖτις ἰόντα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.27, compare 151, 5.484}}, etc.; so ἢ.. ἤτοι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 6.4, “fragment” 138; ἤτοι.. ἤ.. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 662, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1182, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.40, etc. (in Classical Gr. the alternative introduced by ἤτοι}} is emphasized, later no distinction is implied, {{NT+2nd c.BC+|NT.Rom.6.16; ἤτοι.. ἢ.. ἤ.. “PTeb.” 5.59 (2nd c.BC))}}; ἤ repeated any number of times, ἐγὼ δέ κεν αὐτὸς ἕλωμαι ἢ τεὸν ἢ Αἴαντος ἰὼν γέρας ἢ Ὀδυσῆος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.138, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.84, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 707}}; ἤ is probably wrongly accented in codices of [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.289, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.348, 19.109, see.{ἦ}}}] _adverb_ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1.3: ἢ πόλις βροτός θ᾽ ὁμοίως 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 524 (Lyric poetry) is exceptional.}}
**A.3** **or else, otherwise**, εἰδέναι δεῖ περὶ οὗ ἂν ᾖ ἡ βουλή, ἢ παντὸς ἁμαρτάνειν ἀνάγκη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 237c; μή με λυπεῖτε, ἢ φεύξομ᾽ ἐκ τῆς οἰκίης 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 5.74}}; ζῶντα κακῶς λέγειν ἐκώλυσε.., ἢ τρεῖς δραχμὰς ἀποτίνειν ἔταξε Legal {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus cited in 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Solon” 21, compare 24, “IG” 12.94.10, 4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “Orationes” 21.260a.}}
**A.II** in Questions or Deliberations in Disj. form(the accentuation is ἢ (ἠέ) followed by ἦ (ἦε), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus Gr. 2.24, +others, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Conjunctionibus;” 224.28):}}
**A.II.1** Direct questions,
**A.II.1.a** introduced by ἢ (ἠέ), ἢ δολιχὴ νοῦσος ἦ Ἄρτεμις ἰοχέαιρα.. κατέπεφνε; {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.172; ἤ τι κατὰ πρῆξιν ἦ μαψιδίως ἀλάλησθε..; 3.72, compare 1.408, 16.462, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.378, 15.735, 16.12, etc.}}
**A.II.1.b** without an introductory Particle, θεός νύ τις ἦ βροτός ἐσσ; art thou a goddess **or** a mortal? {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.149, compare 1.226, 4.314, 372, 643, 20.130, 21.194, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.63, 425, 534, 15.203: accented ἦ, Hdn.Gr. 2.145, +others, but ἤ}} frequently in codices of {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus and always in codices of later writers: ἤκουσας ἢ οὐκ ἤκουσας ἢ κωφῇ λέγ; 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 202}}; ἄρτι δὲ ἥκεις ἢ πάλα; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 43a; κακουργεῖν δεῖ ἢ ο; [prev. work] 49c}}; preceded by πότερον, πότερον δοκεῖ σοι κάκιον εἶναι, τὸ ἀδικεῖν ἢ τὸ ἀδικεῖσθα; {{Refs|[prev. author] “Grg.” 474c, etc.}}
**A.II.2** Indirect questions, frequently epexegetic of a preceding question and identical in form with direct questions.
**A.II.2.a** εἴπ᾽ ἄγε,.. ἤ ῥ᾽ ἐθέλει.., ἦ ἀπέειπε.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.674; ὄφρα δαῶμεν ἢ ἐτεὸν Κάλχας μαντεύεται ἦε καὶ οὐκί 2.300; διάνδιχα μερμήριξεν ἢ ὅ γε.. ἐναρίζοι ἦε χόλον παύσειεν 1.190; later with εἰ.. ἤ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 890, “Ag.” 478, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 80, etc.}}; πότερον or πότερα.. ἤ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pers.” 148, 352, “Ag.” 630, etc.; sometimes εἴτε.. ἤ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 897; ἢ.. εἴτε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 177.}}
**A.II.2.b** without introductory Particle, οὐδέ τι οἶδα ζώει ὅ γ᾽ ἦ τέθνηκε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.464, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.546, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.238.}}
**B** COMPARATIVE, **than, as**, after a _comparative_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.162, etc.: after positive _adjectives_ which imply comparison, ἄλλος, ἕτερος ἤ.., 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 595, “Tr.” 835 (Lyric poetry); ἐναντίος ἤ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 481c; ἴδιόν τι πάσχειν πάθος ἢ οἱ ἄλλοι [prev. passage]: after _adverbs_ or adverbial phrases, πλήν, πρίν, πρόσθεν, χωρίς (which see), ἀλλά (see. ἀλλ᾽ ἤ); τῇ ὑστεραίᾳ ἤ.. [prev. author] “Cri.” 44a (_falsa lectio_ in “Smp.” 173a); ἐν τῷ πέμπτῳ καὶ δεκάτῳ ϝέτει ἀπὸ τῶ ποτεχεῖ ϝέτεος ἢ Ἀριστίων ἐφορεύει “Tab.Heracl.” 1.121; παρὰ δόξαν ἢ ὡς αὐτὸς κατεδόκεε 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.79, compare 8.4; διαφερόντως ἤ.. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 85b}}; οὐδ᾽ ὅσον ἤ.. not so much **as.. **, not more **than.. **, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 9.21: after Verbs implying comparison, βούλεσθαι ἤ..}} to wish **rather than.. **, see at {βούλομαι} IV, αἱρέω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 11.1b}}; so φθάνειν ἤ.. to come sooner **than.. **, {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.445, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.58; ἐπιθυμεῖν ἤ.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.3; δέχεσθαι ἤ.. 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 10.21: less frequently after a word not implying comparison, δίκαιον ἡμέας ἔχειν.. (i.e. μᾶλλον) ἤ περ Ἀθηναίους 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.26; ἐμοὶ πικρὸς.. ἢ κείνοις γλυκύς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 966 (assuming variant); δεδικαιωμένος ἢ ἐκεῖνος NT.Luke.18.14.}}
**B.2** joining two Comparatives which refer to the same subject, πάντες κ᾽ ἀρησαίατ᾽ ἐλαφρότεροι πόδας εἶναι ἢ ἀφνειότεροι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.165; ταχύτερα ἢ σοφώτερα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.65; μανικώτεροι ἢ ἀνδρειότεροι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 144b, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1078.}}
**B.3** rarely after a _superlative_, πλεῖστα θωμάσια ἔχει Αἴγυπτος ἢ ἄλλη πᾶσα χώρη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.35 (assuming variant); πίθοιτό κεν ὔμμι μάλιστα ἢ ἐμοί 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.91.}}
**B.4** ἢ οὐ is used when a _negative_ precedes, οὐδέν τι μᾶλλον ἐπ᾽ ἡμέας ἢ οὐ καὶ ἐπ᾽ ὑμέας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.118, compare 5.94, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.62, etc.}}: after an implied _negative_, ὠμὸν.. πόλιν ὅλην διαφθεῖραι μᾶλλον ἢ οὐ τοὺς αἰτίους {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.36.}}
**B.5** frequently omitted with numerals after πλείων, ἐλάττων, μείων, ἔτη.. πλείω ἑβδομήκοντα variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 17d; οὐ μεῖον πεντακοσίους 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.4.24}}: sometimes with an _infinitive_ or conditional clause, τί γὰρ ἀνδρὶ κακὸν μεῖζον ἁμαρτεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 879}}; τίς εὐπραξία σπανιωτέρα.., εἰ [δύναμις] πάρεστιν (for ἢ δύναμιν παρεῖναι); {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.33.}}
**B.6** _pleonastic_ with a _genitive_, τίς ἂν αἰσχίων εἴη ταύτης δόξα, ἢ δοκεῖν.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 44c, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 10.28.}}
**B.7** the Disj. and _comparative_ uses are found together in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.511 βέλτερον, ἢ ἀπολέσθαι ἕνα χρόνον ἠὲ βιῶναι, ἢ δηθὰ στρεύγεσθαι ἐν αἰνῆ δηϊοτῆτι}} better, **either** to die once for all **or** win life, **than** long to toil in battle. [ἢ οὐ, ἢ οὐκ combine by Synizesis into one syllable in Trag. and Comedy texts, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 330, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 334, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 128}}; so usually in _Epic dialect_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.298, +others; ἢ αὐτός 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Fragmenta” 194; ἢ εἰ 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 201.]}}

2229:
** ἦ**,
_adverb_, never in the form ἦε (ἠέ):
**I** TO CONFIRM an assertion, **in truth, of a surety**, ἦ ὀλίγον οἷ παῖδα ἐοικότα γείνατο Τυδεύς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.800, etc.; ironically, 1.229, +others}}; with concessive force, **it is true that.. **: hence, **although.. **, ἦ καὶ γένει ὕστερος ἦεν {{Refs|3.215}}: frequently strengthened by the addition of one or two other Particles, as ἦ ἄρα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.193; ἦ ἄρα δή 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.446; ἦ ῥα 4.82; ἦ ῥά νυ 6.215; ἦ γάρ 1.78; ἦ γάρ τοι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.199; ἦ δή 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.272; ἦ δή που 21.583; ἦ δῆτα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 429; ἦ θην 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.452; ἦ κάρτα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1279; ἦ μάλα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.204; ἦ μάλα δή 5.422}}; ἦ μήν and ἦ μέν (see. below); ἦ νυ [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.11; ἦ τάχα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.73; ἦ τε 13.211: and to express doubt, ἦ που, see at {ἦ που} and ποῦ: especially ἦ μήν}}] used in oaths and asseverations, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.291, 7.393, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 73, 168, etc.; also ἦ μάν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.370, 13.354, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 23.5; ἦ μέν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.65 (later εἶ μήν, see at {εἷ})}}]: with _infinitive_ in indirect speech, after Verbs of swearing, etc., σὺ δὲ σύνθεο, καί μοι ὄμοσσον, ἦ μέν μοι πρόφρων ἔπεσιν καὶ χερσὶν ἀρήξειν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.77}}; ἦ μέν also in _Ionic dialect_ historical Prose, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.154, 5.93, +others; ἐγγυᾶσθαι, ἦ μὴν παραμενεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 115d}}; ἐγγυητὰς καταστῆσαι ἦ μὴν ἐκτείσειν Legal cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.39}}: with other Particles, ἦ μὲν δή {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.798, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.257, +others; ἦ δὴ μάν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.538.}}
**I.2** in the combinations ἐπεὶ ἦ, ὅτι ἢ and τί ; {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Conjunctionibus;” 255.5, “Synt.” 307.19 (cf. Hdn.Gr. 1.520), recognizes an 'expletive' ἠ}} (παραπληρωματικὸς σύνδεσμος) _perispomenon_ after ἐπεί, barytone after ὅτι or τί. It is probably the same as the affirmative ἦ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|compare (2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “Conj.”[same place])}}, and occurs in the same combinations, ἐπεὶ ἦ πολύ.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.169, +others}}; ἐπεὶ ἦ μάλα{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 156, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.465; ἐπεὶ ἦ καί.. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.437, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.442. 1st c.BC: Trypho Grammaticus took τίη}} as one word, and this can be supported by τί ἢ δὲ σύ.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.55, but 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus (“Conj.”[same place]) infers from the accent of ὅτι ἢ that τί ἢ}} was two words. The Attic accentuation is said by {{Refs|12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 45 [near the start], 118.39, 907.14 to be τιή, ὁτιή(which see).}}
**I.3** this ἦ (or ἢ) is probably to be recognized in ὥς τε γὰρ ἦ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.289, ὥς τέ τευ ἦ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.348, 19.109, where codices have ἢ (in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.348 ἠδέ (conjecture Bekker) should perhaps be read for ἠέ).}}
**II** in Questions not involving alternatives:
**II.1** Direct questions,
**II.1.a** epexegetic of a preceding question, suggesting the answer to it, τίπτ᾽ εἰλήλουθα; ἦ ἵνα ὕβριν ἴδῃ Ἀγαμέμνονο; why hast thou come? **is it** that thou mayst see.. ? {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.203, compare 5.466, 7.26, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.710, 13.418, 17.376, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 17.5}}; τί δῆτα χρῄζει; ἦ με γῆς ἔξω βαλεῖ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 622, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1425 (ἤ codices)}}; τίς σοι διηγεῖτ; ἦ αὐτὸς Σωκράτη; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 173a (_perispomenon_, codex B): on the accent, 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus Gr. 2.112.}}
**II.1.b** not epexegetic of a preceding question, ἦ σύ γ᾽ Ὀδυσσεύς ἐσσι πολύτροπο; art thou the wily Odysseus? {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.330, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.666, 15.504}}; ἦ οὐκ ὀτρύνοντος ἀκούετε.. Ἕκτορο; do you not hear.{{Refs 8th c.BC+|.? [prev. work] 506, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.424}}; ἦ τὸ πλοῖον ἀφῖκτα; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 43c}}; ἦ οὐ δοκεῖ καὶ σοὶ οὕτ; don't you think so too? {{Refs|[prev. author] “Grg.” 479b (_perispomenon_, codex T); ἦ βούλει συλλογισώμεθα αὐτ; shall we work them (the consequences) out? [prev. work] 479c (_perispomenon_, codex T)}}; ἦ τορῶς λέγ; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 269}}; ἦ κἀν δόμοισι τυγχάνει τανῦν παρώ; {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 757: frequently with other Particles, ἦ ἄρ..; 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.166, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.56; mostly ἦ ῥα..; 5.421, 762, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.632}}; also in Trag. (in Lyric poetry), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 633, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 172, 955}}: especially to mark the first of several questions, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 7(6).3}} _ff._; ἦ ἄρα δή..; {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.446; ἦ ῥά νυ..; 4.93; ἦ νυ..; 15.128}}; ἦ ταῦτα δή..; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 565, “El.” 385}}; ἦ ταῦτα δῆτα..; {{Refs|[prev. author] “OT” 429}}; ἦ γάρ..; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 745, 757, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1000: in _Attic dialect_ Prose, ἦ γάρ}}; standing alone, **is it not so?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 160e, “Grg.” 449d, 468d}}; ἦ καί..; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1207, 1362:}} —ἦ usually begins the sentence, except that the vocative may precede, as in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.421, 762, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.632, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 863, 1102, or ἀλλά, as in 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 276, “Ch.” 774:}} —by Crasis ἦ combines with ἄρα in _Attic dialect_ and the κοινή to ἆρα (which see), in all other dialects {{Refs 7th c.BC+|compare (2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Conjunctionibus;” 223.24) to ἦρα (which see), but ἆρα is found in 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.78 (ἄρα codices), +others, 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 86, 89.}}
**II.2** Indirect questions, οἴχετο πευσόμενος μετὰ σὸν κλέος ἤ που ἔτ᾽ εἴης [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.415 (variant{εἰ}}}], which alone has Ms. authority in [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.83, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.325); ὄφρα καὶ Ἕκτωρ εἴσεται ἢ καὶ ἐμὸν δόρυ μαίνεται ἐν παλάμῃσιν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.111 (variant{εἰ})}}]; ἀλλ᾽ ἄγε μοι τόδε εἰπὲ.. ἢ καὶ Λαέρτῃ αὐτὴν ὁδὸν ἄγγελος ἔλθ; [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.138 (variant{εἰ})}}]; ἀμφίστασθαι ἦ κα πεφυτεύκωντι πάντα κὰτ τὰν συνθήκαν they shall investigate **whether..** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|Tab.Heracl. 1.125; μαντεύσασθαί οἱ.. ἦ λώϊόν οἵ κα εἴη 3rd c.BC: Isyllus Lyricus 34; διαφαφίξασθαι ἦ δοκεῖ αὐτὸν στεφανῶσαι “IG” 12(3).170.12 (from Astypalaea): accented ἤ in codices 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, but it should perhaps be _perispomenon_}}
**A ἦ**, for ἔφη, _3rd.pers._ _singular_ _imperfect_ or _aorist 2_ of ἠμί (which see). **ἦ**, for ἦν, _Attic dialect_ _contraction_ from Ionic ἔα, _imperfect_ of εἰμί (**sum**).

2230:
** ἡγεμον-εύω**,
_Doric dialect_ **ἁγ-**, **lead the way**, προτὶ Ἴλιον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.92}}; πρὸς δώματα, ἀγορήνδε, λέχοσδε, δεῦρο, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.386, 8.4, 23.293, 17.372; πρόσθ᾽ ἡγεμόνευεν 22.400, 24.155; αὖλιν ἐφ᾽ ἡμετέρην 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 25.60; ἐπιθυμίας καὶ ἔρωτος ἡγεμονεύσαντος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 197a:}}
**c** _dative_ _person_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.386, 8.4, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 387, etc.; τῇ ἴμεν, ᾗ κεν δὴ σὺ.. ἡγεμονεύῃς 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.46}}; ὁδὸν ἡ. **to lead** the way, ἐγὼ δ᾽ ὁδὸν ἡγεμονεύσω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.261, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 1.5: twice in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, with _dative_ et _accusative_, τοῖσι γέρων ὁδὸν ἡγεμόνευε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.225}}; ὕδατι ῥόον ἡ. **make** a course for the water, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.258.}}
**II** **lead** in war, **rule, command**, once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, with _dative_, Τρωσὶ μὲν ἡγεμόνευε.. Ἕκτωρ 2.816: elsewhere, with _genitive_, Λοκρῶν δ᾽ ἡ. Αἴας [prev. work] 527, compare 552, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.99, 160, etc.}}; ἡγεμόνων ἡ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 1.3, etc.}}; ἡ. τῆς σκέψεως **to take the lead** in it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 351e}}: _absolutely_, **to have** or **take the command**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.2; ἡ. ἐν πόλει 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 474c}}:— _passive_, **to be ruled**, ὑπό τινος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.61.—Signf. 11 never occurs in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”, and signification 1 rarely in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad”}}
**III** **to be governor**, τῆς Συρίας {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Eu. Luc.” 2.2: _absolutely_, “PTeb.” 302.7 (1st c.AD), “IGRom.” 3.162 (Ancyra, 2nd c.AD), etc.}}

2231:
** ἡγεμον-ία**, ἡ,
**leading the way, going first**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.93}}; τῇ τῶν δυναστευόντων ἡ. by their **example**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 711c.}}
**II** **authority, rule**, of dynasties or nations, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.7, 3.65, etc.}}; of a general or officer, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.91; ἐν ἡγεμονίαις [prev. author] 7.15}}; ἡ ἡ. τῶν Ἰώνων τοῦ πολέμου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.2}}; ἡ κατὰ πόλεμον ἡ., τῶν πολεμικῶν ἡ ἡ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1285b9, 18; αἱ ἡ. τῶν στρατοπέδων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 273c; τῶν ὀπισθοφυλάκων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.7.8}}; ἡ. δικαστηρίου **presidency** in a court, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.14}}; **headship** of a philosophical school, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “Academicorum Index” p.59 M.}}
**II.2** **political supremacy**, ἡ ἡ. τῆς 'Ελλάδος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.1.33; παρ᾽ ἑκόντων τῶν 'Ελλήνων τὴν ἡ. ἐλάβομεν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.30; ἡ. ἡ κατὰ θάλατταν [prev. author] 12.67, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 23.2}}; ἡ ἐν 'Αρείῳ πάγῳ βουλὴ οὐδενὶ δόγματι λαβοῦσα τὴν ἡ.{{Refs|[prev. work] 1, compare “Pol.” 1304a23}}; **political leadership** of an individual, {{Refs|[prev. work] 1296a39; γένος ὑπερέχον πρὸς ἡ. πολιτικήν [prev. work] 1288a9.}}
**II.2.b** = Latin _imperium_, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Marius” 36, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 60.17, etc.; Αἴγυπτον δήμου 'Ρωμαίων ἡγεμονίᾳ προσέθηκα “Mon.Anc.Gr.” 15.1; τοῖς καλοῖς τῆς ἡ. νόμοις 2nd c.BC: Athenaeus Mechanicus 39.7}}; τριῶν τῶν μεγίστων ἡ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lucullus” 30}}; **reign of an Emperor,** {{NT|NT.Luke.3.1}}; **office of prefect,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|POxy. 237v6 (2nd c.AD), +others}}
**III** **military unit, regiment,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 22.657 (_plural_), “PRein.” 9.13 (2nd c.BC), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Camillus” 23 (_plural_)}}; but αἱ μείζονες ἡ. the higher **commands**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Tactica - Griechische Kriegsschriftsteller” 10.4.}}
**IV** **chief thing, principal part**, ἡ. τῆς τέχνης {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 17.5.}}
**V** **a principality**, {{LXX|LXX.Gen.36.30}}; **a Roman governorship**, ἡ 'Ιλλυρίδος ἡ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 6.7.2, compare 7.5.2}}; **tenure of office of a governor,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|PRyl. 77.36 (2nd c.AD)}}; ἡ Ἡ. the **Government,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|PGrenf. 2.73.11 (3rd c.AD).}}

2232:
** ἡγεμών**,
_Doric dialect_ **ἁγ-**, _Aeolic dialect_ **ἀγίμων** {{Refs|“IG” 12(2).164 (Mytil.), +others}}, όνος, ; also ἡ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 8(7).22, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 722, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.171, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oec.” (below 11)}}:— **one who leads**; and so,
**I** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”}}, **guide**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|10.505, 15.310, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.14, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1014, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 97b; ἡγεμόνες γενέσθαι τινὶ τῆς ὁδοῦ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.31, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 281, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.3.4; ἡ. ποδὸς τυφλοῦ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1616; ἡγεμόνες τοῦ πλοῦ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.50}}; of a charioteer, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 804.}}
**I.2** **one who does a thing first, shows the way** to others, τοῖς νεωτέροις ἡ. ἠθῶν χρηστῶν γίγνεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 670e; πατέρες τῆς σοφίας καὶ ἡ. [prev. author] “Ly.” 214a; πόνους τοῦ ζῆν ἡδέως ἡγεμόνας νομίζετε 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.5.12; τῆς εἰρήνης ἡ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.24; [ἀχαριστία] ἐπὶ πάντα τὰ αἰσχρὰ ἡ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.7}}: _absolutely_, of choir-**leaders, Mnemos.**{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|47.253 (Argos, 2nd-1st c.BC).}}
**II** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad”}}, **leader, commander, chief**, opposed to λαοί, πληθύς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2.365, 11.304: with _genitive_, ἡγεμόνες Δαναῶν, φυλάκων, etc., 2.487, 9.85, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.43, 7.62, +others}}; στρατηγὸς καὶ ἡ. τῶν 'Ελλήνων πρὸς τὸν βάρβαρον{{Refs|[prev. work] 158; ἡ. τῶν πολέμων [prev. author] 9.33}}; ἔχοντες ἡγεμόνας τῶν πάνυ [στρατηγῶν] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.89}}; ={λοχαγός}, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Tactica” 5.6; ἡ. τῶν ἐν προχειρισμῷ “PAmh.” 2.39 (2nd c.BC)}}; **chief, sovereign**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 8(7).22, etc.; ἡ. γῆς τῆσδε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 103, compare “OC” 289; πάντων.. καὶ αὐτοῦ βασιλέως ἡ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.5.14; ἡ. συμμορίας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.157}}; of the **queen**-bee and **queen**-wasp, regarded by {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus as males, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 553a25, 629a3 (but ἡ τῶν μελισσῶν ἡ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 7.32, compare 38)}}; ὁ ἡ. τῶν προβάτων, of the bell-wether, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 573b24}}; τῶν βοῶν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 575b1; νέμειν τὰ κρέα τοῦ ἡγεμόνος βοός “SIG” 144.36 (Piraeus, 4th c.BC),compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.4.29.}}
**II.b** ἡ. χοροῦ **leader** of a chorus, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 4.106; παῖδες ἡ. “IG” 7.3196 (Orchom. _Boeotian dialect_)}}; **president** of a gymnasium, {{Refs|[prev. work]3.1086, +others}}
**II.c** **a Roman Emperor**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 4.3.2, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cicero” 2, +others}}; as translation of **princeps,** {{Refs|Mon.Anc.Gr. 7.9}}; ἡ. νεότητος, = Latin _princeps juventutis_, {{Refs|[prev. work]18}}; **a provincial governor**, {{NT+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 17.3.25, NT.Matt.27.2, NT.Act.23.24}}: frequently of the **praefectus Aegypti,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|PRyl. 119.4 (1st c.AD)}}, etc.; ἡ. ἀμφοτέρων, i.e. of Upper and Lower Egypt, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“POxy.” 39.6 (1st c.AD)}}; ἡ. Κύπρου {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“Tab. Defix.Aud.” 25.13 (3rd c.AD).}}
**II.2** as _adjective_, ἀνήρ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 119a}}; [ναῦς], of the flagship, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 722; ἡ. τῆς φυλῆς κορυφαῖος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.60 (assuming variant); ἡ. πόδες 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 490b5, “IA” 713b32: as _neuter_, ἡγεμόσι μέρεσι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 91e.}}
**III** in Prosody, ={πυρρίχιος}, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 17, “Dem.” 47.}}
**IV** ἡγεμόνες, _Doric dialect_ ἁγ-, αἱ, in Architecture, **coping-tiles** of the roof, {{Refs|“IG” 22.463.70, 1627.303, 4.1484.100 (Epid.).}}
**V** a kind of fish, ={ἡγητήρ} 2, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.980f.}}

2233:
** ἡγέομαι**,
_Doric dialect_ **ἁγ-** (irregular _present_ _participle_ ἁγώμενος {{Refs|“Hymnus Curetum [3rd c.BC (?)]” 4)}}_imperfect_ ἡγούμην{{Refs|lines12.28, etc.}}, _Ionic dialect_ **-εύμην**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.115, ἡγέοντο [prev. author] 9.15}}: _future_ ἡγήσομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.374, etc.}}: _aorist 1_ ἡγησάμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.48, etc.:}}_aorist 1_ ἡγήθην{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|in _passive_ sense, “PGiss.” 48.20 (3rd c.AD) (compare περιηγ-)}}: _perfect_ ἥγημαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.126, 2.115, ἅγημαι 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.248}}:—**go before, lead the way**, ὣς εἰπὼν ἡγεῖθ᾽, ἡ δ᾽ ἕσπετο Παλλὰς 'Αθήνη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.125; ἂν πάϊς ἡγήσαιτο νήπιος 6.300, etc.; πρόσθεν δὲ.. Ἶρις ἡγεῖτ᾽ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.96; ἡγοῦ πάροιθε 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 834; ἡ. ἐπὶ νῆα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.65; ἐς τεῖχος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.144; κλισίηνδε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14. 48, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.93, etc.; ἡγησόμενος οὐδεὶς ἔσται 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.4.5}}: Astron., **precede** in the daily movement, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Autolycus Astronomus 2.3, +others}}
**b** with _dative_ _person_, Τρωσὶ ποτὶ πτόλιν ἡγήσασθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.101; ἐκ Δουλιχίου.. ἡγεῖτο μνηστῆρσι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.397; οἱ γὰρ βλέποντες τοῖς τυφλοῖς ἡγούμεθα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 15; ἡ. τοῖς πολίταις πρὸς ἀρετήν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 10.2.}}
**c** with ὁδόν added, ὁδὸν ἡγήσασθαι **to go before** on the way, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.263; ἡ. τινὶ τὴν ὁδόν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.15.}}
**d** with _accusative_ loci, ἥ οἱ.. πόλιν ἡγήσαιτο who **might guide** him to the city, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.114, compare 7.22, 15.82; ἡ. βωμοὺς ἀστικούς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 501.}}
**e** ἅρματα ἡ. **drive** chariots, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Philostrati majoris imagines” 2.23.}}
**f** of logical priority, **to be antecedent**, opposed to ἕπεσθαι, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 2.71, 88, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 8.110, +others, 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 241, 6th c.AD: Philoponus, Joannes Philosophus “in Aristotelis libros de Generatione et Corruptione commentaria - pars” 195.13, “in Ph.” 496.14.}}
**g** ἡγούμενον, τό, the **leading principle**, the **main thing**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 63.14, compare 3rd c.BC(?): Sosipater Comicus 1.47.}}
**2** with _dative_ _person_ et _genitive_ _of things_, **to be** one's **leader in** a thing, θεῖος ἀοιδὸς.. ἡμῖν ἡγείσθω.. ὀρχηθμοῖο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.134}}; ἡ. τινὶ σοφίας, ᾠδῆς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” [same place], 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.125d; ἀλήθεια δὴ πάντων μὲν ἀγαθῶν θεοῖς ἡγεῖται πάντων δὲ ἀνθρώποις [prev. author] “Lg.” 730c; ἡ. τοῦ χοροῦ Πέρσαις 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.7.1, compare 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Delum” 313:}} with _genitive_ _of things_, ἁ. νόμων **to lead** the song, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 5.25; φρόνησις ἡ. τοῦ ὀρθῶς πράττειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 97c; ἡ. παντὸς καὶ λόγου καὶ ἔργου 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.3.15: also, τὸ ὀρθῶς τοῖς τοιούτοις χρῆσθαι ἐπιστήμη ἦν ἡγουμένη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 281a.}}
**3** with _dative_ _of things_, **to be leader in.. **, κερδοσύνῃ, νηπιέῃσι ἡ. τινί, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.247, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.469.}}
**4** with _accusative_ _of things_, **lead, conduct**, ἡ. τὰς πομπάς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.174}}; τὴν ἀποδημίαν (variant for{ᾐτήσατο}) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Dinon Historicus 7; τὰς τύχας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 226}}: with adverbial _accusative_, ἡ γλῶσσα πάνθ᾽ ἡγουμένη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 99.}}
**5** _participle_ ἡγούμενος, η, ον, as _adjective_, σκέλη ἡγούμενα, opposed to ἑπόμενα, the **front** legs, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Incessu Animalium” 713b6}}; ὁ ἡ. πούς the **advanced** foot, {{Refs|[prev. author] “fragment” 74.}}
**II** **lead, command** in war, with _dative_, νῆες θοαί, ᾗσιν 'Αχιλλεὺς ἐς Τροίην ἡγεῖτο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.169, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.238}}; οὐ γὰρ ἔην ὅς τίς σφιν ἐπὶ στίχας ἡγήσαιτο **might lead** them to their ranks, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.687; ἡ. Τρώεσσιν ἐς Ἴλιον 5.211; ἡ. Μῄοσιν 2.864; λόγχαισιν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1360; ἑτέροις 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 31.17, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.2.6; ἐν ταῖς στρατείαις, αἷς ἡγεῖται βασιλεύς 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.180: also generally, πόλει 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 282.24; but usually with _genitive_, Σαρπηδὼν δ᾽ ἡγήσατ᾽.. ἐπικούρων 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.101; ἡγήσατο λαῶν 15.311, compare 2.567, +others; ἡ. τῆς ἐξόδου 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.10; ἡγούμενος τῶν ἡδονῶν ἀλλ᾽ οὐκ ἀγόμενος ὑπ᾽ αὐτῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.45}}: _absolutely_, **to be in command**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 16.21, etc.}}
**II.2** **rule, have dominion**, with _genitive_, τῆς 'Ασίης, τῆς συμμαχίης, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.95, 7.148; οἱ Θεσσαλίης ἡγεόμενοι [prev. author] 9.1: _absolutely_, οἱ ἡγούμενοι}} **the rulers**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 386, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1363; ἡ. ἐν τοῖς ἀδελφοῖς}}**leading men,** {{NT|NT.Acts.15.22}}; ἡ. σχολῆς **to be the head** of a philosophical school, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Acad. Ind.” p.107 M., +others}}
**II.3** as official title, ἡγούμενος, ὁ, **president**, συνόδου {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PGrenf.” 2.67.3 (3rd c.AD); γερδίων [prev. work] 43.9 (1st c.AD)}}; ἱερέων {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PLond.” 2.281.2 (1st c.AD): _absolutely_, “PFay.” 110.26 (1st c.AD).}}
**II.3.b** of Roman governors, ἡ. ἔθνους,= Latin _praeses provinciae,_ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|POxy. 1020.5 (2nd-3rd c.AD)}}; ἡ. τῆς Γαλατίας {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 44.}}
**II.3.c** of subordinate **officials**, ἡ. τοῦ στρατηγοῦ {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“POxy.” 294.19 (1st c.AD)}}; κώμης {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PRyl.” 125.3 (1st c.AD).}}
**II.3.d** **abbot**, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Justinianus Imperator “Novellae” 7.1, +others: _feminine_ -μένη}} **abbess**, {{Refs|[prev. passage]}}
**II.4** ἡγούμενος as _adjective_, **principal**, πυλών {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PFlor.” 382.15 (3rd c.AD), “POxy.” 55.9 (3rd c.AD).}}
**III** post-{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, **believe, hold**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus (usually in _perfect_ ἥγημαι, _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἡγέαται)}}, etc.; ἡ. τι εἶναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.126, +others; ἡγεῖσθε δὲ [θεοὺς] βλέπειν.. πρὸς τὸν εὐσεβῆ βροτῶν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 278, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.89, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1020 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}
**III.2** with an attributive word added, ἡ. τινὰ βασιλέα **hold** or **regard** as king, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.52; μηδ᾽ αὐθαδίαν εὐβουλίας ἀμείνον᾽ ἡγήσῃ ποτέ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1035; ἅπαντας ἐχθροὺς τῶν θεῶν ἡγοῦ πλέον [prev. author] “Ch.” 902, compare 905}}; ἡ. τἄλλα πάντα δεύτερα **to hold** everything else secondary, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1442}}; οὐκ αἰσχρὸν ἡγῇ.. τὰ ψευδῆ λέγειν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 108, compare “Ant.” 1167; τὰς τούτων ἀπορίας ἀντιπάλους ἡ. τῷ ἡμετέρῳ πλήθει 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.10; περὶ πολλοῦ ἥγημαι μὴ ξεινοκτονέειν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.115; περὶ πλείονος 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 19.10; περὶ πλείστου 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.89; περὶ οὐδενός 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.26}}; παρ᾽ οὐδέν Decrees cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.164}}: with _participle_, πᾶν κέρδος ἡγοῦ ζημιουμένη φυγῇ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 454.}}
**III.3** especially of belief in gods, τὴν μεγίστην δαίμονα ἥγηνται εἶναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.40, compare 3.8}}; ἡ. θεούς **to believe in** gods, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 32, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 800, “Ba.” 1326; δαίμονας ἡ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 27d.}}
**III.4** ἡγοῦμαι δεῖν **think** fit, **deem** necessary, with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.23, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.20: without δεῖν, παθεῖν μᾶλλον ἡγησάμενοι ἤ.. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.42 (assuming variant); ἡγησάμην διατάγματι αὐτοὺς σωφρονίσαι “Inscription Magn.” 114 (2nd c.AD)}}; ἡγήσατο ἐπαινέσαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 346b}}.
**IV** _perfect_ in _passive_ sense, τὰ ἁγημένα,= τὰ νομιζόμενα, Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 43.66}}; ἡγεόμενον **being led**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.14 (ἀγόμενον Dind.): hence _active_ form ἡγέω, Hdn.Gr. 2.950}}. (**sāg-**, cf. Latin _praesagio._)

2234:
Included with: ** ἡδύς**, ἡδεῖα, ἡδύ, ἡδὺς ἀϋτμή
(as _feminine_) once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.369}}: _Doric dialect_ **ἁδύς** [ᾱ], _Boeotian dialect_ _neuter_ **ϝαδού** (written γάδου) conjecture in {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 17}} (compare _proper name_ ϝαδιούλογος {{Refs|“IG” 7.2788.3)}}, Elean **βᾱδύς** (which see): irregular _accusative_ ἁδέα for ἡδύν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 20.44, for ἡδεῖαν [prev. work] [8], 2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus 3.82: _Ionic dialect_ _feminine_ ἡδέᾰ, _Doric dialect_ ἁδέα: _comparative_ ἡδίων [ῑ]}}, _superlative_ ἥδιστος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.80, etc.; also ἡδύτερος 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.2.1, 1st c.AD(?): Pseudo-Phocylidea 195, “Anthologia Graeca” 9.247 (1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus)}}; ἡδύτατος{{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. work] 11.298.7, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.98e.}}
**I** **pleasant** to the taste, _adverb_ (see. above),compare ἥδομαι. [ἥδῐον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 1101 (assuming variant), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 298 (Lyric poetry), 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 25.6, but ἡδίον (α) [ῑ] 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 251, etc.]}} (Sanskrit _svādús_, Latin _suāvis._)

2235:
** ἤδη**,
_adverb_:
**1** **already, by this time**, νὺξ ἤ. τελέθει 'tis **already** night, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.282; ἤ. Τρώεσσιν ὀλέθρου πείρατ᾽ ἐφῆπται [prev. work] 402}}: with numerals, ἤ. γὰρ τρίτον ἔτος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.89; τρίτην ἤ. ἡμέραν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 309d; ἔτος τόδ᾽ ἤ. δέκατον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 312}}; ἦν δ᾽ ἦμαρ ἤ. δεύτερον{{Refs|[prev. work] 354; τελοῦντες ἕκτον ἕβδομόν τ᾽ ἤ. δρόμον [prev. author] “El.” 726; ἤ. γὰρ πολὺς ἐκτέταται χρόνος [prev. author] “Aj.” 1402.}}
**2** **forthwith, immediately**, φρονέω δὲ διακρινθήμεναι ἤ. Ἁργείους καὶ Τρῶας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.98; λέξον νῦν με τάχιστα, ὄφρα καὶ ἤδη.. ταρπώμεθα κοιμηθέντες 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.635, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.294; ἤ. νῦν.. μεγάλ᾽ εὔχεο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.844}}; στείχοις ἂν ἤ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 624}}; ἤδη.. στέλλεσθ; {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 466}}; μετὰ τοῦτ᾽ ἤ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 655}}; ἤ. ποτέ **now at length**, Mitteis {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Chr.” 87.8 (2nd c.AD)}}; on the verso of a letter, **urgent, immediate, PCair. Zen.**154 {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|(3rd c.BC)}}; ἤ. ἤ. ταχὺ ταχύ {{Refs|“PMag.Osl.” 1.319.}}
**3** opposed to to the future or past, **actually, now**, οὐκ ὄναρ, ἀλλ᾽ ὕπαρ ἤ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.90; τοῖς μὲν γὰρ ἤ., τοῖς δ᾽ ἐν ὑστέρῳ χρόνῳ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 614}}; οἱ μὲν τάχ᾽, οἱ δ᾽ ἐσαῦθις, οἱ δ᾽ ἤ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 551}}; οὐ τάχ᾽, ἀλλ᾽ ἤ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 527}}; ἡ ἤ. χάρις **present** favour, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.134; τὸ ἤ. κολάζειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.7.24.}}
**4** of logical proximity, ἤ. γὰρ ἂν προστίθεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 201e}}; τὰ ἐκ τούτων ἤ. συγκείμενα{{Refs|[prev. work] 202b; πᾶς ἤ. ἂν εὕροι [prev. author] “R.” 398c; ἃν σὺ ὁμολογήσῃς, ταῦτ᾽ ἤ. ἐστὶν αὐτὰ τἀληθῆ [prev. author] “Grg.” 486e}}; τοῦτο τοὔπος δεινὸν ἤ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 315}}; τὰ πάντα τὰ πράγματα διαφθείραντα ταῦτ᾽ ἐστὶν ἤ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.19.}}
**4.b** καὶ ἤ. and **further, as well**, ἐμέ τε καὶ σὲ καὶ τἆλλ᾽ ἤ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 159b, compare 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 1.53, 219, etc.}}
**4.c** **for instance**, {{Refs|Aen. Tact. 4.1, 10.25.}}
**4.d** **only then, then and not before**, τότ᾽ ἤδη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 911, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.25, “Ep.” 6.9}}; ἐνταῦθ᾽ ἤ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.140; κακοπαθοῦντες ἤ. τῶν λόγων ἅπτονται 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.78.}}
**5** with _superlative_, ὦ πάντων ἀνδρῶν ἤ. μάλιστα.. κτησάμενε **up to this time**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.106; μέγιστος ἤ. διάπλους 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.31}}: with _comparative_, ἤδη.. λόγου μέζων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.148.}}
**II** joined with other words of time, ἤ. νῦν **now already**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.456, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1578}}; νῦν ἤ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.54, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 801 (_anapaest meter_); ἤ. ποτέ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.260, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1142, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 346}}; ποτ᾽ ἤ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 50; ἤ. πώποτε 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 214, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 493d}}; πάλαι ἤ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 510 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἤ. τότε **even then**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 417b}}; ἐπεὶ ἤ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.260}}; εἰ ἤ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.52}}; τὸ τηνίκ᾽ ἤ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 440}}; τὸ λοιπὸν ἤ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 454; ἄλλοτε ἤ. πολλάκις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 507a}}; ἤ. γε even **now**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.52.}}
**III** of place, ἀπὸ ταύτης ἤ. Αἴγυπτος after this Egypt begins, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.5, compare 2.15, 4.99, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1200; Φωκεῦσιν ἤ. ὅμορος ἡ Βοιωτία ἐστίν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.95. (In general, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 222b7.)}}

2236:
Included with: ** ἡδύς**, ἡδεῖα, ἡδύ, ἡδὺς ἀϋτμή
(as _feminine_) once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.369}}: _Doric dialect_ **ἁδύς** [ᾱ], _Boeotian dialect_ _neuter_ **ϝαδού** (written γάδου) conjecture in {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 17}} (compare _proper name_ ϝαδιούλογος {{Refs|“IG” 7.2788.3)}}, Elean **βᾱδύς** (which see): irregular _accusative_ ἁδέα for ἡδύν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 20.44, for ἡδεῖαν [prev. work] [8], 2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus 3.82: _Ionic dialect_ _feminine_ ἡδέᾰ, _Doric dialect_ ἁδέα: _comparative_ ἡδίων [ῑ]}}, _superlative_ ἥδιστος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.80, etc.; also ἡδύτερος 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.2.1, 1st c.AD(?): Pseudo-Phocylidea 195, “Anthologia Graeca” 9.247 (1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus)}}; ἡδύτατος{{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. work] 11.298.7, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.98e.}}
**I** **pleasant** to the taste, _adverb_ (see. above),compare ἥδομαι. [ἥδῐον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 1101 (assuming variant), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 298 (Lyric poetry), 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 25.6, but ἡδίον (α) [ῑ] 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 251, etc.]}} (Sanskrit _svādús_, Latin _suāvis._)

2237:
** ἡδονή**,
_Doric dialect_ **ἁδονά** (or in Trag. chorus ἡδονά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1339)}}, ἡ, (ἥδομαι) **enjoyment, pleasure**, first in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 71, S.[same place], 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.24, +others}}; properly of sensual pleasures, αἱ τοῦ σώματος or περὶ τὸ σῶμα ἡ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.8.22, 6.1.4}}; αἱ κατὰ τὸ σῶμα ἡ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 328d}}; σωματικαὶ ἡ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1151a13}}; αἱ περὶ πότους καὶ περὶ ἐδωδὰς ἡ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 389e}}; but also ἀκοῆς ἡ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.38}}; ἡ ἀπὸ τοῦ εἰδέναι ἡ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 582b}}; of **malicious pleasure**, ἡ ἐπὶ τοῖς τῶν φίλων κακοῖς, ἐπὶ ταῖς λοιδορίαις ἡ., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Phlb.” 50a, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.138}}; ἡδονῇ ἡσσᾶσθαι, ἡδοναῖς χαρίζεσθαι, to give way **to pleasure**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus [same place], 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 727c}}; κότερα ἀληθείη χρήσομαι ἢ ἡδον; shall I speak truly or **so as to humour you?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.101; εἰ ὑμῖν ἡδονὴ τοῦ ἡγεμονεύειν [prev. work] 160}}; ἡ. εἰσέρχεταί τινι εἰ.. one feels **pleasure** at the thought that.., {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.24}}; ἡδονὴν ἔχειν τινός to be **satisfied with.. **, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1604}}; ἡδονὴν ἔχει, φέρει, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 145.2, 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 263.6}}; ἡδονὴ ἰδέσθαι (like θαῦμα ἰδέσθαι), of a temple, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.137}}: with Preps. in _adverb_ sense, δαίμοσιν πρὸς ἡδονήν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 494}}; ὃ μέν ἐστι πρὸς ἡ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.4}}; πρὸς ἡ. λέγειν to speak so **as to please another**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 921, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.65; δημηγορεῖν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.38; οὐ πρὸς ἡ. οἱ ἦν τὰ ἀγγελλόμενα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.126; πάντα πρὸς ἡ. ἀκούοντας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.34; later πρὸς ἡδονῆς εἶναί τινι 1st c.BC: Parthenius Mythographus 8.8, 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 12.1; καθ᾽ ἡδονὴν κλύειν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 197}}; καθ᾽ ἡδονήν [ἐστί] μοι with _infinitive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 263}}; καθ᾽ ἡ. τι δρᾶν, ποιεῖν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.37, 53; καθ᾽ ἡδονὰς τῷ δήμῳ τὰ πράγματα ἐνδιδόναι [prev. work] 65}}; ἐν ἡδονῇ ἐστί τινι it is **a pleasure** or **delight** to another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.139}}; followed by _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 494}}; by _accusative_ et _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.15}}; ἐν ἡδονῇ ἔχειν τινάς to take **pleasure** in them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.9}}; ἐν ἡδονῇ ἄρχοντες, opposed to οἱ λυπηροί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.99; μεθ᾽ ἡδονῆς [prev. author] 4.19; ὑφ᾽ ἡδονῆς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 648, etc.}}; ὑπὸ τῆς ἡ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 24, 110.23: as _dative_ modi, ἡδονᾷ}} **with pleasure**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1339 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.137 (_falsa lectio_).}}
**2** concrete, **a pleasure**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 873 (_plural_), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1072 (_plural_)}}; ἡδοναὶ τραγημάτων **sweetmeats**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Sopater Comicus 17.}}
**3** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus}}, **desires after pleasure, pleasant lusts**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.23, NT.Tit.3.3, +others}}
**II** in _Ionic dialect_ Philosophers, **taste, flavour**, usually joined with χροιή, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Diogenes Apolloniates Philosophus 5, 5th c.BC: Anaxagoras Philosophus 4 (_plural_), compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 660b9, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.4.7, LXX.Num.11.8, 4th c.BC: Eudemus Philosophus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 9.369f, Mnesith. cited in [same work] 8.357f.}}

2238:
** ἡδύ-οσμος**, ον,
**sweet-smelling, fragrant**, στρώματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 695.}}
**II** **ἡδύοσμον**, τό,= μίνθη, **green mint, Mentha viridis**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 7.7.1, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 8.3.14}}; ἡ. ἄγριον **wild mint, M. longifolia**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 3.34. [As _trisyllable_, “Anthologia Graeca” 11.413 (2nd c.AD: Ammianus Epigrammaticus).]}}

2239:
** ἦθος**, εος, τό
(compare ἔθος), **an accustomed place**: hence, in _plural_, **haunts** or **abodes** of animals, μετά τ᾽ ἤθεα καὶ νομὸν ἵππων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.511; [σύας] ἔρξαν κατὰ ἤθεα κοιμηθῆναι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.411}}; of lions, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.125}}; of fish, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 1.93}}; of **the abodes of men**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 167, 525, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.15, 157, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 64 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 274, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 865e, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 398b33}}; ἔλεγον ἐξ ἠθέων τὸν ἥλιον ἀνατεῖλαι away from his **accustomed place**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.142}}; of plants, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Callistratus Sophista “Statuarum descriptiones” 7}}: _metaphorically_, with play on _signification_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|11, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 277a.}}
**II** **custom, usage**: in _plural_, **manners, customs**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 137, “Th.” 66, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.30, 35, 4.106, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.61; τρόποι καὶ ἤθη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 896c}}; ἐθρέψω Ξέρξην ἐν τοῖς αὐτοῖς ἤ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|. [prev. work] 695e; φθείρουσιν ἤθη χρήσθ᾽ ὁμιλίαι κακαί “PHib.” 1.7.94 (5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 1024 = 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 218); τοῖς ἤθεσιν ἁπλοῦς 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 5.21.}}
**II.2** **disposition, character**, ἐπίκλοπον ἦθος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 67, 78; ἦ. ἐμφυές 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 11(10).21}}; ἀκίχητα ἤ., of Zeus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 187; τοὐμὸν ἦ. παιδεύειν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 595}}; ὦ μιαρὸν ἦ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ant.” 746}}; τὸ τῆς πόλεως ἦ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.31}}; βελτίων τῆς πόλεως τὸ ἦ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.14}}; especially **moral character**, opposed to διάνοια, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1139a1}}; as the result of habit, τὸ πᾶν ἦ. διὰ ἔθος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 792e, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Eudemia” 1220a39; ἦ. ἀνθρώπῳ δαίμων 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 119}}; ἦ. πηγὴ βίου Stoic. cited in {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 2.7.1}}; τῆς ψυχῆς, τῆς γνώμης, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 400d, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 61.16: frequently opposed to πάθος, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1356a23 (_plural_), etc.; ἠθῶν τε καὶ παθῶν μίμησις 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Epistula ad Pompeium” 3; τὸ ἦ. πρᾶος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 243c: less frequently in _dative_, ἀγοραῖος τῷ ἤ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 6.2, compare “Inscription Magn.” 164.3 (1st-2nd c.AD)}}: _plural_, **traits, characteristics**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 402d, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1144b4 (in _sing._, τὸ τῆς ἀνδρείας ἦ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 836d)}}: seldom in _plural_, of an individual, στερρὸν τὰ ἤθεα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιστολαί” 11; ἱερὸς κατὰ τὰ ἤθη 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 1.1e.}}
**II.2.b** of outward **bearing**, ὡς ἱλαρὸν τὸ ἦ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 8.3; ὑγρότης ἤθους 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 33}}; ὑψηλὸς τῷ ἤ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Dio” 4}}: in _plural_, of facial expression, ὀφθαλμῶν ἤθη {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “de gymnastica” 25.}}
**II.2.c** in Rhet., **delineation of character**, ἦ. ἔχουσιν οἱ λόγοι ἐν ὅσοις δήλη ἡ προαίρεσις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1395b13; ἦ. ἐμφαίνειν 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.200S.}}; especially opposed to πάθος, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 9.15, etc.}}; κατ᾽ ἦ. λέγεσθαι, opposed to κατὰ πάθος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 22, compare “Lys.” 19: in _plural_, πραγμάτων καὶ ἠθῶν 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ ποιημάτων” 5.5; ἐν πάθεσι καὶ ἤθεσιν 4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 28, etc.}}; so of works of art, ἡ Ζεύξιδος γραφὴ οὐδὲν ἔχει ἦ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1450a29; πάθος καὶ ἦ. καὶ σχημάτων χρῆσις 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 4.3; πολλὰ ἤθη ἐπιφαίνει 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Heroicus” 2.10}}; also of Music, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 6.49.}}
**II.2.d** **dramatis persona**, εἰσάγει ἄνδρα ἢ γυναῖκα ἢ ἄλλο τι ἦ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1450a11, +others}}
**II.3** also of animals, ἦ. τὸ πρὸς τοκέων (probable reading for ἔθος) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 727, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1219, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 375e, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 487a12 (_plural_); τὸ ἦ. ἀσθενής, of a bird, [prev. work] 615a18}}; of things, **nature, kind**, παρὰ δ᾽ ἦ. ἑκάστῳ (to each of the four elements) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 17.28; τοῦ πυρετοῦ 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 7.353.}}
**II.4** ἐν ἤθει **tactfully** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|compare (ἠθικός 11.2), προσφέρεσθαι τοῖς ἁμαρτάνουσιν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.73e. compare 5th c.AD: Hermias Alexandrinus Philosophus “in Platonis Phaedrum scholia” p.195A.}}; διὰ μέτριον ἦθος, of the expression δοκεῖ μοι, {{Refs 7th c.AD+|7th c.AD: Stephanus Medicus “in Hp.” 1.59D.}}

2240:
** ἥκω**,
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.478:}}_imperfect_ ἧκον (see. below): _future_ ἥξω (see. below); _Doric dialect_ ἡξῶ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 4.47, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 1.65}} P. (in _Doric dialect_ and {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus more commonly ἵκω)}}: all other tenses late; _aorist 1_ _participle_ ἥξας {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 2.11.5, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.56, 10.609}}: _perfect_ ἧκα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 3.24, 2nd c.BC: Scymnus Geographus 62}}, _1st pers._ _plural_ ἥκαμεν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 72.9 (2nd c.BC), “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 4762 (Egypt, 1st c.AD), _Doric dialect_ ἥκαμες}} _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.225b}}, _2nd pers._ _plural_ ἥκατε {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“PGrenf.” 2.36.18 (1st c.BC)}}, _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἥκασι {{LXX+NT|LXX.2Ki.20.14, NT.Mark.8.3}}; _infinitive_ ἡκέναι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 6.30 (2nd c.BC)}}: _pluperfect_ ἥκεσαν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 19.1.14}}: —_middle_, _present_ _subjunctive_ ἥκηται {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων χρονίων παθῶν” 2.1:}}_future_ ἥξομαι{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|variant in 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 2.4}}:—**to have come, be present**, properly in a _perfect_ sense, with _imperfect_ ἧκον as _pluperfect_, I **had come**, and _future_ ἥξω as _future_ _perfect_, I **shall have come**, μάλα τηλόθεν ἥκω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.478, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.325, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 4.12 (ἵκω codices vett.)}}: _imperfect_ ἧκον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 661, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.91, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 327c, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.50, etc.}}: _future_ ἥξω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 103, +others, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 738, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 265, Oracle texts cited in 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.54, etc.}}; ἧκε _imperative_, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1116, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 275, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.25; ἡκέτω 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 337:—Constr. mostly with εἰς, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.50, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 3, etc.; παρά τινα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.157, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.137; πρός τινα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 659; πρὸς δαίμονα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 770; especially in worship, ἥκω πρὸς τὴν κυρίαν Ἶσιν “OGI” 186.6 (Egypt, 1st c.BC),cf. NT.John.6.37; πρὸς πόλιν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 734}}; ἐπί τινα **to set upon, attack**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 336b, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.178}}; but ἥ. ἐπὶ τὸ στράτευμα **to have come to fetch** the army, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.6.2; οἱ ἐπὶ ταῦθ᾽ ἥκοντες 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.28; ἐπ᾽ ὀλέθρῳ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 886 (troch.); περὶ σπονδῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.3.4: with _accusative_, ἥξεις ποταμόν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 717, compare 724, 730; ἥ. δῆμον τὸν Λυρκείου 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 271.6, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1; ἥκουσιν αὐτῷ ἄγγελοι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.3.26}}; ἐς ταὐτὸν ἥ. **to have come** to the same point, to agree, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 748, “Hipp.” 273: with}} _adverb_ of motion, ἥ. ἐνθάδε, δεῦρο{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 377, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.58; βῆναι κεῖθεν ὅθενπερ ἥκει 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1227: with _neuter_ _pronoun_, αὐτὰ ταῦτα ἥκω παρά σε 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 310e}}; ἐρωτώμενοι ὅ τι ἥκοιεν for what they **had come**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.5.9: with _accusative_ _cognate_, ὁδὸν μακρὰν ἥκειν [prev. author] “Cyr.” 5.5.42: c.}}_infinitive_, μανθάνειν γὰρ ἥκομεν we **are here** to learn, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 12.}}
**2** **to have reached a point**, ἐς τοσήνδ᾽ ὕβριν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1030; εἰς τοῦτο ἀμαθίας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 170; εἰς τοσοῦτον ἀμαθίας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 25e; εἰς ὅσον ἡλικίας [prev. author] “Chrm.” 157d, etc.; πρὸς γάμων ἀκμάς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1492; ὁρᾷς ἵν᾽ ἥκει; [prev. work] 687}}, etc.; Geometry texts, **pass** through a point, διὰ τῶν πόλων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Autolycus Astronomus “Sph.” 10, compare 3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ κωνοειδέων καὶ σφαιροειδέων” 9.}}
**2.b** διὰ μάχης, δι᾽ ὀργῆς ἥκειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 475, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 905; compare διά 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus IV.}}
**2.c** with an _adverb_ followed by _genitive_, οὕτω πόρρω σοφίας ἥκεις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 294e}}; εὖ ἥκειν τινός **to be** well **off for** a thing, **have plenty of** it, τοῦ βίου, χρημάτων, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.30, 5.62; ἑωυτῶν [prev. author] 1.102; θεῶν χρηστῶν [prev. author] 8.111}}; πιθανότητος Demetr.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Magnes Comicus cited in 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Dinarcho” 1}}; οὐκ ὁμοίως ἥ. τινός not **to be** equally **well off** in respect of.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.149}}; πῶς ἀγῶνος ἥκομε; how **have** we **sped** in the contest? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 751}}; ὧδε γένους ἥ. τινί **to be** this **degree** of kin to him, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Heracl.” 213; ὡς δυνάμεως ἥκεις 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 4.21.10; ἐς μῆκος εὖ ἥκων 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 4.34: _absolutely_, εὖ ἥκειν}} **to be flourishing**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.30: rarely with _genitive_ only, σὺ δὲ δυνάμιος ἥκεις μεγάλης}} thou **art in** great power, {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.157 (nisi to be read μεγάλως).}}
**3** **to have come back, returned**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.73; from exile, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.13}}; αὐτίκα ἥξω I **shall be back** in a moment, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.1.9}}; ἧκέ νυν ταχύ **come back** soon, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 275; ἄψορρον ἥξεις 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1021; ἄψορρον ἥξομεν πάλιν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 53.}}
**4** with _participle_, ἥκω φέρων I **have come** bringing (i.e. with), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “OC” 579, compare 357, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 265, 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 22 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 518d; ἧκεν ἄγων [prev. author] “Phd.” 117a; ἕτερόν τι ἥκεις ἕχων [prev. author] “Grg.” 491c, etc.: with}}_future_ _participle_, like{ἔρχομαι}, ἥκω φράσων, ἀγγελῶν, etc., I **am going**, I **intend** to say, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 706, 1075, etc.}}
**5** **to have come to be**, θεοῖς ἔχθιστος ἥκω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1519 (troch.), compare “Aj.” 636 (Lyric poetry), “El.” 1201, etc.}}; **take one's origin**, ἀπὸ πολιτειῶν τοιούτων ἥκετε, ἐν αἷς.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.126.}}
**II** of things, in various uses: of meats, **to have come to table**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 132; ὡς τὰ περιφερόμενα ἧκε πρὸς ἡμᾶς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.2.3}}; of reports, ἐμοὶ ἀγγελίη ἥκει παρὰ βασιλέος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.140.ά, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1177}}; of events, πῆμα ἥκει τινί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 103, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 606, etc.; ἐπ᾽ ἀνδρὶ ἥκει βίον τελευτή 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1472}}; ἵν᾽ ἥκειτὰ μαντεύματα what they **have come to**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “OT” 953; ὡς αὐτὸν ἥξοι μοῖρα [prev. work] 713}} codices; ἥξει πόλεμος Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.54; ἐς αὐτὸν ἥξει τὸ δεινόν [prev. author] 6.77}}; of Time, ἥκει ἦμαρ, νύξ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1301, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 42; ἥκει ὑμῖν ὁ καιρός 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.79; τὸ μέλλον ἥξει 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1240.}}
**II.2** **concern, relate to**, ποῖ λόγος ἥκε; to what **do** the words **relate?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 154 (Lyric poetry); εἰς ἔμ᾽ ἥκει.. τὰ πράγματα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 919}}; εἰς ἐμὲ τὸ ἐλλεῖπον ἥξει **will fall** upon me, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.5.13}}: frequently in _participle_, τὰ εἰς τοὺς κινδύνους ἥκοντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.81}}; τὰ εἰς πλοῦτον ἥ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Eryxias” 392d}}; τὰ πρὸς ἔπαινον, εἰς φιλανθρωπίαν ἥ., {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 12.15.9, 28.17.2, etc.}}
**II.3** **depend upon**, ὅσα τῆς Φωκέων σωτηρίας ἐπὶ τὴν πρεσβείαν ἧκε {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.30; τό γε ἐπ᾽ αὐτοὺς ἧκον μέρος 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.21; ὅσα γ᾽ εἰς βούλησιν ἥκειν τὴν ἐμήν 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 4.7.}}
**II.4** with _infinitive_, ἧκέ μοι γένει.. πενθεῖν **it has come** to me by birth.., my birth lays it on me.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 738, compare “Ichn.” 356}}; καλῶς αὐτοῖς κατθανεῖν ἧκον βίου **it being well for them at their age to die**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 291.}}
**II.5** with _participle_, ὃ καὶ νῦν ἥκει γινόμενον which **commonly** happens even now, [{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 24.9.11 codices (variant{γενόμενον}). (Prob. from same root as ἵκω.)}}]

2244:
** ἡλῐκί-α**,
_Ionic dialect_ ἡλικίη, _Doric dialect_ **ἁλικία**, ἡ, (ἧλιξ) **time of life, age**, ἤν πως ἡλικίην αἰδέσσεται ἠδ᾽ ἐλεήσῃ γῆρας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.419; γηραιὸν μέρος ἁλικίας 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.157; παρὰ τὸν ἁλικίας ἐοικότα χρόνον [prev. author] “O.” 4.29}}; τήνδ᾽ ἡ. ἀστῶν, i.e. their old **age**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 914}}: _accusative_ used adverbially, **in age**, νέος ἡλικίην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.134; ἐτέων ἐὼν ἡλικίην πέντε καὶ τριήκοντα [prev. author] 1.26, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 2.3}}: so in _dative_, ἡλικίᾳ ἔτι τότε ὢν νέος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.43}}; προεληλυθότες ταῖς ἡ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.1.4}}; also ὑπὸ τῆς ἡ. from our **age**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 180d; αἱ δι᾽ ἡλικίαν ἄτοκοι [prev. author] “Tht.” 149c}}; οἱ ἐν τῇ αὐτῆ ἡ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.80}}; τὸ ἀχρεῖον τῆς ἡ. {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.44}}; ὅταν.. τοῦ γεννᾶν ἐκβῶσι τὴν ἡ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 461b}}; πόρρω τῆς ἡ. to an advanced **age**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Grg.” 484c}}; προήκων ἐς βαθὺ τῆς ἡ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 514}}; προϊούσης τῆς ἡ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 279a}}; ὁ παρ᾽ ἡλικίαν νοῦς beyond **one's age**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 690: in _plural_, ἐν ἁπάσαις ταῖς ἡ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 412e, compare “Lg.” 625b, +others}}
**2** **prime of life, manhood**, ἐν ἁλικίᾳ πρώτᾳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 9.42; αὐτὴ ἡ ἡ. τῶν νέων κατέκρινε 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.4.2}}; ἡλικίαν ἔχειν, εἰς ἡ. ἐλθεῖν, ἀφικέσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 306d, “Tht.” 142d, “Men.” 89b}}; ἡλικίην ἔχειν with _infinitive_, to be **of fit age** for doing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.209, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 146b; ἡλικίας μετέχειν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.60}}; οἱ ἐν τῇ ἡλικίᾳ men **of military age**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 8.75; ἐν ἡλικίᾳ στρατεύεσθαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.7; ἐστρατευμένος ἁπάσας τὰς ἐν ἡλικίᾳ στρατείας [prev. author] 21.95; οἱ τῆς ἡ. ἐντὸς γεγονότες 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.50}}; ἡ καθεστηκυῖα ἡ. maturity, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.36, compare “IG” 12(7).239.21 (from Amorgos)}}; of women, **womanhood, marriageable age**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προρρητικόν” 2.30, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.22; αἱ ἐν ἡ. γυναῖκες 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 461b; τὴν ἡλικίαν τὴν ἑαντοῦ καταμεμψάμενος 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 7.14: in _plural_, οἱ ταῖς ἡ. οὐ καλῶς κεχρημένοι 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.194.}}
**3** **youthful passion**, ἡλικίῃ καὶ θυμῷ ἐπιτρέπειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.36; εἴκειν [prev. author] 7.18.}}
**4** **maidenhood**, τὴν ἡ. οὐ καλῶς διαφυλάξασαν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.182.}}
**II** as collective Noun,= οἱ ἥλικες, **those of the same age, comrades**, ὃς ἡλικίην ἐκέκαστο ἔγχεϊ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.808, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.74}}; especially **those of military age**, τῆς ἡ. ἀπούσης ἐν ταῖς ναυσί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.49, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.67, 8.1, etc.}}; also, **men of any age**, παίδων τε καὶ ἀνδρῶν καὶ πάσης ἡ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 959e.}}
**III** **time**, ταῦτα ἡλικίην ἂν εἴη κατὰ Λάϊον about the **time** of Laius, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.59, compare 60, 71; ἡ. τετρακοσίοισι ἔτεσι.. πρεσβυτέρους [prev. author] 2.53.}}
**IV** **age, generation**, ἐπὶ τῆς νῦν ἡ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.167}}; πρὸ τῆς ἡμετέρας ἡ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.38}}; εἰς τὴν νῦν ζῶσαν ἡ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 60.11}}; πολλαῖς ἔμπροσθεν ἡ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 27, compare 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 5.37.}}
**V** of the body, **stature**, as a sign of age, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.16, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 271b, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 40.56; τῇ ἡ. μικρός NT.Luke.19.3 (but προσθεῖναι ἐπὶ τὴν ἡ. πῆχυν ἕνα}} add a cubit to one's **age** (compare πήχυιος), {{NT|NT.Matt.6.27)}}; ἄνδρας ἡμισταδιαίους τὰς ἡ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “VH” 1.40}}; **height** of a pillar, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Syr.D.” 28.}}

2245:
** ἡλίκος** [ῐ], η, ον,
**as big as**, πόσος τις; μικρός, ἡλίκος Μόλων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 55}}; τί τοσοῦτον ὕβρισεν, ἡλίκον..; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.147; τηλικοῦτος, ἡ. οὐδείς πω βασιλεύς [prev. author] 1.9.}}
**2** of age, **as old as**, ἄνδρα.. ἡλίκον Θουκυδίδην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 703}}; τοῖσιν ἡλίκοισι νῷν, ={τηλίκοις ἡλίκοι νώ}, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ec.” 465}}; οἱ ἡλίκοι ἐγώ,= τηλίκοι ἡλίκοι ἐγώ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 180d: rare in Trag., ὁρᾷς μὲν ἡμᾶς, ἡλίκοι..}} **of what various ages.. **, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 15.}}
**3** in indirect questions, **how big, how great**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 23.2, 4th c.BC: Crates Thebanus Poeta Philosophus 18, etc.; ὁρῶν ἡ. ἐστὶ Φίλιππος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 6.6, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 154b}}; frequently in expressions of wonder, θαυμάσι᾽ ἡλίκα **extraordinarily great**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.24; θαυμαστὸν ἡλίκον [prev. author] 24.122; μέγιστα ἡλίκα 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Merc.Cond.” 13}}; also, **how small**, ἰδοὺ ἡλίκον πῦρ ἡλίκην ὕλην ἀνάπτει {{NT+4th c.BC+|NT.Jam.3.5; ἂν ἴδω γὰρ ἡλίκον ἰχθὺν ὅσου τιμῶσι 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 166.6, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 5.}}
**4** in exclamations, ἡλίκον λαλεῖς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 40}}. (Compd. of **yo-**, _relative_ _pronoun_ stem (compare ὅς), and **-āli-** (compare ἧλιξ), with suffix -κο-; compare πηλίκος, τηλίκος.)

2246:
** ἥλιος**, ὁ,
_Epic dialect_ **ἠέλιος**, as always in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} (except in the late passage {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.271) and 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus, compare 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τροφῆς” 42}}: _Doric dialect_ **ἀέλιος** [ᾱ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.144, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Cererem” 92, “Lav.Pall.” 89, and Lyric poetry in Trag., 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 809, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 175, +others}}, but **ἅλιος** [ᾱ], {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 96, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 395 (ᾰέλιος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 835)}}: Cretan dialect **ἀβέλιος** (i.e. ἀϝ-), {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}: _Aeolic dialect_ **ἀέλιος** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 79(= “Oxy.” 1787 “fragment” 1.25), “Supp.” 25.7}}; **ἄλιος** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 69 (assuming variant): 4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus}} **ἀέλιος** (or ἁ-) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 5(2).4.12 (Tegea, 4th c.BC)}}:— **sun**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.421, etc.}}; ὁρᾶν φάος ἠελίοιο to see **the light of life, live**, {{Refs|18.61}}, etc.; ὑπ᾽ ἠελίῳ τε καὶ οὐρανῷ ἀστερόεντι ναιετάουσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4.44; γυνὴ.. ἀρίστη τῶν ὑφ᾽ ἡλίῳ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 151; οὐκέτ᾽ ἔστιν ὑφ᾽ ἁλίῳ [prev. work] 395}}; also ὑπὸ ἡλίου ἑωρᾶσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.102; οἱ ὑπὸ τοῦτον τὸν ἥλιον ἄνθρωποι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.270; τριῶν τῶν ὑπὸ τὸν ἥ. μεγίστων ἡγεμονιῶν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lucullus” 30: proverbial, οὐδ᾽ ὁ ἥ. εἴσεται 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 7.21}}; ὥσπερ σελήνη γ᾽ ἡλίῳ (i.e. ὅμοιος) a pale reflection, {{Refs|“Comica Adespota” 5.15D.}}
**2** to determine the cardinal points, πρὸς ἠῶ τ᾽ ἠέλιόν τε towards the {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“East”, opposed to πρὸς ζόφον: εἴτ᾽ ἐπὶ δεξί᾽ ἴωσι πρὸς ἠῶ τ᾽ ἠέλιόν τε, εἴτ᾽ ἐπ᾽ ἀριστερὰ τοί γε ποτὶ ζόφον ἠερόεντα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.239, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.26; ὅσοι ναίουσι πρὸς ἠῶ τ᾽ ἠέλιόν τε, ἠδ᾽ ὅσσοι μετόπισθε ποτὶ ζόφον 13.240}}; πρὸς ἠῶ τε καὶ ἡλίου ἀνατολάς, opposed to πρὸς ἑσπέρην, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.58; τὰ πρὸς ἠῶ τε καὶ ἥλιον ἀνατέλλοντα [prev. author] 4.40}}; οἱ ἀπὸ ἡλίου ἀνατολέων Αἰθίοπες the **eastern.. **, {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.70.}}
**3** **day**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 424}}; **a day**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.37, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τροφῆς” 42, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 652 (_plural_), Ps.- 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Philopatr.” 4,26, etc.}}; later, **year**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 10.1.}}
**4** **sunshine, sun's heat**, ἐπὶ τοῖς ὄρεσιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 116e; ἥ. πολύς 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nav.” 35, compare “Herm.” 25}}; πολὺντὸν ἥ. ἐμφαίνειν, of a sunburnt person, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ind.” 3, compare “Rh.Pr.” 9}}: _plural_, **sunbeams**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Signis Tempestatum” 22, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 16.17}}; **hot sunny days**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.87.}}
**5** _metaphorically_, **sunshine, brightness**, ψυχῆς {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.994e, compare 2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 2.36, etc.}}; of a person, Ἑλλάνων δόξης δεύτερον Ἀέλιον {{Refs|“IG” 14.1188}}; of {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Ptolemaeus Epigrammaticus VI, “UPZ” 15.33}}; νέος Ἥ., of Nero and Caligula, {{Refs|“SIG” 814.34, 798.3.}}
**II** as _proper name_, **Helios, the sun-god**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.271, etc.}}; νὴ τὸν Ἥ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 108}}; ὑπὸ Δία Γῆν Ἥλιον, in manumission-formula, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“POxy.” 48.6, 49.8 (1st c.AD), “IG” 9(1).412 (from Aetolia), “IPE” 2.54.10(3rd c.AD)}}; [Ἥλιος] δούλους ἐλευθέρους ποιεῖ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 2.36}}; identified with Apollo, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Carmina Popularia” 12, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 781.11; with Dionysus, 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 31.11, etc.}}
**II.2** Ἡλίου ἀστήρ, of the planet {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Saturn”, variant in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epinomis” 987c, compare 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 2.30, 2nd c.AD: Theon Smyrnaeus Philosophus p.130H. (I.- 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **sāwelios**, cf. Cretan dialect ἀβέλιος, Lithuanian _sáulė_, Latin _sōl._)

2247:
** ἧλος**,
_Doric dialect_ **ἇλος**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 4.1484.62(Epid.), “SIG” 245i69 (Delph., 4th c.BC), 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus (see. below), _Aeolic dialect_ (?)}} **ϝάλλοι** (_plural_) written γάλλοι, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}: ὁ:— **nail-head, stud**, as an ornament, σκῆπτρον χρυσείοις ἥλοισι πεπαρμένον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.246, compare 11.633}}; ἐν δέ οἱ [τῷ ξίφει] ἧλοι χρύσειοι πάμφαινον{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 29, compare 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 11.488b}}, c: hence, of the stars, {{Refs|“Placita Philosophorum” 2.14.3.}}
**2** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, **nail**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.71: ἧλοι σιδηροῖ καὶ ξύλινοι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 9.12, etc.}}; of shoenails, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 4.13: proverbial, ἥλῳ ἐκκρούειν τὸν ἧλον 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Laps.” 7}}; ἥλῳ ὁ ἧλος (i.e. ἐκκρούεται) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1314a5, etc.}}
**3** = Latin _acutus_ (= **spur**), {{Refs|“Corpus Glossariorum Latinorum”}}
**II** **wart, callus**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Igne” 37, 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 272; ἧλοι καὶ τύλοι 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.104, compare 3rd c.BC: Asclepiades Epigrammaticus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 13.647; also on plants, especially the olive, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.14.3.}}

2248:
** ἐγώ**,
I: _pronoun_ of the first person:—_Epic dialect_ mostly **ἐγών** before vowels (so in _Doric dialect_, before consonants, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 85, 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 81, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 748, 754), rarely in Trag., 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 932 (Lyric poetry)}}; _Boeotian dialect_ **ἱών** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 51.4}}:— strengthened **ἔγωγε**, **I at least, for my part, indeed, for myself** (more frequently in _Attic dialect_ than in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus)}}: _Doric dialect_ **ἐγώνγα** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 51, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 736, “Lys.” 986, _dative_ ἐμίνγα “IG” 22.1126.7 (Amphict. Delph.)}}: _Boeotian dialect_ **ἱώνγα** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 21}}; **ἱώνει** {{Refs|Ead. 10}}; **ἰώγα** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 898}}: _Laconian dialect_ and _Tarentum dialect_ **ἐγώνη**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Conjunctionibus;” 255.29.}}
**II** oblique cases from a different root, _genitive_ ἐμοῦ, _enclitic_ μο; _Ionic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ ἐμέο, ἐμεῦ, μευ, also ἐμέθεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.525, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 177 (Lyric poetry)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἔμεθεν {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 23.7; ἐμεῖο “IG” 3.1337; μεθέν 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 20}}; _Doric dialect_ ἐμέος, ἐμεῦς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 144}}; _Boeotian dialect_ ἐμοῦς {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 37; also ἐμῶς, ἐμίο, ἐμίω, ἐμίως 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 74.17:}} — _dative_ ἐμοί, _enclitic_ μοι (which may be compared with Sanskrit _genitive_ **me** in κλῦθί μοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.115, +others); ὅ μοι πόσις “Schwyzer” 683 (5th c.AD: Cypria)}}; _Doric dialect_ ἐμίν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 99, “AJA” 29.461 (Rhodian, 5th c.BC), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 733, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 4.30}}; _Tarentum dialect_ ἐμίνη {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Rhinthon Comicus 13: _accusative_ ἐμέ}}, _enclitic_ μ; {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Cypria μι “Inscription Cyprian dialect” 59,60 H.}}
**III** dual, _nominative_ and _accusative_, **νῶι**, **we two**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.34, etc.}}; _accusative_ νῶιν Zenod.ad {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.377}}; _Attic dialect_ νώ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 278b (also 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.219, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.475); νῶε 5th-6th c.BC: Antimachus Colophonius Elegiacus et Epicus 39, 6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 5: _genitive_, _dative_ νῶι; νῷν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 3}}; νῶι _dative_, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Lithica” 773}}; νῶιν, ={ἡμῖν}, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 1.213, etc.}}
**IV** _plural_, _nominative_ ἡμεῖς (ἡμέες _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.6, +others, rejected by 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 93.1)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἄμμες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.303, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 18.3, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.144}}; _Doric dialect_ ἁμές {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 65, 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 42, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 168:— _genitive_ ἡμῶν (also ἥμων 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 130.23)}}; _Ionic dialect_ ἡμέων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.112, etc.; ἡμείων 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.170, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 1.46}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἀμμέων {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 88, “Milet.” 3 No.152.29}}; ἄμμων {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work]74, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 95.3}}; _Doric dialect_ ἁμέων {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 66}}; ἁμῶν [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus] 266, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 168, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.158}}; Cretan dialect, _Boeotian dialect_ ἁμίων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 528.5, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 95.21:— _dative_ ἡμῖν, in 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus also ἡμίν}} (ῐ) (or ἧμιν Aristarch.ad {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.214, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 95.3)}}; also rarely in Comedy texts, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Phrynichus Comicus 37, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 386 (uncertain)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἄμμῐν, ἄμμῐ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.384, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 80, +others, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.155, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 156 (Lyric poetry), “Milet.” 3 No.152; ἄμμεσιν 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 100}}; _Doric dialect_ also ἁμίν or ἇμιν, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 77, 78, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 347 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1081}}; with ῑ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ach.” 821, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.145:— _accusative_ ἡμᾶς (also ἧμᾰς 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.372)}}; _Ionic dialect_ ἡμέας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.211, “SIG” 273.25 (Milet., 4th c.BC)}}; ἥμεας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.294 (cf. Hdn.Gr. 2.140)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἄμμε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.59, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 115}}, Theocr.8.25; _Doric dialect_ ἁμέ {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“SIG” 1 (Abu Simbel, 6th c.BC), 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 173, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 759 codices, “Lys.” 95.}}—On these dialectic varieties, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|see 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 50}} _ff._ (Cf. Sanskrit _ahám_ (ἐγών), _accusative_ _plural_ **asmā´n**; for νώ cf. Sanskrit _nau_):—frequently in answers, as an affirmative, especially in form ἔγωγε, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1248, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 149b, etc.}}; οὗτος ἐ. here am {{Refs 5th c.BC+|I, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 4.26; ὅδ᾽ ἐκεῖνος ἐ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 138 (Lyric poetry)}}; rarely with _Article_, τὸν ἐμέ **myself**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 166a, “Sph.” 239b (but ὁ ἐ.}} **the Self, the Ego**, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 444)}}; τίς ὢν οὗτος ὁ ἐγὼ τυγχάν; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1119a; τί ἐστι φίλο; ἄλλος ἐ. 4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Hermias Alexandrinus Philosophus “in Platonis Phaedrum scholia” p.166 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus}}; τί τοῦτ᾽ ἐμο; ἡμῖν τί τοῦτ᾽ ἔστ; Latin _quid mea hoc refert_ ? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 498, etc.}}; ἐγ; in a question, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1336, +others}}; ἡμεῖς **the self**, ἔνθα δὴ ἡμεῖς μάλιστα {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 1.1.7.}}

