o.152; ἄμμεσιν 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 100}}; _Doric dialect_ also ἁμίν or ἇμιν, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 77, 78, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 347 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1081}}; with ῑ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ach.” 821, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.145:— _accusative_ ἡμᾶς (also ἧμᾰς 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.372)}}; _Ionic dialect_ ἡμέας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.211, “SIG” 273.25 (Milet., 4th c.BC)}}; ἥμεας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.294 (cf. Hdn.Gr. 2.140)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἄμμε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.59, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 115}}, Theocr.8.25; _Doric dialect_ ἁμέ {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“SIG” 1 (Abu Simbel, 6th c.BC), 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 173, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 759 codices, “Lys.” 95.}}—On these dialectic varieties, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|see 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 50}} _ff._ (Cf. Sanskrit _ahám_ (ἐγών), _accusative_ _plural_ **asmā´n**; for νώ cf. Sanskrit _nau_):—frequently in answers, as an affirmative, especially in form ἔγωγε, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1248, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 149b, etc.}}; οὗτος ἐ. here am {{Refs 5th c.BC+|I, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 4.26; ὅδ᾽ ἐκεῖνος ἐ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 138 (Lyric poetry)}}; rarely with _Article_, τὸν ἐμέ **myself**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 166a, “Sph.” 239b (but ὁ ἐ.}} **the Self, the Ego**, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 444)}}; τίς ὢν οὗτος ὁ ἐγὼ τυγχάν; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1119a; τί ἐστι φίλο; ἄλλος ἐ. 4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Hermias Alexandrinus Philosophus “in Platonis Phaedrum scholia” p.166 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus}}; τί τοῦτ᾽ ἐμο; ἡμῖν τί τοῦτ᾽ ἔστ; Latin _quid mea hoc refert_ ? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 498, etc.}}; ἐγ; in a question, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1336, +others}}; ἡμεῖς **the self**, ἔνθα δὴ ἡμεῖς μάλιστα {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 1.1.7.}}

2250:
** ἡμέρα**,
_Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ **ἡμέρη** {{Refs|“IG” 12(5).1 (from Ios)}}, _Doric dialect_ **ἀμέρα** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work = 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies”]5(1).213.43, +others, 1390.109, 1432.25, “Test.Epict.” 4.12, “Michel” 995 A 32, etc.}}, _Locrian dialect_ **ἀμάρα** {{Refs|“IG” 9(1).334.42}} (aspirated perhaps only in _Attic dialect_ and West _Ionic dialect_, compare ἐπάμερος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus, etc., αὐθημερόν “IG” 7.235.18 (from Oropus)}}, etc.; usually unaspirated in early _Attic dialect_ Inscrr., {{Refs|“IG” 12.49.6, +others}}; aspirated in codices even in dialects: original ἀμέρα probably took aspirate from ἑσπέρα): ἡ:—**day**, less frequently than ἦμαρ in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, ἡ. ἥδε κακὸν φέρει 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.541, 13.828}}; τίς νύ μοι ἡ. ἥδ; {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.514; νύκτες τε καὶ ἡ. 14.93}}; μῆνές τε καὶ ἡ{{Refs 8th c.BC+|. [prev. work] 293; νοῦσοι ἐφ᾽ ἡμέρῃ αἳ δ᾽ ἐπὶ νυκτί 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 102}}; ἡ σήμερον ἡ., see at {σήμερον· ἅμα ἡμέρᾳ} or ἅμα τῇ ἡμέρᾳ **at daybreak**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.3.6, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.76; ἅμ᾽ ἡμέρῃ διαφωσκούσῃ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.86}}; ἡ. διέλαμψεν, ἐξέλαμψεν, ὑπέφαινε, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 744, “Pax” 304, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.14}}; τῆς ἡ. ὀψέ late **in the day**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “HG” 2.1.23.}}
**2** sometimes like _Epic dialect_ ἦμαρ, with _adjectives_ to describe **a state** or **time of life**, ἐπίπονος ἁ. **a life** of misery, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 654 (Lyric poetry)}}; λυπρὰν ἄγειν ἡ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 364}}; ἐχθρὰ ἡ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 540}}; παλαιὰ ἁ. old **age**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 623 (but θεία ἡ. [prev. author] “fragment” 950 is uncertain reading)}}; τερμία ἁ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ant.” 1330 (Lyric poetry)}}; αἱ μακραὶ ἁμέραι length of **days**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “OC” 1216 (Lyric poetry)}}; νέα ἁ. youth, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 720 (Lyric poetry)}}; so τῇ πρώτῃ ἡ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1389a24}}; ἐπὶ τῇ τελευταίᾳ ἡ. at the close of **life**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1389b33, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1529}}; ζοὴν βλέπουσιν ἡ. look **life-like**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 4.68.}}
**3** _poetry_ for **time**, ἡ. κλίνει τε κἀνάγει πάλιν ἅπαντα τἀνθρώπεια {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 131; ἐς τόδ᾽ ἡμέρας [prev. author] “OC” 1138: _plural_, ἐν ἡμέραις τινός}} **in the days** of.., {{LXX|LXX.1Ch.4.41, etc.}}; ἡ. ἀρχαῖαι{{LXX|LXX.Psa.142(143).5.}}
**4** **birthday**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 4.41.}}
**5** **a fixed day**, τακτὴ ἡ. {{NT|NT.Act.12.21}}; ῥητὴ ἡ. {{NT+1st c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 19; ἡ. ἔστησαν ἀρχαιρεσιῶν 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 6.48, cf. NT.Act.17.31; ἡ. Κυρίου LXX.Joel.2.1, cf. NT.2Pet.3.12, etc.; ἡ. κρίσεως NT.Matt.10.15: so _absolutely_, ὑπὸ ἀνθρωπίνης ἡμέρας}} a human **tribunal**, {{NT|NT.1Cor.4.3; ἡμέραι καὶ ἀγῶνες “Jahresh.” 23 “Beibl.” 93 (Pamphyl.).}}
**6** in _plural_, **age**, προβεβηκὼς ἐν ταῖς ἡ. {{LXX+NT|NT.Luke.1.7, cf. LXX.Gen.47.8, etc.}}
**II** _absolutely_ usages,
**II.1** _genitive_, τριῶν ἡμερέων **within** three **days**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.115, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.3}}; ἡμερῶν ὀλίγων **within** a few **days**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.26, etc.}}; ἄλλης ἡ. another **day**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 698}}; τῆς αὐτῆς ἡ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.87; μιᾶς ἀμέρας “IG” 5(1).213.43 (Sparta, 5th c.BC)}}; ἡμέρας **by day**, opposed to νυκτός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 65; οὔθ᾽ ἡμέρας οὔτε νυκτός 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 240c}}; τοὺς.. τῆς ἡ. ἄρτους δ ¯ **daily**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 47.21 (2nd c.BC)}}; δὶς τῆς ἡμέρης ἑκάστης twice every **day**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.37}}; δίς τῆς ἡ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 207}}; πεντάκις τῆς ἡ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 326}}; κατεσθίω.. τῆς ἡ. πένθ᾽ ἡμιμέδιμνα five **every day**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 1.}}
**II.2** _dative_, τῇδε θἠμέρᾳ,= σήμερον, (5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1283; τῇ τόθ᾽ ἡ [prev. author] “El.” 1134.
**II.3** _accusative_, πᾶσαν ἡ. any **day**, i.e. soon, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.111, 7.203}}; τὴν μὲν αὐτίχ᾽ ἡ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 433}}; ὅλην τὴν ἡ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 233}}; τρίτην ἡ. ἥκων two **days** after one's arrival, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.23; οὐδεμίαν ἡ. ὑπεύθυνος εἶναί φημι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.112}}; πέντε ἡμέρας during five **days**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.103}}; τὰς ἡ. in the **daytime**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.12}}; τὴν ἡ. **daily**, {{LXX|LXX.Exo.29.38.}}
**III** with Preps., μίαν ἀν᾽ ἁμέραν on one **day**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.85}}; ἀνὰ πᾶσαν ἡ. every **day**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.198}}; ἀφ᾽ ἡμέρας τῆς νῦν from this **day**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 351}}; but ἀφ᾽ ἡμέρας γίνεσθαι ἐν τῷ Μουσείῳ **from early in the day**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 8.25.11: δι᾽ ἡμέρης, _Attic dialect_ -ρας}}, the whole **day** long, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.97, 2.173, 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 64, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 260 (Lyric poetry)}}; διὰ τρίτης ἡ. every other **day**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.37}}; διὰ πολλῶν ἡ. at a distance of many **days**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.29; δι᾽ ἡμερῶν τινων 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.3.6}}; εἰσ ἡμέραν **yearly**, {{LXX|LXX.Judg.17.10}}; ἐν ἡμέρῃ **in a single day**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.126, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 377; ἐνἡ. μιᾷ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 615}}; τῇδ᾽ ἐν ἡ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “OC” 1612}}; ἐν ἐκείνῃ τῇ ἡ. {{NT|NT.John.14.20}}; ἐν ἑστέραισιν ἡ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1666}}; ἐν ὀκτὼ ἡ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 20.10}}; but ἐν τρισὶν ἡ. **within** three **days,** {{NT|NT.John.2.19}}; ἐξ ἡμέρας by **day**, οὔτε νυκτὸς οὔτ᾽ ἐξ ἡ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 780}}; ἡμέραν ἐξ ἡμέρας **day** after **day**, {{NT+4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Heniochus Comicus 5.13, LXX.Gen.39.10, NT.2Pet.2.8 (but ἐξ ἡμερῶν εἰς ἡμέρας LXX.2Ch.21.15)}}; ἐπ᾽ ἡμέρην ἔχειν, ἐφ᾽ -ραν χρῆσθαι, **sufficient for the day**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.69; τὸ γὰρ βρότειον σπέρμ᾽ ἐφ᾽ ἡ. φρονεῖ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 399; τῆς ἐφ᾽ ἡ. βορᾶς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 429}}; but τοὐφ᾽ ἡμέραν **day by day**, [{{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyc.” 336: with _dative_, ἐπ᾽ ἡμέρῃ ἑκάστῃ (variant{-ρης -της})}}] every **day**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.117; ὁ ἥλιος νέος ἐφ᾽ ἡμέρῃ 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 6}}; καθ᾽ ἡμέραν **by day**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 818 (Lyric poetry)}}; καθ᾽ ἡ. τὴν νῦν **to-day**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 3, “Aj.” 801}}; but καθ᾽ ἡ. commonly means **day by day,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 12.84.40, etc.}}; καθ᾽ ἡ. ἀεί [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus] “fragment” 1120.4: with _Article_, τὸν καθ᾽ ἡ. βίον [prev. author] “OC” 1364; ἡ καθ᾽ ἡ. ἀναγκαία τροφή 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.2; τὰ καθ᾽ ἡ. ἐπιτηδεύματα [prev. author] 2.37; τὸ καθ᾽ ἡ. ἀδεές [prev. author] 3.37, etc.}}; τὸ καθ᾽ ἡ. **every day**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1126 (Lyric poetry), etc.; also τὰ καθ᾽ ἑκάστην τὴν ἡ. ἐπιτηδεύματα 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.78}}; μετ᾽ ἡμέρην **in broad daylight**, opposed to νυκτός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.150, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 930}}; opposed to νύκτωρ, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.77}}; μεθ᾽ ἡμέρας **some days** after, {{LXX|LXX.Judg.15.1}}; ἡμέρα παρ᾽ ἡμέραν γιγνομένη **day** following on **day**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.72}}; but παρ᾽ ἡμέραν **every other day**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 3.137, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DDeor.” 24.2; παρ᾽ ἡ. ἄρχειν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Fabius Maximus” 15; καθ᾽ ἡμέραν εἰώθειν ὀργίζεσθαι, νῦν παρ᾽ ἡμέραν, εἶτα παρὰ δύο, εἶτα παρὰ τρεῖς 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.18.13}}; πρὸ ἡμέρας before **day-break**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 22}}; but πρὸ ἀμερᾶν δέκα ἤ κα μέλλωντι ἀναγινώσκεν {{Refs|“GDI” 5040.42 (from Crete)}}; πρὸ ἡμερῶν ἑπτὰ εἰδυῶν Ὀκτωμβρίων {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 646.2 (Thisbe, 2nd c.BC)}}; γίγνεται, ἔστι πρὸς ἡμέραν, towards **day**, near **day**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.6, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.14}}; also, **for the day, daily**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Charito Scriptor Eroticus 4.2.}}
**IV** as _proper name_, **the goddess of day**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 124.}}
**IV.2** see at {ἥμερος} {{Refs|11.}}

2251:
** ἡμέτερος**,
_Doric dialect_ **ἁμέτ-**, _Aeolic dialect_ **ἀμμέτ-**, α, ον, (ἡμεῖς) **our**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.374, etc.}}; εἰς ἡμέτερον (i.e. δῶμα) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.55, 17.534; so ἡμέτερόνδε 8.39, 15.513}}; ἐφ᾽ ἡμέτερ{{Refs 8th c.BC+|' [prev. work] 88, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.619; ἐν ἡμετέρου 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.35, 7.8}}.; ἡ ἡ. (i.e. χώρα) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.21, etc.}}; τὸ ἡ. **our case**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 27d; τὸ ἡ. γέλωτ᾽ ἂν πάμπολυν ὄφλοι [prev. author] “Lg.” 778e, etc.}}; τὰ ἡ. φρονεῖν to take **our part**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.3.14, etc.}}; ἄνδρες ἡ. they are **in our power**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 556d, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.3.2}}; ἡ. κέρδη τῶν σοφῶν,= ἡμῶν τῶν σοφῶν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1202}}; ἡμέτερον αὐτῶν [οἰκοδόμημα],= ἡμῶν αὐτῶν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 514b}}; representing an objective _genitive_, τὸ ἡ. δέος fear **of us**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.77; εἰς τὴν ἡ. διδασκαλίαν NT.Rom.15.4.}}
**II** sometimes for ἐμός, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.562, +others, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.31, etc.}}; τὰ ἡ. **my** property, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PRyl.” 114.18 (3rd c.AD)}}; so in Imperial titles, as ἡ ἡ. ἡμερότης {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Justinianus Imperator “Novellae” 115 “Pr.”}}

2252:
** εἰμί**
(**sum**), _Aeolic dialect_ **ἔμμι** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 2.15, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 20.32}}; Cretan dialect **ἠμί** {{Refs|“GDI” 4959a}}; _2nd pers._ _singular_ εἶ, _Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ εἰς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.388, +others, _Aeolic dialect_ ἔσσι}}, _Epic dialect_ and _Doric dialect_ ἐσσί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.176, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.90, 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 134; ἐσί “GDI” 4959a}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἐστί, _Doric dialect_ ἐντί {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 12(1).677 (from Rhodes), 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.17, etc.}}; _3rd.pers._ _dual_ ἐστόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.112}}; _1st pers._ _plural_ ἐσμέν, _Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ εἰμέν (also in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.60), ἐμέν 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 294}}, _Doric dialect_ εἰμές {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.73, but ἠμέν “GDI” 5178.34}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ εἰσί (-ίν), _Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ ἔασι (-ιν) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.73, 6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 8.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Antimachus Colophonius Elegiacus et Epicus 29, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 4.84}}, _Doric dialect_ ἐντί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.24, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 11.45, “IG” 9(1).32.22 (from Phocis), etc.: _imperative_ ἴσθι (ἔσθι 4th-5th c.BC: Hecataeus Milesius Historicus 361 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus)}}, _Epic dialect_ and Lyric poetry also in _middle_ form ἔσσο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.200, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 1.28, Macedonius Lyricus “Paean” 31, late Prose ἔσο 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.241d, 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 3.5, 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 5.12, 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “ad Marcellam - Porphyrii Opuscula” 34}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἔστω (ἤτω {{LXX+5th c.BC+|LXX.Psa.103.31, and late Inscrr., “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2664, +others; but in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 361c to be read ἴτω), _Doric dialect_ εἴτω, ἤτω, 4th c.AD: Heraclides Historicus cited in 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1411.21, Elean ἤστω “Schwyzer” 424}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἔστωσαν, but ἔστων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 502a, ὄντων [prev. author] “Lg.” 879b}}, and early _Attic dialect_ Inscrr., {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“IG” 12.22, etc. (ἔστωσαν first in 2nd c.BC, [prev. work]22.1328), _Doric dialect_ ἐόντων [prev. work]1126:}}_subjunctive_ ὦ, ᾖς, ᾖ, _Epic dialect_ ἔω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.18}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἔῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.300, +others (also ἔῃσι 2.366, +others, ᾖσι (ν) 19.202, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 294)}}, also _Boeotian dialect_ ἔνθω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“IG” 7.3172.165, μετείω 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.47 and perhaps εἴῃ 9.245, etc.}}; _Doric dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ὦντι {{Refs|“SIG” 940.3 (from Crete), ἔωντι “GDI” 5040.14 (from Hierapytna)}}, _Boeotian dialect_ ἴωνθι {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“IG” 7.3171.46 (3rd c.BC): _optative_ εἴην, -ης (εἴησθα 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 715)}}, -η, also ἔοις, ἔοι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.284, 142, +others, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.6}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ εἴοισαν Ἀρχ. Ἐφ. {{Refs|1911.133 (from Gonni)}}; _3rd.pers._ _dual_ εἴτην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 149e, “Sph.” 243e}}; _1st pers._ _plural_ εἶμεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 921 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus}}; _2nd pers._ _plural_ εἶτε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.195}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ εἶεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.372, etc., εἴησαν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.2, etc.}}; Elean ἔα, ={εἴη}, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|“SIG” 9 (6th c.BC), and σύν-εαν, ={συνεῖεν}, “GDI” 1149 (6th c.BC):}}]_infinitive_ εἶναι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus ἦναι “SIG” 306.9 (Tegea, 4th c.BC)}}; _Epic dialect_ ἔμμεναι (also _Aeolic dialect_ ἔμμεν᾽ {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 34), ἔμμεν (also 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 6.42, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 623 (Lyric poetry)), ἔμεναι, ἔμεν, also ἔμειν “SIG” 1166 (from Dodona)}}; _Doric dialect_ εἶμεν Foed. cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.77, 79, “IG” 7.1.7 (from Megara), ἦμεν “Test.Epict.” 5.16, “Tab.Heracl.” 1.75, Cretan dialect ἦμεν or ἤμην “Leg.Gort.” 1.15, +others, “GDI” 4998i 2, +others, Megarian dialect εἴμεναι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 775, εἴμειν “IG” 12(1).155.100 (from Rhodes), 14.952 (from Agrigentum); εἶν [prev. work] 12(9).211.10 (from Eretria), “SIG” 135.4 (from Olynthus), etc.:}}_participle_ ὤν, _Epic dialect_ ἐών, ἐοῦσα, ἐόν, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Cypria ἰών “Inscription Cyprian dialect” 135.23 H.}}; _Boeotian dialect_ _feminine_ ἰῶσα {{Refs|“IG” 7.3172.116 (Orchom.)}}, _Aeolic dialect_ and _Doric dialect_ _feminine_ ἔσσα {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 75.4, “IG” 4.952.2 (Epid.), 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 28.16, ἐοῖσα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.265, ἔασσα “Lyrica Alexandrina Adespota” 9, Diotogenes Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.7.62, εὖσα Erinna Lyrica 5.5}} (also _Ionic dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 5.16, εὔντων 2.85), ἐᾶσα 5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 96d, “IG” 5(1).1470.8 (from Messene), ἴαττα “Leg.Gort.” 8.47; _accusative_ _sing._ εὖντα 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.3}}; _nominative_ _singular_ εἴς in {{Refs 7th c.BC+|4th c.AD: Heraclides Historicus cited in 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1756.13, _plural_ ἔντες “Tab.Heracl.” 1.117; _dative_ _plural_ ἔντασσι [prev. work]104; _genitive_ _plural_ παρ-έντων 7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 64}}: _imperfect_ ἦν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.77, etc., _Epic dialect_ ἔον}} (also _Aeolic dialect_, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 127, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in 1787 “fragment” 3 ii 21), in _Attic dialect_ ἦ}} (uncertain in _Aeolic dialect_, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 14.9), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 77, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 61b, etc., but usually altered to ἦν in codices (and ἦν}} is required by metre in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 280), _contraction_ from _Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ ἦα (8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.808, +others, “IG” 12(8).449.2 (from Thasos)}}, whence Hom.and later _Ionic dialect_ ἔᾱ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.321, +others, ἔας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.187, ἔατε [prev. author] 4.119)}}; _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἦεν, always with ν in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}; ἔην as _1st pers._ _singular_, only {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.762 (assuming variant, +others ἔον), frequently as _3rd.pers._ _sing._ (generally before a consonant, so that ἔεν is possible), sometimes also ἤην}}; _2nd pers._ _singular_ ἦσθα, later ἦς (which is variant in {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 1.26), sometimes in LXX (LXX.Judg.11.35, LXX.Ruth.3.2, +others), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 365e, Erinna Lyrica 4.4, NT.Matt.25.21, +others, ἦσθας 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 156, _Epic dialect_ ἔησθα}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἦν, _Epic dialect_ ἔην, ἤην, ἦεν (see. above), _Doric dialect_ and _Aeolic dialect_ ἦς {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 30.1, 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 102, 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 59, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.90, “SIG” 241.145 (Delph.)}}; _3rd.pers._ _dual_ ἤστην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.10, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 387, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 982, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 272a, +others}}; _Doric dialect_ _1st pers._ _plural_ ἦμες {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lycurgus” 21}}; _2nd pers._ _plural_ ἦτε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 276c, ἦστε 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 821, “Ec.” 1086}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἦσαν, _Ionic dialect_ and poetical ἔσαν (in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 321, 825, ἦν is not _plural_ for ἦσαν}}, but is rather a peculiarity of syntax, see below see, but is _3rd.pers._ _plural_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 46, +others, “SIG” 560.15 (Epidamnus, 3rd c.BC))}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἔον {{NT+5th c.BC+|“Schwyzer” 644.12; later ἤμην “PSI” 4.362.21 (3rd c.BC), “SIG” 527.46 (Crete, 3rd c.BC), “IGRom.” 4.1740 (from Cyme), always in LXX as LXX.Bar.1.19, cf. NT.Matt.23.30, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.174a, etc., and sometimes in codices of earlier writers, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.34, “Tragica Adespota” 124 (cited from 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 931 by 4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus and from [prev. author] “Tr.” 474 by Aps.), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.9, 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “adv. Athenogenem” 26}}, _2nd pers._ _singular_ ἦσο {{Refs|“epigram Gr.” 379 (from Aezani)}}, _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἦστο {{Refs|“Supp.epigram” 1.455.7 (from Phrygia)}}, _1st pers._ _plural_ ἤμεθα {{NT+3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 2p.11 (3rd c.BC), LXX.Bar.1.19, LXX.1Ki.25.16, NT.Eph.2.3}}; _subjunctive_ ὦμαι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PBaden” 48.12 (2nd c.BC), ἦται “GDI” , ἦνται probably in “IG” 5(1).1390.83 (from Andania)}}; _Ionic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ also ἔσκον, used by {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 656 (Lyric poetry):}}_future_ ἔσομαι, ἔσται, _Epic dialect_ and _Aeolic dialect_ also ἔσσομαι, ἔσεται, ἔσσετα; _Aeolic dialect_ _2nd pers._ _singular_ ἔσσῃ probably in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 67, 87; _Doric dialect_ 2 and _3rd.pers._ _sing._ ἐσσῇ, ἐσσεῖται, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.393, 13.317, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 10.5, _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἐσσοῦνται Foed. cited in 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.77 codices (but ἔσσονται “Tab.Heracl.” 1.113)}}, _infinitive_ ἐσσεῖσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 57.}} —All forms of the _present_ _indicative_ are enclitic (except _2nd pers._ _singular_ εἶ and _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἔασι); but _3rd.pers._ _singular_ is written ἔστι when it begins a sentence or verse, or when it immediately follows οὐκ, καί, εἰ, ὡς, ἀλλά, or τοῦτ᾽, Hdn.Gr.{{Refs|1.553 (also μή _accusative_ to “Etymologicum Magnum” 301.3)}}; later Grammars wrote ἔστι as _substantive_ Verb, {{Refs 9th c.AD+|9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc., 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 880.22.}}
**A** as the _substantive_ Verb,
**A.I** of persons, **exist**, οὐκ ἔσθ᾽ οὗτος ἀνήρ, οὐδ᾽ ἔσσεται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.437}}; ἔτ᾽ εἰσί they **are** still **in being**,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|15.433, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 445, etc.; τεθνηῶτος.. μηδ᾽ ἔτ᾽ ἐόντος 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.289}}; οὐκέτ᾽ ἐστί he **is** no more, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1162}}; οὐδὲ δὴν ἦν he **was** not long-**lived**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.131}}; ὁ οὐκ ὤν, οἱ οὐκ ὄντες, of **those who are** no more, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.45, 44}}; οἱ ὄντες the **living**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.29.2}}; ὁ ὤν the {{LXX+8th c.BC+|“Eternal”, LXX.Exo.3.14, +others, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.289; θεοὶ αἰὲν ἐόντες 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.290}}; ἐσσόμενοι **posterity**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|2.119; κἀγὼ γὰρ ἦ ποτ᾽, ἀλλὰ νῦν οὐκ εἴμ᾽ ἔτι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 284}}; ὡς ἂν εἶεν ἅνθρωποι **might continue in being**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 190c; ζώντων καὶ ὄντων Ἀθηναίων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.72, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione et Corruptione” 318b25}}; of things, εἰ ἔστι ἀληθέως [ἡ τράπεζα] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.17, etc.}}; of cities, ὄλωλεν, οὐδ᾽ ἔτ᾽ ἐστὶ Τροία {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1292, compare “Heracl.” 491}}; δοκεῖ μοι Καρχηδόνα μὴ εἶναι **censeo Carthaginem esse delendam**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Ma.” 27}}; ἂν ᾖ τὸ στράτευμα **be in existence**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.17}}; of money, **to be in hand**, τῶν ὄντων χρημάτων καὶ τῶν προσιόντων {{Refs|“IG” 12.91.25}}; τὰ ὄντα **property**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 511a, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 24, etc.}}; τὸ ἐσόμενον ἐκ.. **future revenue** from.., {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BCH” 46.420 (Olymos, 1st c.BC)}}; of place, τὴν οὖσαν ἐκκλησίαν the **local** church, {{NT|NT.Act.13.1}}; of time, τοῦ ὄντος μηνός in the **current** month, {{Refs|“BGU” 146.4, etc.}}; **in office**, ἱερέων τῶν ὄντων {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PPar.” 5.4 (2nd c.BC)}}; αἱ οὖσαι [ἐξουσίαι] the powers **that be, Ep. Rom.**{{Refs|13.1.}}
**A.II** of the **real** world, **be**, opposed to become, γίγνεται πάντα ἃ δή φαμεν εἶναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 152d, etc.}}; τὸ ὄν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Being”, 4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 8.35, 5th c.BC: Protagoras Philosophus 2, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 27d, etc.}}; opposed to τὸ μὴ ὄν, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Gorgias Rhetor et Sophista “fragment” 3 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, etc.; οὐδὲν γίνεται ἐκ τοῦ μὴ ὄντος 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.5U.; ἐξ οὐκ ὄντων ἐποίησεν αὐτὰ ὁ θεός LXX.2Mac.7.28}}; τὰ ὄντα the **world of things**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 7, 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 129.5, etc.}}; ὄν_indeclinable_, τῶν ὂν εἰδῶν species **of Being**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.2.10.}}
**A.II.2** of circumstances, events, etc., **to happen**, τά τ᾽ ἐόντα, τά τ᾽ ἐσσόμενα, πρό τ᾽ ἐόντα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.70; ἡ ἐσβολὴ ἔμελλεν ἔσεσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.13, etc.}}; τῆς προδοσίας οὔσης since treachery **was there**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.103}}; ἕως ἂν ὁ πόλεμος ᾖ so long as it **last**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.58; αἱ σπονδαὶ ἐνιαυτὸν ἔσονται [prev. author] 4.118}}; τί ἐστι; what **is** it? what's the matter? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 193}}; τί οὖν ἦν τοῦτ; how **came** it **to pass**? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 58a: repeated with a _relative_ to avoid a positive assertion, ἔστι δ᾽ ὅπῃ νῦν ἔ.}} things **are** as they **are**, i.e. are ill, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 67.}}
**A.III** **be the fact** or **the case**, διπλασίαν ἂν τὴν δύναμιν εἰκάζεσθαι ἤ ἐστιν twice as large as it **really is**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.10}}; αὐτὸ ὅ ἐστι καλόν beauty **in its essence**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 211c, compare “Phd.” 74b}}; frequently in _participle_, τὸν ἐόντα λόγον λέγειν or φαίνειν the **true** story, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.95, 116}}; τῷ ἐόντι χρήσασθαι tell the **truth**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 30; τὰ ὄντα ἀπαγγέλλειν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.8}}; σκῆψιν οὐκ οὖσαν, λόγον οὐκ ὄντα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 584, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1052}}; τῷ ὄντι **in reality, in fact**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 328d, etc.}}; to apply a quotation to a case in point, τῷ ὄντι κλαυσίγελως **real** 'smiles through tears' (with allusion to {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.484), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.2.9, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 196d}}; κατὰ τὸ ἐόν according to the **fact, right**ly, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.97}}; πᾶν τὸ ἐόν the whole **truth**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 9.11; τοῦ ἐόντος ἀποτεύξεται 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 2.}}
**A.IV** followed by the _relative_, οὐκ ἔστιν ὅς or ὅστις **no one**, οὐκ ἔσθ᾽ ὃς.. ἀπαλάλκοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.348; οὐκ ἔ. οὐδεὶς ὅς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 903}}; οὐκ ἔ. ὅτῳ, ={οὐδενί}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 293 (_anapaest meter_), compare 989: frequently in _plural_, εἰσὶν οἵ}}, = Latin _sunt qui_, used exactly like{ἔνιοι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.88, 7.44, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 77d, “Grg.” 503a, etc. (εἰσί τινες οἵ.. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.24)}}; ἐστὶν ἃ χωρία, πολίσματα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.12, 65; ἐστὶν ἃ εἰπεῖν [prev. author] 2.67; ἦσαν οἵ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.2.14}}; the _singular_ Verb is used even with _masculine_ and _feminine_ _plural_, ἐστὶν οἵ, αἵ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 1, “VC” 4, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.3.16}}; more frequently in oblique cases, ποταμῶν ἐστὶ ὧν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.187; ἐστὶν ἀφ᾽ ὧν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.65}}; ἐστὶ παρ᾽ οἷς, ἐστὶν ἐν οἷς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.23, 5.25: in questions ὅστις is used, ἔστιν ἥντινα δόξαν.. ἀπεκρίνατ; 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 85b: with _relative_ Particles, ἐστὶν ἔνθα}}, = Latin _est ubi_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.4.15, etc.}}; ἐ. ὅπῃ, ἔσθ᾽ ὅπου, **some**how, **some**where, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 331d, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 517, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 448, etc.}}; in questions expecting a _negative_ answer, ἐ. ὁπόθεν, ὅπω; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 35a, “R.” 493e, etc.; οὐ γάρ ἐσθ᾽ ὅπως 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 61, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.102, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 620}}; οὐκ ἔ. ὅπως οὐ **in any case, necessarily**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 188; οὐκ ἔ. ὡς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 76e, etc.}}; ἐ. ὅτε, ἔσθ᾽ ὅτε, **some**times, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 180.2, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 56, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.21, etc.}}
**A.V** ἦν is sometimes used with _plural_ _masculine_ and _feminine_, usually at the beginning of a sentence, **there was**, τῆς δ᾽ ἦν τρεῖς κεφαλαί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 321; (but in ἦν δ᾽ ἐρῳδιοί τε πολλοί 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 46, compare 59, +others, it may be taken as _Doric dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_)}}; ἦν δ᾽ ἀμφίπλεκτοι κλίμακες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 520 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἦν ἄρα κἀκεῖνοι ταλακάρδιοι epigram cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.184; less frequently ἔστι, ἔστι δὲ μεταξὺ.. ἑπτὰ στάδιοι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.26, compare 7.34; ἔστι.. ἄρχοντές τε καὶ δῆμος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 463a}}; before dual Nouns, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 58, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 500d.}}
**A.VI** ἔστι _impersonal_, with _infinitive_, **it is possible**, ἔστι γὰρ ἀμφοτέροισιν ὀνείδεα μυθήσασθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.246; ἔστι μὲν εὕδειν, ἔστι δὲ τερπομένοισιν ἀκούειν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.392}}; εἴ τί πού ἐστι (i.e. πιθέσθαι){{Refs 5th c.BC+|4.193; τοιάδε.. ἐστὶν ἀκοῦσαι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1055 (_anapaest meter_); ἔστι τεκμήρια ὁρᾶν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.2.13, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1163, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.105, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.272, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 53.6, etc.}}; so in _imperative_, _optative_, and _subjunctive_, ἔστω ἀποφέρεσθαι τῷ βουλομένῳ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“IG” 12.10.7; μυρία ἂν εἴη λέγειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 271e; ὅπως ἂν ᾖ δρᾶν “IG” 2.1054.91: more frequently in _negative_ clauses, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.267, etc.}}; followed by ὥστε with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 656}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, ἁδόντα δ᾽ εἴη με τοῖς ἀγαθοῖς ὁμιλεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.96; ἔστιν ἐκπεσεῖν ἀρχῆς Δία 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 757}}: sometimes not _impersonal_ in this sense, θάλασσα δ᾽ οὐκέτ᾽ ἦν ἰδεῖν {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pers.” 419.}}
**A.VI.b** ἔστω in argument, **let it be granted**, ἔστω τοῦτο ἀληθὲς εἶναι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 25; ἔστω σοι τοῦθ᾽ οὕτως 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.987b; ἔστω εἶναί τινα τοιοῦτον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator Chr.74.24.}}
**B** most frequently, **to be**, the Copula connecting the predicate with the Subject, both being in the same case: hence, **signify, import**, τὸ γὰρ εἴρειν λέγειν ἐστίν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 398d}}; especially in the phrase τοῦτ᾽ ἔστι, **hoc est**; Σκαιόλαν, ὅπερ ἐστὶ Λαϊόν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Publicola” 17: with numerals, τὰ δὶς πέντε δέκα ἐστίν}} twice five **are** ten, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.4.7}}; εἶναί τις or τι, **to be** somebody, something, **be** of some consequence, see at {τι; οὐδὲν εἶναι} {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 562d, etc.}}
**B.2** _periphrastic_ with the Participle to represent the finite Verb: with _perfect_ _participle_ once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, τετληότες εἰμέν, for τετλήκαμεν, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.873}}; so in Trag. and _Attic dialect_, ἦν τεθνηκώς, for ἐτεθνήκει, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 869}}; ἔσται δεδορκώς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1179; εἰμὶ γεγώς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1299; πεφυκός ἐστι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1473; δεδρακότες εἰσίν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.68; κατακεκονότες ἔσεσθε 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.6.36}}: with _aorist_ _participle_, once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, βλήμενος ἦν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.211}}; so προδείσας εἰμί, οὐ σιωπήσας ἔσε; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 90, 1146, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 460}}: with _present_ _participle_, ἦν προκείμενον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pers.” 371; φεύγων Ὀρέστης ἐστίν [prev. author] “Ch.” 136; εἴην οὐκ ἂν εὖ φρονῶν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1330}}; τί δ᾽ ἐστί.. φέρο; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “OT” 991, compare 274, 708; λέγων ἐστίν τις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1179; ἦν τίς σ᾽ ὑβρίζων [prev. author] “HF” 313; πόρρω ἤδη εἶ πορευόμενος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 204b; βαδίζων εἰμί 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 36; frequently in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, ἦσαν ἱέντες 1.57, +others; even εἰσὶ διάφοροι ἐόντες 3.49 (assuming variant):}} — if the _Article_ is joined with the Part., the noun is made emphatic, Κᾶρές εἰσι οἱ καταδέξαντες the persons who showed her **were** Carians, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.171; αὐτὸς ἦν ὁ μαρτυρῶν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 798; δόλος ἦν ὁ φράσας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 197 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**C** εἶναι is frequently modified in sense by the addition of _adverbs_, or the cases of Nouns without or with Preps.:
**C.I** εἶναι with _adverbs_, where the _adverb_ often merely represents a Noun and stands as the predicate, ἅλις δέ οἱ ἦσαν ἄρουραι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.122, etc.}}; ἀκέων, ἀκήν εἶναι, **to be** silent,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|4.22, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.82; σῖγα πᾶς ἔστω λεώς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 532; διαγνῶναι χαλεπῶς ἦν ἄνδρα ἕκαστον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.424}}; ἀσφαλέως ἡ κομιδὴ ἔσται **will go on** safely, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.134}}; ἐγγύς, πόρρω εἶναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.88, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 356e: frequently _impersonal_ with words implying good or ill fortune, Κουρήτεσσι κακῶς ἦν}} **it fared** ill with them, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.551; εὖ γὰρ ἔσται 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 89, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1188, etc.; ἡδέως ἂν αὐτοῖς εἴη 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.30.}}
**C.II** with _genitive_, to express descent or extraction, πατρὸς δ᾽ εἴμ᾽ ἀγαθοῖο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.109; αἵματός εἰς ἀγαθοῖο 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.611, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.71, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.71, etc.; πόλεως μεγίστης εἶ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.3.19.}}
**C.II.b** to express the material of which a thing is made, ἡ κρηπίς ἐστι λίθων μεγάλων **consists of**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.93; τῆς πόλιος ἐούσης δύο φαρσέων [prev. work] 186}}; τοιούτων ἔργων ἐστὶ ἡ τυραννίς **is made up of**.., {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.92}}.ή, etc.
**C.II.c** to express the class to which a person or thing belongs, εἶ γὰρ τῶν φίλων **you are one of** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 345; ἐτύγχανε βουλῆς ὤν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.70; ὅσοι ἦσαν τῶν προτέρων στρατιωτῶν [prev. author] 7.44; Κριτίας τῶν τριάκοντα ὤν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.31}}; ἔστι τῶν αἰσχρῶν **it is in the class of** disgraceful things, i. e. **it is** disgraceful, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.2.}}
**C.II.d** to express that a thing belongs to another, Τροίαν Ἀχαιῶν οὖσαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 269; τὸ πεδίον ἦν μέν κοτε Χορασμίων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.117, etc.}}: hence, **to be of the party of**, ἦσαν.. τινὲς μὲν φιλίππου, τινὲς δὲ τοῦ βελτίστου {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.56, compare 37.53}}; **to be de pendent upon**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 737, etc.}}; **to be at the mercy of**, ἔστι τοῦ λέγοντος, ἢν φόβους λέγῃ {{Refs|[prev. author] “OT” 917.}}
**C.II.e** to express one's duty, business, custom, nature, and the like, οὔτοι γυναικός ἐστι **'tis** not a woman's **part**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 940; τὸ ἐπιτιμᾶν παντὸς εἶναι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.16}}; τὸ δὲ ναυτικὸν τέχνης ἐστίν **is matter of** art, **requires** art, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.142, compare 83.}}
**C.II.f** in LXX, **to be occupied about**, ἦσαν τοῦ θύειν {{LXX|LXX.2Ch.30.17}}; ἔσεσθαι, with _genitive_, **to be about to**, ἐσόμεθα τοῦ σῶσαί σε {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.10.11.}}
**C.III** with the _dative_, ἐστί μοι I **have**, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc.}}
**C.III.2** with two datives., σφίσι τε καὶ Ἀθηναίοισι εἶναι οὐδὲν πρῆγμα that they and the Athenians **have** nothing **to do** one with another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.84; μηδὲν εἶναι σοὶ καὶ φιλίππῳ πρᾶγμα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.283}}; more shortly, σοί τε καὶ τούτοισι πρήγμασι τί ἐστ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.33}}; τί τῷ νόμῳ καὶ τῇ βασάν; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 29.36}}; τί ἐμοὶ καὶ σο; Latin _quid tecum est mihi?_ {{NT|NT.Mark.5.7, etc.}}; also ἐμοὶ οὐδὲν πρὸς τοὺς τοιούτους (i.e. ἐστίν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.12; ἐν οἷς πρὸς τοὺς ἐναντίους ἐστὶ τῷ δήμῳ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.278}}; ἔσται αὐτῳ πρὸς τὸν θεόν, in tomb inscriptions, {{Refs|“JHS” 18.113, etc.}}
**C.III.3** with ἄσμενος, βουλόμενος, etc., added, ἐμοὶ δέ κεν ἀσμένῳ εἴη **'twould be** to my delight, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.108; οὐκ ἂν σφίσι βουλομένοις εἶναι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.35; προσδεχομένῳ [prev. author] 6.46; θέλοντι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1356 (Lyric poetry); ἡδομένοις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 187c.}}
**C.IV** with Preps., εἶναι ἀπό τινος, ={εἶναί τινος}{{Refs 5th c.BC+|(above 11.a), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.6.9; εἰσὶν ἀπ᾽ ἐναντίων αὗται πραγμάτων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 12d}}; but εἶναι ἀπ᾽ οἴκου **to be** away from.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.99.}}
**C.IV.2** εἶναι ἔκ τινος **to be sprung** from, εἴμ᾽ ἐκ Παιονίης, Μυρμιδόνων ἔξ εἰμι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.154, 24.397, etc.}}; ἔστιν ἐξ ἀνάγκης **it is** of necessity, i. e. necessary, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 256d.}}
**C.IV.3** εἶναι ἐν.. **to be** in a certain state, ἐν εὐπαθείῃσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.22}}; ἐν ἀθυμία, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.46, etc.; ἐν ταραχαῖς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.218}}; εἶναι ἐν ἀξιώματι **to be** in esteem, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.130}}; οἱ ἐν τέλεϊ ἐόντες those in office, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.18, etc.}}; but εἶναι ἐν τέχνῃ, ἐν φιλοσοφία **to be engaged** in.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 562, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 59a.}}
**C.IV.3.b** ἐν σοί ἐστι **it depends** on thee, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.109, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 963; ἐν σοὶ γάρ ἐσμεν [prev. author] “OT” 314; so also ἐπί τινι [prev. author] “Ph.” 1003, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.2, etc.}}
**C.IV.4** εἶναι διά.., much like{εἶναι ἐν}.., εἶναι διὰ φόβου, ={φοβεῖσθαι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.34}}; εἶναι δι᾽ ὄχλου, ={ὀχληρὸν εἶναι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.73; εἶναι διὰ μόχθων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.25}}; εἶναι δι᾽ αἰτίας, ={αἰτιᾶσθαι}, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.70}}; Geometry texts, **pass** through, διὰ τᾶς ἑτέρας διαμέτρου ἐόντος τοῦ ἐπιπέδου {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ κωνοειδέων καὶ σφαιροειδέων” 20.}}
**C.IV.5** εἶναι ἐφ᾽ ἑαυτῆς **to be** by oneself, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.23}}; εἶναι ἐπὶ ὀνόματος **to bear** a name, {{Refs|[prev. author] 39.21}}; εἶναι ἐπὶ τοῖς πράγμασιν **to be engaged** in.., {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.12}}; εἶναι ἐπί τινα **to be** against him, {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.33}}; εἶναι ἐφ᾽ ἑξήκοντα στάδια **to reach** sixty stadia, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.6.11}}; εἶναι ἐπὶ τὰς ἁφάς **pass** through the points of contact, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Biographus Perg. “Con.” 4.1}}; εἶναι ἐπί τινι, see above 3 b.
**C.IV.6** εἶναι πρός τινος **to be** in one's favour, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.10, 29, etc.}}; **to suit**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.11, etc.}}; εἶναι πρός τινι **engaged** in, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 84c, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 5.31}}; πρὸς τοῖς ἰδίοις **mind** one's own affairs, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1309a6, “Ath.” 16.3; εἶναι πρὸς τὸ κωλύειν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.26.3}}; πρὸς τὸ πονεῖν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Teles p.46 H.; εἶναι περί τι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.5.7, etc.}}
**C.IV.7** εἶναι παρά τινι or τινα, = παρειναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 6.2.15, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.140.ά}} (assuming variant).
**C.IV.8** εἶναι ὑπό τινα or τινι **to be** subject to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.2.17 (assuming variant), 6.2.4.}}
**C.IV.9** περὶ τούτων ἐστίν that **is** the question, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 30.}}
**C.IV.10** εἶναι ἀπό.., in Geometry texts, **to be constructed** upon, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ σφαίρας καὶ κυλίνδρου” 2.9, “Con.Sph.” 7.}}
**D** ἐστί is very frequently omitted, mostly in the _present_ _indicative_ before certain predicates, as ἀνάγκη, ἄξιον, δυνατόν, εἰκός, ἕτοιμον, οἷόν τε, ῥᾴδιον, χρεών, etc., and after the _neuter_ of Verbals in -τέος, and such forms as θαυμαστὸν ὅσον: less frequently with other persons and moods, εἰμί omitted, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 92, “Aj.” 813}}; εἶ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.206}}; ἐσμέν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 634}}; ἐστέ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.463}}; εἰσί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 499 (Lyric poetry), “IG” 2.778 B}}; _subjunctive_ ᾖ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.376, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 659, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.32}}; _optative_ εἴη, {{Refs|“IG” 22.1183.12}}; _imperfect_ ἦν, {{Refs|[prev. work]2.778 B}}; _future_ ἔσονται, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.394.}}
**E** the Inf. frequently seems redundant,
**E.1** in phrases implying power or will to do a thing, ἑκὼν εἶναι (see. ἑκών) κατὰ δύναμιν εἶναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 2.32; εἰς δύναμιν εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 300c}}; τὸ ἐπ᾽ ἐκείνοις εἶναι, **quantum in illis esset**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.48, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.5.9, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.58; τὸ ἐπὶ σφᾶς εἶναι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.28; τὸ κατὰ τοῦτον εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.6.9; κατὰ τοῦτο εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 317a}}; τὸ τήμερον, τὸ νῦν εἶναι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Cra.” 396e, “La.” 201c, Theopomp. Comedy texts 98, Decrees cited in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 31.2, etc.}}
**E.2** after Verbs of naming or choosing, σοφιστὴν ὀνομάζουσι τὸν ἄνδρα εἶναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 311e; σύμμαχόν μιν εἵλοντο εἶναι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.134}}; of giving, δῶκε ξεινήϊον εἶναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.20.}}
**F** _imperfect_ ἦνissts. used where other languages take the _present_,
**F.1** after ἄρα, to express a fact which **is and has always been** the same, δέρμα δὲ ἀνθρώπου.. ἦν ἄρα σχεδὸν δερμάτων πάντων λαμπρότατον human skin then **it appears is**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.64; Κύπρις οὐκ ἄρ᾽ ἦν θεός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 359}}; ὡς ἄρ᾽ ἦσθ᾽ ἐμὸς πατὴρ ὀρθῶς{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. work] 1169; ἦ πολύμοχθον ἄρ᾽ ἦν γένος.. ἁμερίων [prev. author] “IA” 1330; ἦ στωμύλος ἦσθα 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.79}}; so also when there is reference to a past thought, τουτὶ τί ἦ; what **is** this? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 157, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 387c: so in the Aristotelian formula τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι (“APo.” 82b38, +others)}}, used to express **the essential nature** of a thing, where τί ἦν (for ἐστί) takes the place of the _dative_ in such phrases as τὸ ἀγαθῷ εἶναι, τὸ μεγέθει εἶναι, {{Refs|“APr.” 67b12, “de An.” 429b10.}}
**G** ἐγώ εἰμι, in {{LXX|LXX, pleonastic for ἐγώ, ἐγώ εἰμι οὐχ ἥμαρτον LXX.Judg.11.27, compare 6.18; also ἔσται πᾶς ἀποκτενεῖ με LXX.Gen.4.14.}}

2254:
** ἐγώ**,
I: _pronoun_ of the first person:—_Epic dialect_ mostly **ἐγών** before vowels (so in _Doric dialect_, before consonants, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 85, 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 81, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 748, 754), rarely in Trag., 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 932 (Lyric poetry)}}; _Boeotian dialect_ **ἱών** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 51.4}}:— strengthened **ἔγωγε**, **I at least, for my part, indeed, for myself** (more frequently in _Attic dialect_ than in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus)}}: _Doric dialect_ **ἐγώνγα** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 51, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 736, “Lys.” 986, _dative_ ἐμίνγα “IG” 22.1126.7 (Amphict. Delph.)}}: _Boeotian dialect_ **ἱώνγα** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 21}}; **ἱώνει** {{Refs|Ead. 10}}; **ἰώγα** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 898}}: _Laconian dialect_ and _Tarentum dialect_ **ἐγώνη**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Conjunctionibus;” 255.29.}}
**II** oblique cases from a different root, _genitive_ ἐμοῦ, _enclitic_ μο; _Ionic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ ἐμέο, ἐμεῦ, μευ, also ἐμέθεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.525, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 177 (Lyric poetry)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἔμεθεν {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 23.7; ἐμεῖο “IG” 3.1337; μεθέν 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 20}}; _Doric dialect_ ἐμέος, ἐμεῦς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 144}}; _Boeotian dialect_ ἐμοῦς {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 37; also ἐμῶς, ἐμίο, ἐμίω, ἐμίως 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 74.17:}} — _dative_ ἐμοί, _enclitic_ μοι (which may be compared with Sanskrit _genitive_ **me** in κλῦθί μοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.115, +others); ὅ μοι πόσις “Schwyzer” 683 (5th c.AD: Cypria)}}; _Doric dialect_ ἐμίν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 99, “AJA” 29.461 (Rhodian, 5th c.BC), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 733, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 4.30}}; _Tarentum dialect_ ἐμίνη {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Rhinthon Comicus 13: _accusative_ ἐμέ}}, _enclitic_ μ; {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Cypria μι “Inscription Cyprian dialect” 59,60 H.}}
**III** dual, _nominative_ and _accusative_, **νῶι**, **we two**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.34, etc.}}; _accusative_ νῶιν Zenod.ad {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.377}}; _Attic dialect_ νώ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 278b (also 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.219, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.475); νῶε 5th-6th c.BC: Antimachus Colophonius Elegiacus et Epicus 39, 6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 5: _genitive_, _dative_ νῶι; νῷν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 3}}; νῶι _dative_, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Lithica” 773}}; νῶιν, ={ἡμῖν}, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 1.213, etc.}}
**IV** _plural_, _nominative_ ἡμεῖς (ἡμέες _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.6, +others, rejected by 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 93.1)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἄμμες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.303, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 18.3, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.144}}; _Doric dialect_ ἁμές {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 65, 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 42, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 168:— _genitive_ ἡμῶν (also ἥμων 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 130.23)}}; _Ionic dialect_ ἡμέων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.112, etc.; ἡμείων 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.170, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 1.46}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἀμμέων {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 88, “Milet.” 3 No.152.29}}; ἄμμων {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work]74, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 95.3}}; _Doric dialect_ ἁμέων {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 66}}; ἁμῶν [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus] 266, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 168, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.158}}; Cretan dialect, _Boeotian dialect_ ἁμίων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 528.5, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 95.21:— _dative_ ἡμῖν, in 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus also ἡμίν}} (ῐ) (or ἧμιν Aristarch.ad {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.214, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 95.3)}}; also rarely in Comedy texts, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Phrynichus Comicus 37, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 386 (uncertain)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἄμμῐν, ἄμμῐ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.384, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 80, +others, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.155, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 156 (Lyric poetry), “Milet.” 3 No.152; ἄμμεσιν 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 100}}; _Doric dialect_ also ἁμίν or ἇμιν, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 77, 78, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 347 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1081}}; with ῑ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ach.” 821, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.145:— _accusative_ ἡμᾶς (also ἧμᾰς 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.372)}}; _Ionic dialect_ ἡμέας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.211, “SIG” 273.25 (Milet., 4th c.BC)}}; ἥμεας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.294 (cf. Hdn.Gr. 2.140)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἄμμε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.59, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 115}}, Theocr.8.25; _Doric dialect_ ἁμέ {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“SIG” 1 (Abu Simbel, 6th c.BC), 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 173, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 759 codices, “Lys.” 95.}}—On these dialectic varieties, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|see 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 50}} _ff._ (Cf. Sanskrit _ahám_ (ἐγών), _accusative_ _plural_ **asmā´n**; for νώ cf. Sanskrit _nau_):—frequently in answers, as an affirmative, especially in form ἔγωγε, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1248, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 149b, etc.}}; οὗτος ἐ. here am {{Refs 5th c.BC+|I, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 4.26; ὅδ᾽ ἐκεῖνος ἐ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 138 (Lyric poetry)}}; rarely with _Article_, τὸν ἐμέ **myself**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 166a, “Sph.” 239b (but ὁ ἐ.}} **the Self, the Ego**, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 444)}}; τίς ὢν οὗτος ὁ ἐγὼ τυγχάν; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1119a; τί ἐστι φίλο; ἄλλος ἐ. 4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Hermias Alexandrinus Philosophus “in Platonis Phaedrum scholia” p.166 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus}}; τί τοῦτ᾽ ἐμο; ἡμῖν τί τοῦτ᾽ ἔστ; Latin _quid mea hoc refert_ ? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 498, etc.}}; ἐγ; in a question, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1336, +others}}; ἡμεῖς **the self**, ἔνθα δὴ ἡμεῖς μάλιστα {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 1.1.7.}}

2255:
** ἥμῐσυς**, εια, υ,
_genitive_ ἡμίσεος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.126, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.78, 4.83, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 18.8, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 205e, “IG” 22.1612.267, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.213, etc. (ἡμίσεως}} is sometimes a variant, as in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus}} [same places], and is found in later writers, as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.70); also as _feminine_, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.104}}; later _contraction_ ἡμίσους {{LXX+8th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.17, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Marius” 34, etc. (as _feminine_, LXX.1Ki.16.9): _nominative_ and _accusative_ _plural_ _masculine_, _Ionic dialect_ ἡμίσεες, -εας, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.7, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.51}}, _Attic dialect_ ἡμίσεις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.20, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 154c (ἡμίσεας is preferred by 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista “Praeparatio Sophistica” p.73B.): _neuter_ _plural_ ἡμίσεα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.16, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 438c, later ἡμίση 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 36.36 (codex S), +others, “IG” 22.1678.23, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 30.16, “IG” 12 (5).872.107 (from Tenos), “SIG” 2588.4(Delos, 2nd c.BC)}}, etc.: _Ionic dialect_ _feminine_ ἡμίσεᾰ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.111 (hyperion. -σέη 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Syr.D.” 14}}), _accusative_ _plural_ **-έας** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.27, also _accusative_ _sing._ ἡμίσεαν “IG” 2.1055.16, 1059.14, _genitive_ ἡμισέας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 83c}}: **ἥμυσυς** (assim.), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Rev.Phil.” 54.192 (Erythrae, 5th c.BC), “IG” 22.43 A 45 (4th c.BC), “PEleph.” 20.40 (4th c.BC), “IG” 11(2).161 A 23(Delos, 3rd c.BC), “UPZ” 54.6 (2nd c.BC)}}, etc.: _neuter_ **ἥμισον**, τό, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Berl.Sitzb.” 1927.8 (_Locrian dialect_, 5th c.BC), “SIG” 1011.7 (Chalcedon, 3rd-2nd c.BC), [prev. work]671 A 13 (Delph., 2nd c.BC), “BGU” 183.41 (1st c.AD): _plural_ ἥμισα “SIG” 56.7 (Argos, 5th c.BC)}}; also **ἥμισσον**, τό, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work]306.14 (4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus, 4th c.BC), 1009.20 (Ephesus, 3rd-2nd c.BC): _plural_ ἥμισσα [prev. work] 240 P (Delph., 4th c.BC)}}: _accusative_ _plural_ τοὺς ἡμίσους {{Refs|“Not.Arch.” 4.20 (Cyrene, Aug.)}}:—**half**,
**I** as _adjective_, ἡμίσεες λαοί **half** the people, ἡ. δ᾽ ἄρα λαοὶ ἐρητύοντο.. ἡ. δ᾽ ἀναβάντες ἐλαύνομεν [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.155 {ἡμισύτριτον}, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.7 (elsewhere 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus uses only _neuter_ ἥμισυ}}] as _substantive_ (see.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|below 11)); τοὺς ἡμίσεας ἀποστέλλειν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.51, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.20, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.16, etc.}}; ἥμισυς λόγος **half** the tale, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 428 (λόγου codex _middle_)}}; τὸ ἥμισυ τεῖχος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.78; ὁ ἥ. ἀριθμός 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 946a: with _genitive_, like a _comparative_, τὸ ὕψος ἥμισυ ἐτελέσθη οὗ διενοεῖτο}} **half of** what he intended, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.93: _metaphorically_, οὐδ᾽ ἂν ἥμισυς ἑαυτοῦ γένοιτο πρὸς ἀρετήν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 647d (οὐ δι᾽ ἥμισυν stands for οὐ διήμ}}. '**half-and-half**', {{Refs|[prev. work] 806c).}}
**I.2** in Prose also with the _substantive_ in _genitive_ and giving its gender and number to ἥμισυς, τῶν νήσων τὰς ἡμισέας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.10; τῶν ἀνδραπόδων τὰ ἡμίσεα [prev. author] 6.23}}; αἱ ἡμίσειαι τῶν νεῶν **half of** the ships, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.8; οἱ ἡμίσεις τῶν ἄρτων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.4; ὁ ἥμισυς τοῦ ἀριθμοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 104a; τοῦ χρόνου 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.8: _absolutely_, οἱ ἡ}}. **half** of them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.20.}}
**II** as _substantive_ in _neuter_, ἥ. τιμῆς, ἐνάρων, ἀρετῆς{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.616, 17.231, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.322}}; τὸ μὲν.., τὸ δ᾽ ἥ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.565; πλέον ἥ. παντός 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 40, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 466c; ὑπὲρ ἥ. πάντων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.47; ἥ. οὗ δεῖ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 77c, etc.}}; ἐν ἡμίσει τῆς νυκτός at **mid**night, {{LXX+8th c.BC+|LXX.Judg.16.3: usually with _Article_, τὸ ἥ. τοῦ στρατοῦ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.83, etc.; also τὤμισυ 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 559, “Schwyzer” 701 (Erythrae, 5th c.BC)}}; θἤμισυ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 116}}: _indeclinable_, ἀπὸ τοῦ ἥ. {{LXX|LXX.Exo.30.15}}; τῷ ἥ. φυλῆς{{LXX+5th c.BC+|LXX.Num.32.33: _plural_, τῆς χορείας τὰ ἡμίσεα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 672e; ἄρτων ἡμίσεα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.9.26}}; ῥαφανίδων τὰ ἡ. Thphr.[same place]: after Numerals, ἐν δυοῖν καὶ ἡμίσει ἡμέρας {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“IG” 22.1673.73; δεκατεττάρων καὶ ἡμίσους 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 2.5.39; μνῶν.. δώδεκα καὶ ἡμίσους 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.17; τετραποδίαν μίαν καὶ ἥμισυ “IG” 12.373.28; without καί, μυριάδων ἑπτὰ ἡμίσους 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Marius” 34}}: _indeclinable_, τριῶν ἥμισυ σταδίων {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 8.6.21, compare “PTeb.” 110.5 (1st c.BC), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Mi.” 44, etc.}}: as _adverb_, ἥ. μὲν νύμφην.., ἥ. δ᾽ αὖτε ὄφιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 298, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.87}}: so in _plural_, τὰ μὲν ἡμίσεα φιλόπονος, τὰ δὲ ἡ. ἄπονος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 535d: with Preps., οὐδ᾽ εἰς ἥ}}. not **half**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 452: regular}} _adverb_ ἡμισέως **half-done**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 601c.}}
**II.b** ἥμισυ, τό,= ἡμίεκτον, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**II.2** _feminine_, ἡ ἡμίσεια (i.e. μοῖρα), τῇ ἡμισείᾳ τῆς γῆς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.31; ἡ ἡ. τοῦ τιμήματος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 956d}}; οὐ γὰρ ἐφ᾽ ἡμισείᾳ χρηστὸν εἶναι δεῖ **by halves**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.277; ἐξ ἡμισείας 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Cat.” 1, 2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 1.26, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 10.145. (ἡμισυ- from ἡμιτυ-, ἡμισσο- from ἡμιτϝο-, compare ἡμίτεια, ἡμιτύεκτον}}; enlarged from ἡμι-.)

2257:
** ἐγώ**,
I: _pronoun_ of the first person:—_Epic dialect_ mostly **ἐγών** before vowels (so in _Doric dialect_, before consonants, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 85, 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 81, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 748, 754), rarely in Trag., 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 932 (Lyric poetry)}}; _Boeotian dialect_ **ἱών** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 51.4}}:— strengthened **ἔγωγε**, **I at least, for my part, indeed, for myself** (more frequently in _Attic dialect_ than in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus)}}: _Doric dialect_ **ἐγώνγα** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 51, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 736, “Lys.” 986, _dative_ ἐμίνγα “IG” 22.1126.7 (Amphict. Delph.)}}: _Boeotian dialect_ **ἱώνγα** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 21}}; **ἱώνει** {{Refs|Ead. 10}}; **ἰώγα** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 898}}: _Laconian dialect_ and _Tarentum dialect_ **ἐγώνη**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Conjunctionibus;” 255.29.}}
**II** oblique cases from a different root, _genitive_ ἐμοῦ, _enclitic_ μο; _Ionic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ ἐμέο, ἐμεῦ, μευ, also ἐμέθεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.525, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 177 (Lyric poetry)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἔμεθεν {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 23.7; ἐμεῖο “IG” 3.1337; μεθέν 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 20}}; _Doric dialect_ ἐμέος, ἐμεῦς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 144}}; _Boeotian dialect_ ἐμοῦς {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 37; also ἐμῶς, ἐμίο, ἐμίω, ἐμίως 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 74.17:}} — _dative_ ἐμοί, _enclitic_ μοι (which may be compared with Sanskrit _genitive_ **me** in κλῦθί μοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.115, +others); ὅ μοι πόσις “Schwyzer” 683 (5th c.AD: Cypria)}}; _Doric dialect_ ἐμίν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 99, “AJA” 29.461 (Rhodian, 5th c.BC), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 733, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 4.30}}; _Tarentum dialect_ ἐμίνη {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Rhinthon Comicus 13: _accusative_ ἐμέ}}, _enclitic_ μ; {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Cypria μι “Inscription Cyprian dialect” 59,60 H.}}
**III** dual, _nominative_ and _accusative_, **νῶι**, **we two**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.34, etc.}}; _accusative_ νῶιν Zenod.ad {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.377}}; _Attic dialect_ νώ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 278b (also 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.219, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.475); νῶε 5th-6th c.BC: Antimachus Colophonius Elegiacus et Epicus 39, 6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 5: _genitive_, _dative_ νῶι; νῷν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 3}}; νῶι _dative_, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Lithica” 773}}; νῶιν, ={ἡμῖν}, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 1.213, etc.}}
**IV** _plural_, _nominative_ ἡμεῖς (ἡμέες _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.6, +others, rejected by 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 93.1)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἄμμες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.303, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 18.3, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.144}}; _Doric dialect_ ἁμές {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 65, 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 42, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 168:— _genitive_ ἡμῶν (also ἥμων 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 130.23)}}; _Ionic dialect_ ἡμέων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.112, etc.; ἡμείων 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.170, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 1.46}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἀμμέων {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 88, “Milet.” 3 No.152.29}}; ἄμμων {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work]74, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 95.3}}; _Doric dialect_ ἁμέων {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 66}}; ἁμῶν [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus] 266, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 168, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.158}}; Cretan dialect, _Boeotian dialect_ ἁμίων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 528.5, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 95.21:— _dative_ ἡμῖν, in 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus also ἡμίν}} (ῐ) (or ἧμιν Aristarch.ad {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.214, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 95.3)}}; also rarely in Comedy texts, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Phrynichus Comicus 37, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 386 (uncertain)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἄμμῐν, ἄμμῐ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.384, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 80, +others, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.155, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 156 (Lyric poetry), “Milet.” 3 No.152; ἄμμεσιν 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 100}}; _Doric dialect_ also ἁμίν or ἇμιν, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 77, 78, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 347 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1081}}; with ῑ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ach.” 821, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.145:— _accusative_ ἡμᾶς (also ἧμᾰς 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.372)}}; _Ionic dialect_ ἡμέας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.211, “SIG” 273.25 (Milet., 4th c.BC)}}; ἥμεας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.294 (cf. Hdn.Gr. 2.140)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἄμμε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.59, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 115}}, Theocr.8.25; _Doric dialect_ ἁμέ {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“SIG” 1 (Abu Simbel, 6th c.BC), 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 173, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 759 codices, “Lys.” 95.}}—On these dialectic varieties, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|see 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 50}} _ff._ (Cf. Sanskrit _ahám_ (ἐγών), _accusative_ _plural_ **asmā´n**; for νώ cf. Sanskrit _nau_):—frequently in answers, as an affirmative, especially in form ἔγωγε, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1248, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 149b, etc.}}; οὗτος ἐ. here am {{Refs 5th c.BC+|I, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 4.26; ὅδ᾽ ἐκεῖνος ἐ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 138 (Lyric poetry)}}; rarely with _Article_, τὸν ἐμέ **myself**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 166a, “Sph.” 239b (but ὁ ἐ.}} **the Self, the Ego**, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 444)}}; τίς ὢν οὗτος ὁ ἐγὼ τυγχάν; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1119a; τί ἐστι φίλο; ἄλλος ἐ. 4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Hermias Alexandrinus Philosophus “in Platonis Phaedrum scholia” p.166 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus}}; τί τοῦτ᾽ ἐμο; ἡμῖν τί τοῦτ᾽ ἔστ; Latin _quid mea hoc refert_ ? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 498, etc.}}; ἐγ; in a question, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1336, +others}}; ἡμεῖς **the self**, ἔνθα δὴ ἡμεῖς μάλιστα {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 1.1.7.}}

2258:
** εἰμί**
(**sum**), _Aeolic dialect_ **ἔμμι** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 2.15, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 20.32}}; Cretan dialect **ἠμί** {{Refs|“GDI” 4959a}}; _2nd pers._ _singular_ εἶ, _Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ εἰς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.388, +others, _Aeolic dialect_ ἔσσι}}, _Epic dialect_ and _Doric dialect_ ἐσσί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.176, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.90, 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 134; ἐσί “GDI” 4959a}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἐστί, _Doric dialect_ ἐντί {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 12(1).677 (from Rhodes), 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.17, etc.}}; _3rd.pers._ _dual_ ἐστόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.112}}; _1st pers._ _plural_ ἐσμέν, _Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ εἰμέν (also in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.60), ἐμέν 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 294}}, _Doric dialect_ εἰμές {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.73, but ἠμέν “GDI” 5178.34}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ εἰσί (-ίν), _Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ ἔασι (-ιν) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.73, 6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 8.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Antimachus Colophonius Elegiacus et Epicus 29, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 4.84}}, _Doric dialect_ ἐντί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.24, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 11.45, “IG” 9(1).32.22 (from Phocis), etc.: _imperative_ ἴσθι (ἔσθι 4th-5th c.BC: Hecataeus Milesius Historicus 361 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus)}}, _Epic dialect_ and Lyric poetry also in _middle_ form ἔσσο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.200, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 1.28, Macedonius Lyricus “Paean” 31, late Prose ἔσο 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.241d, 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 3.5, 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 5.12, 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “ad Marcellam - Porphyrii Opuscula” 34}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἔστω (ἤτω {{LXX+5th c.BC+|LXX.Psa.103.31, and late Inscrr., “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2664, +others; but in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 361c to be read ἴτω), _Doric dialect_ εἴτω, ἤτω, 4th c.AD: Heraclides Historicus cited in 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1411.21, Elean ἤστω “Schwyzer” 424}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἔστωσαν, but ἔστων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 502a, ὄντων [prev. author] “Lg.” 879b}}, and early _Attic dialect_ Inscrr., {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“IG” 12.22, etc. (ἔστωσαν first in 2nd c.BC, [prev. work]22.1328), _Doric dialect_ ἐόντων [prev. work]1126:}}_subjunctive_ ὦ, ᾖς, ᾖ, _Epic dialect_ ἔω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.18}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἔῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.300, +others (also ἔῃσι 2.366, +others, ᾖσι (ν) 19.202, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 294)}}, also _Boeotian dialect_ ἔνθω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“IG” 7.3172.165, μετείω 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.47 and perhaps εἴῃ 9.245, etc.}}; _Doric dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ὦντι {{Refs|“SIG” 940.3 (from Crete), ἔωντι “GDI” 5040.14 (from Hierapytna)}}, _Boeotian dialect_ ἴωνθι {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“IG” 7.3171.46 (3rd c.BC): _optative_ εἴην, -ης (εἴησθα 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 715)}}, -η, also ἔοις, ἔοι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.284, 142, +others, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.6}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ εἴοισαν Ἀρχ. Ἐφ. {{Refs|1911.133 (from Gonni)}}; _3rd.pers._ _dual_ εἴτην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 149e, “Sph.” 243e}}; _1st pers._ _plural_ εἶμεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 921 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus}}; _2nd pers._ _plural_ εἶτε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.195}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ εἶεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.372, etc., εἴησαν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.2, etc.}}; Elean ἔα, ={εἴη}, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|“SIG” 9 (6th c.BC), and σύν-εαν, ={συνεῖεν}, “GDI” 1149 (6th c.BC):}}]_infinitive_ εἶναι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus ἦναι “SIG” 306.9 (Tegea, 4th c.BC)}}; _Epic dialect_ ἔμμεναι (also _Aeolic dialect_ ἔμμεν᾽ {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 34), ἔμμεν (also 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 6.42, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 623 (Lyric poetry)), ἔμεναι, ἔμεν, also ἔμειν “SIG” 1166 (from Dodona)}}; _Doric dialect_ εἶμεν Foed. cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.77, 79, “IG” 7.1.7 (from Megara), ἦμεν “Test.Epict.” 5.16, “Tab.Heracl.” 1.75, Cretan dialect ἦμεν or ἤμην “Leg.Gort.” 1.15, +others, “GDI” 4998i 2, +others, Megarian dialect εἴμεναι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 775, εἴμειν “IG” 12(1).155.100 (from Rhodes), 14.952 (from Agrigentum); εἶν [prev. work] 12(9).211.10 (from Eretria), “SIG” 135.4 (from Olynthus), etc.:}}_participle_ ὤν, _Epic dialect_ ἐών, ἐοῦσα, ἐόν, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Cypria ἰών “Inscription Cyprian dialect” 135.23 H.}}; _Boeotian dialect_ _feminine_ ἰῶσα {{Refs|“IG” 7.3172.116 (Orchom.)}}, _Aeolic dialect_ and _Doric dialect_ _feminine_ ἔσσα {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 75.4, “IG” 4.952.2 (Epid.), 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 28.16, ἐοῖσα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.265, ἔασσα “Lyrica Alexandrina Adespota” 9, Diotogenes Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.7.62, εὖσα Erinna Lyrica 5.5}} (also _Ionic dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 5.16, εὔντων 2.85), ἐᾶσα 5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 96d, “IG” 5(1).1470.8 (from Messene), ἴαττα “Leg.Gort.” 8.47; _accusative_ _sing._ εὖντα 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.3}}; _nominative_ _singular_ εἴς in {{Refs 7th c.BC+|4th c.AD: Heraclides Historicus cited in 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1756.13, _plural_ ἔντες “Tab.Heracl.” 1.117; _dative_ _plural_ ἔντασσι [prev. work]104; _genitive_ _plural_ παρ-έντων 7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 64}}: _imperfect_ ἦν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.77, etc., _Epic dialect_ ἔον}} (also _Aeolic dialect_, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 127, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in 1787 “fragment” 3 ii 21), in _Attic dialect_ ἦ}} (uncertain in _Aeolic dialect_, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 14.9), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 77, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 61b, etc., but usually altered to ἦν in codices (and ἦν}} is required by metre in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 280), _contraction_ from _Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ ἦα (8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.808, +others, “IG” 12(8).449.2 (from Thasos)}}, whence Hom.and later _Ionic dialect_ ἔᾱ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.321, +others, ἔας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.187, ἔατε [prev. author] 4.119)}}; _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἦεν, always with ν in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}; ἔην as _1st pers._ _singular_, only {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.762 (assuming variant, +others ἔον), frequently as _3rd.pers._ _sing._ (generally before a consonant, so that ἔεν is possible), sometimes also ἤην}}; _2nd pers._ _singular_ ἦσθα, later ἦς (which is variant in {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 1.26), sometimes in LXX (LXX.Judg.11.35, LXX.Ruth.3.2, +others), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 365e, Erinna Lyrica 4.4, NT.Matt.25.21, +others, ἦσθας 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 156, _Epic dialect_ ἔησθα}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἦν, _Epic dialect_ ἔην, ἤην, ἦεν (see. above), _Doric dialect_ and _Aeolic dialect_ ἦς {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 30.1, 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 102, 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 59, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.90, “SIG” 241.145 (Delph.)}}; _3rd.pers._ _dual_ ἤστην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.10, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 387, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 982, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 272a, +others}}; _Doric dialect_ _1st pers._ _plural_ ἦμες {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lycurgus” 21}}; _2nd pers._ _plural_ ἦτε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 276c, ἦστε 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 821, “Ec.” 1086}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἦσαν, _Ionic dialect_ and poetical ἔσαν (in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 321, 825, ἦν is not _plural_ for ἦσαν}}, but is rather a peculiarity of syntax, see below see, but is _3rd.pers._ _plural_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 46, +others, “SIG” 560.15 (Epidamnus, 3rd c.BC))}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἔον {{NT+5th c.BC+|“Schwyzer” 644.12; later ἤμην “PSI” 4.362.21 (3rd c.BC), “SIG” 527.46 (Crete, 3rd c.BC), “IGRom.” 4.1740 (from Cyme), always in LXX as LXX.Bar.1.19, cf. NT.Matt.23.30, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.174a, etc., and sometimes in codices of earlier writers, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.34, “Tragica Adespota” 124 (cited from 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 931 by 4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus and from [prev. author] “Tr.” 474 by Aps.), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.9, 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “adv. Athenogenem” 26}}, _2nd pers._ _singular_ ἦσο {{Refs|“epigram Gr.” 379 (from Aezani)}}, _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἦστο {{Refs|“Supp.epigram” 1.455.7 (from Phrygia)}}, _1st pers._ _plural_ ἤμεθα {{NT+3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 2p.11 (3rd c.BC), LXX.Bar.1.19, LXX.1Ki.25.16, NT.Eph.2.3}}; _subjunctive_ ὦμαι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PBaden” 48.12 (2nd c.BC), ἦται “GDI” , ἦνται probably in “IG” 5(1).1390.83 (from Andania)}}; _Ionic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ also ἔσκον, used by {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 656 (Lyric poetry):}}_future_ ἔσομαι, ἔσται, _Epic dialect_ and _Aeolic dialect_ also ἔσσομαι, ἔσεται, ἔσσετα; _Aeolic dialect_ _2nd pers._ _singular_ ἔσσῃ probably in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 67, 87; _Doric dialect_ 2 and _3rd.pers._ _sing._ ἐσσῇ, ἐσσεῖται, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.393, 13.317, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 10.5, _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἐσσοῦνται Foed. cited in 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.77 codices (but ἔσσονται “Tab.Heracl.” 1.113)}}, _infinitive_ ἐσσεῖσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 57.}} —All forms of the _present_ _indicative_ are enclitic (except _2nd pers._ _singular_ εἶ and _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἔασι); but _3rd.pers._ _singular_ is written ἔστι when it begins a sentence or verse, or when it immediately follows οὐκ, καί, εἰ, ὡς, ἀλλά, or τοῦτ᾽, Hdn.Gr.{{Refs|1.553 (also μή _accusative_ to “Etymologicum Magnum” 301.3)}}; later Grammars wrote ἔστι as _substantive_ Verb, {{Refs 9th c.AD+|9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc., 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 880.22.}}
**A** as the _substantive_ Verb,
**A.I** of persons, **exist**, οὐκ ἔσθ᾽ οὗτος ἀνήρ, οὐδ᾽ ἔσσεται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.437}}; ἔτ᾽ εἰσί they **are** still **in being**,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|15.433, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 445, etc.; τεθνηῶτος.. μηδ᾽ ἔτ᾽ ἐόντος 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.289}}; οὐκέτ᾽ ἐστί he **is** no more, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1162}}; οὐδὲ δὴν ἦν he **was** not long-**lived**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.131}}; ὁ οὐκ ὤν, οἱ οὐκ ὄντες, of **those who are** no more, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.45, 44}}; οἱ ὄντες the **living**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.29.2}}; ὁ ὤν the {{LXX+8th c.BC+|“Eternal”, LXX.Exo.3.14, +others, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.289; θεοὶ αἰὲν ἐόντες 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.290}}; ἐσσόμενοι **posterity**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|2.119; κἀγὼ γὰρ ἦ ποτ᾽, ἀλλὰ νῦν οὐκ εἴμ᾽ ἔτι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 284}}; ὡς ἂν εἶεν ἅνθρωποι **might continue in being**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 190c; ζώντων καὶ ὄντων Ἀθηναίων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.72, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione et Corruptione” 318b25}}; of things, εἰ ἔστι ἀληθέως [ἡ τράπεζα] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.17, etc.}}; of cities, ὄλωλεν, οὐδ᾽ ἔτ᾽ ἐστὶ Τροία {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1292, compare “Heracl.” 491}}; δοκεῖ μοι Καρχηδόνα μὴ εἶναι **censeo Carthaginem esse delendam**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Ma.” 27}}; ἂν ᾖ τὸ στράτευμα **be in existence**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.17}}; of money, **to be in hand**, τῶν ὄντων χρημάτων καὶ τῶν προσιόντων {{Refs|“IG” 12.91.25}}; τὰ ὄντα **property**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 511a, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 24, etc.}}; τὸ ἐσόμενον ἐκ.. **future revenue** from.., {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BCH” 46.420 (Olymos, 1st c.BC)}}; of place, τὴν οὖσαν ἐκκλησίαν the **local** church, {{NT|NT.Act.13.1}}; of time, τοῦ ὄντος μηνός in the **current** month, {{Refs|“BGU” 146.4, etc.}}; **in office**, ἱερέων τῶν ὄντων {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PPar.” 5.4 (2nd c.BC)}}; αἱ οὖσαι [ἐξουσίαι] the powers **that be, Ep. Rom.**{{Refs|13.1.}}
**A.II** of the **real** world, **be**, opposed to become, γίγνεται πάντα ἃ δή φαμεν εἶναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 152d, etc.}}; τὸ ὄν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Being”, 4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 8.35, 5th c.BC: Protagoras Philosophus 2, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 27d, etc.}}; opposed to τὸ μὴ ὄν, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Gorgias Rhetor et Sophista “fragment” 3 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, etc.; οὐδὲν γίνεται ἐκ τοῦ μὴ ὄντος 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.5U.; ἐξ οὐκ ὄντων ἐποίησεν αὐτὰ ὁ θεός LXX.2Mac.7.28}}; τὰ ὄντα the **world of things**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 7, 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 129.5, etc.}}; ὄν_indeclinable_, τῶν ὂν εἰδῶν species **of Being**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.2.10.}}
**A.II.2** of circumstances, events, etc., **to happen**, τά τ᾽ ἐόντα, τά τ᾽ ἐσσόμενα, πρό τ᾽ ἐόντα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.70; ἡ ἐσβολὴ ἔμελλεν ἔσεσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.13, etc.}}; τῆς προδοσίας οὔσης since treachery **was there**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.103}}; ἕως ἂν ὁ πόλεμος ᾖ so long as it **last**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.58; αἱ σπονδαὶ ἐνιαυτὸν ἔσονται [prev. author] 4.118}}; τί ἐστι; what **is** it? what's the matter? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 193}}; τί οὖν ἦν τοῦτ; how **came** it **to pass**? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 58a: repeated with a _relative_ to avoid a positive assertion, ἔστι δ᾽ ὅπῃ νῦν ἔ.}} things **are** as they **are**, i.e. are ill, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 67.}}
**A.III** **be the fact** or **the case**, διπλασίαν ἂν τὴν δύναμιν εἰκάζεσθαι ἤ ἐστιν twice as large as it **really is**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.10}}; αὐτὸ ὅ ἐστι καλόν beauty **in its essence**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 211c, compare “Phd.” 74b}}; frequently in _participle_, τὸν ἐόντα λόγον λέγειν or φαίνειν the **true** story, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.95, 116}}; τῷ ἐόντι χρήσασθαι tell the **truth**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 30; τὰ ὄντα ἀπαγγέλλειν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.8}}; σκῆψιν οὐκ οὖσαν, λόγον οὐκ ὄντα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 584, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1052}}; τῷ ὄντι **in reality, in fact**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 328d, etc.}}; to apply a quotation to a case in point, τῷ ὄντι κλαυσίγελως **real** 'smiles through tears' (with allusion to {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.484), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.2.9, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 196d}}; κατὰ τὸ ἐόν according to the **fact, right**ly, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.97}}; πᾶν τὸ ἐόν the whole **truth**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 9.11; τοῦ ἐόντος ἀποτεύξεται 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 2.}}
**A.IV** followed by the _relative_, οὐκ ἔστιν ὅς or ὅστις **no one**, οὐκ ἔσθ᾽ ὃς.. ἀπαλάλκοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.348; οὐκ ἔ. οὐδεὶς ὅς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 903}}; οὐκ ἔ. ὅτῳ, ={οὐδενί}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 293 (_anapaest meter_), compare 989: frequently in _plural_, εἰσὶν οἵ}}, = Latin _sunt qui_, used exactly like{ἔνιοι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.88, 7.44, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 77d, “Grg.” 503a, etc. (εἰσί τινες οἵ.. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.24)}}; ἐστὶν ἃ χωρία, πολίσματα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.12, 65; ἐστὶν ἃ εἰπεῖν [prev. author] 2.67; ἦσαν οἵ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.2.14}}; the _singular_ Verb is used even with _masculine_ and _feminine_ _plural_, ἐστὶν οἵ, αἵ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 1, “VC” 4, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.3.16}}; more frequently in oblique cases, ποταμῶν ἐστὶ ὧν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.187; ἐστὶν ἀφ᾽ ὧν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.65}}; ἐστὶ παρ᾽ οἷς, ἐστὶν ἐν οἷς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.23, 5.25: in questions ὅστις is used, ἔστιν ἥντινα δόξαν.. ἀπεκρίνατ; 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 85b: with _relative_ Particles, ἐστὶν ἔνθα}}, = Latin _est ubi_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.4.15, etc.}}; ἐ. ὅπῃ, ἔσθ᾽ ὅπου, **some**how, **some**where, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 331d, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 517, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 448, etc.}}; in questions expecting a _negative_ answer, ἐ. ὁπόθεν, ὅπω; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 35a, “R.” 493e, etc.; οὐ γάρ ἐσθ᾽ ὅπως 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 61, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.102, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 620}}; οὐκ ἔ. ὅπως οὐ **in any case, necessarily**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 188; οὐκ ἔ. ὡς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 76e, etc.}}; ἐ. ὅτε, ἔσθ᾽ ὅτε, **some**times, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 180.2, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 56, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.21, etc.}}
**A.V** ἦν is sometimes used with _plural_ _masculine_ and _feminine_, usually at the beginning of a sentence, **there was**, τῆς δ᾽ ἦν τρεῖς κεφαλαί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 321; (but in ἦν δ᾽ ἐρῳδιοί τε πολλοί 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 46, compare 59, +others, it may be taken as _Doric dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_)}}; ἦν δ᾽ ἀμφίπλεκτοι κλίμακες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 520 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἦν ἄρα κἀκεῖνοι ταλακάρδιοι epigram cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.184; less frequently ἔστι, ἔστι δὲ μεταξὺ.. ἑπτὰ στάδιοι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.26, compare 7.34; ἔστι.. ἄρχοντές τε καὶ δῆμος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 463a}}; before dual Nouns, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 58, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 500d.}}
**A.VI** ἔστι _impersonal_, with _infinitive_, **it is possible**, ἔστι γὰρ ἀμφοτέροισιν ὀνείδεα μυθήσασθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.246; ἔστι μὲν εὕδειν, ἔστι δὲ τερπομένοισιν ἀκούειν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.392}}; εἴ τί πού ἐστι (i.e. πιθέσθαι){{Refs 5th c.BC+|4.193; τοιάδε.. ἐστὶν ἀκοῦσαι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1055 (_anapaest meter_); ἔστι τεκμήρια ὁρᾶν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.2.13, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1163, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.105, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.272, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 53.6, etc.}}; so in _imperative_, _optative_, and _subjunctive_, ἔστω ἀποφέρεσθαι τῷ βουλομένῳ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“IG” 12.10.7; μυρία ἂν εἴη λέγειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 271e; ὅπως ἂν ᾖ δρᾶν “IG” 2.1054.91: more frequently in _negative_ clauses, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.267, etc.}}; followed by ὥστε with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 656}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, ἁδόντα δ᾽ εἴη με τοῖς ἀγαθοῖς ὁμιλεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.96; ἔστιν ἐκπεσεῖν ἀρχῆς Δία 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 757}}: sometimes not _impersonal_ in this sense, θάλασσα δ᾽ οὐκέτ᾽ ἦν ἰδεῖν {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pers.” 419.}}
**A.VI.b** ἔστω in argument, **let it be granted**, ἔστω τοῦτο ἀληθὲς εἶναι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 25; ἔστω σοι τοῦθ᾽ οὕτως 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.987b; ἔστω εἶναί τινα τοιοῦτον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator Chr.74.24.}}
**B** most frequently, **to be**, the Copula connecting the predicate with the Subject, both being in the same case: hence, **signify, import**, τὸ γὰρ εἴρειν λέγειν ἐστίν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 398d}}; especially in the phrase τοῦτ᾽ ἔστι, **hoc est**; Σκαιόλαν, ὅπερ ἐστὶ Λαϊόν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Publicola” 17: with numerals, τὰ δὶς πέντε δέκα ἐστίν}} twice five **are** ten, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.4.7}}; εἶναί τις or τι, **to be** somebody, something, **be** of some consequence, see at {τι; οὐδὲν εἶναι} {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 562d, etc.}}
**B.2** _periphrastic_ with the Participle to represent the finite Verb: with _perfect_ _participle_ once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, τετληότες εἰμέν, for τετλήκαμεν, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.873}}; so in Trag. and _Attic dialect_, ἦν τεθνηκώς, for ἐτεθνήκει, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 869}}; ἔσται δεδορκώς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1179; εἰμὶ γεγώς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1299; πεφυκός ἐστι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1473; δεδρακότες εἰσίν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.68; κατακεκονότες ἔσεσθε 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.6.36}}: with _aorist_ _participle_, once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, βλήμενος ἦν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.211}}; so προδείσας εἰμί, οὐ σιωπήσας ἔσε; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 90, 1146, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 460}}: with _present_ _participle_, ἦν προκείμενον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pers.” 371; φεύγων Ὀρέστης ἐστίν [prev. author] “Ch.” 136; εἴην οὐκ ἂν εὖ φρονῶν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1330}}; τί δ᾽ ἐστί.. φέρο; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “OT” 991, compare 274, 708; λέγων ἐστίν τις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1179; ἦν τίς σ᾽ ὑβρίζων [prev. author] “HF” 313; πόρρω ἤδη εἶ πορευόμενος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 204b; βαδίζων εἰμί 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 36; frequently in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, ἦσαν ἱέντες 1.57, +others; even εἰσὶ διάφοροι ἐόντες 3.49 (assuming variant):}} — if the _Article_ is joined with the Part., the noun is made emphatic, Κᾶρές εἰσι οἱ καταδέξαντες the persons who showed her **were** Carians, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.171; αὐτὸς ἦν ὁ μαρτυρῶν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 798; δόλος ἦν ὁ φράσας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 197 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**C** εἶναι is frequently modified in sense by the addition of _adverbs_, or the cases of Nouns without or with Preps.:
**C.I** εἶναι with _adverbs_, where the _adverb_ often merely represents a Noun and stands as the predicate, ἅλις δέ οἱ ἦσαν ἄρουραι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.122, etc.}}; ἀκέων, ἀκήν εἶναι, **to be** silent,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|4.22, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.82; σῖγα πᾶς ἔστω λεώς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 532; διαγνῶναι χαλεπῶς ἦν ἄνδρα ἕκαστον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.424}}; ἀσφαλέως ἡ κομιδὴ ἔσται **will go on** safely, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.134}}; ἐγγύς, πόρρω εἶναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.88, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 356e: frequently _impersonal_ with words implying good or ill fortune, Κουρήτεσσι κακῶς ἦν}} **it fared** ill with them, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.551; εὖ γὰρ ἔσται 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 89, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1188, etc.; ἡδέως ἂν αὐτοῖς εἴη 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.30.}}
**C.II** with _genitive_, to express descent or extraction, πατρὸς δ᾽ εἴμ᾽ ἀγαθοῖο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.109; αἵματός εἰς ἀγαθοῖο 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.611, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.71, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.71, etc.; πόλεως μεγίστης εἶ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.3.19.}}
**C.II.b** to express the material of which a thing is made, ἡ κρηπίς ἐστι λίθων μεγάλων **consists of**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.93; τῆς πόλιος ἐούσης δύο φαρσέων [prev. work] 186}}; τοιούτων ἔργων ἐστὶ ἡ τυραννίς **is made up of**.., {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.92}}.ή, etc.
**C.II.c** to express the class to which a person or thing belongs, εἶ γὰρ τῶν φίλων **you are one of** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 345; ἐτύγχανε βουλῆς ὤν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.70; ὅσοι ἦσαν τῶν προτέρων στρατιωτῶν [prev. author] 7.44; Κριτίας τῶν τριάκοντα ὤν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.31}}; ἔστι τῶν αἰσχρῶν **it is in the class of** disgraceful things, i. e. **it is** disgraceful, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.2.}}
**C.II.d** to express that a thing belongs to another, Τροίαν Ἀχαιῶν οὖσαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 269; τὸ πεδίον ἦν μέν κοτε Χορασμίων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.117, etc.}}: hence, **to be of the party of**, ἦσαν.. τινὲς μὲν φιλίππου, τινὲς δὲ τοῦ βελτίστου {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.56, compare 37.53}}; **to be de pendent upon**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 737, etc.}}; **to be at the mercy of**, ἔστι τοῦ λέγοντος, ἢν φόβους λέγῃ {{Refs|[prev. author] “OT” 917.}}
**C.II.e** to express one's duty, business, custom, nature, and the like, οὔτοι γυναικός ἐστι **'tis** not a woman's **part**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 940; τὸ ἐπιτιμᾶν παντὸς εἶναι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.16}}; τὸ δὲ ναυτικὸν τέχνης ἐστίν **is matter of** art, **requires** art, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.142, compare 83.}}
**C.II.f** in LXX, **to be occupied about**, ἦσαν τοῦ θύειν {{LXX|LXX.2Ch.30.17}}; ἔσεσθαι, with _genitive_, **to be about to**, ἐσόμεθα τοῦ σῶσαί σε {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.10.11.}}
**C.III** with the _dative_, ἐστί μοι I **have**, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc.}}
**C.III.2** with two datives., σφίσι τε καὶ Ἀθηναίοισι εἶναι οὐδὲν πρῆγμα that they and the Athenians **have** nothing **to do** one with another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.84; μηδὲν εἶναι σοὶ καὶ φιλίππῳ πρᾶγμα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.283}}; more shortly, σοί τε καὶ τούτοισι πρήγμασι τί ἐστ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.33}}; τί τῷ νόμῳ καὶ τῇ βασάν; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 29.36}}; τί ἐμοὶ καὶ σο; Latin _quid tecum est mihi?_ {{NT|NT.Mark.5.7, etc.}}; also ἐμοὶ οὐδὲν πρὸς τοὺς τοιούτους (i.e. ἐστίν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.12; ἐν οἷς πρὸς τοὺς ἐναντίους ἐστὶ τῷ δήμῳ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.278}}; ἔσται αὐτῳ πρὸς τὸν θεόν, in tomb inscriptions, {{Refs|“JHS” 18.113, etc.}}
**C.III.3** with ἄσμενος, βουλόμενος, etc., added, ἐμοὶ δέ κεν ἀσμένῳ εἴη **'twould be** to my delight, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.108; οὐκ ἂν σφίσι βουλομένοις εἶναι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.35; προσδεχομένῳ [prev. author] 6.46; θέλοντι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1356 (Lyric poetry); ἡδομένοις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 187c.}}
**C.IV** with Preps., εἶναι ἀπό τινος, ={εἶναί τινος}{{Refs 5th c.BC+|(above 11.a), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.6.9; εἰσὶν ἀπ᾽ ἐναντίων αὗται πραγμάτων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 12d}}; but εἶναι ἀπ᾽ οἴκου **to be** away from.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.99.}}
**C.IV.2** εἶναι ἔκ τινος **to be sprung** from, εἴμ᾽ ἐκ Παιονίης, Μυρμιδόνων ἔξ εἰμι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.154, 24.397, etc.}}; ἔστιν ἐξ ἀνάγκης **it is** of necessity, i. e. necessary, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 256d.}}
**C.IV.3** εἶναι ἐν.. **to be** in a certain state, ἐν εὐπαθείῃσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.22}}; ἐν ἀθυμία, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.46, etc.; ἐν ταραχαῖς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.218}}; εἶναι ἐν ἀξιώματι **to be** in esteem, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.130}}; οἱ ἐν τέλεϊ ἐόντες those in office, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.18, etc.}}; but εἶναι ἐν τέχνῃ, ἐν φιλοσοφία **to be engaged** in.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 562, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 59a.}}
**C.IV.3.b** ἐν σοί ἐστι **it depends** on thee, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.109, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 963; ἐν σοὶ γάρ ἐσμεν [prev. author] “OT” 314; so also ἐπί τινι [prev. author] “Ph.” 1003, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.2, etc.}}
**C.IV.4** εἶναι διά.., much like{εἶναι ἐν}.., εἶναι διὰ φόβου, ={φοβεῖσθαι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.34}}; εἶναι δι᾽ ὄχλου, ={ὀχληρὸν εἶναι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.73; εἶναι διὰ μόχθων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.25}}; εἶναι δι᾽ αἰτίας, ={αἰτιᾶσθαι}, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.70}}; Geometry texts, **pass** through, διὰ τᾶς ἑτέρας διαμέτρου ἐόντος τοῦ ἐπιπέδου {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ κωνοειδέων καὶ σφαιροειδέων” 20.}}
**C.IV.5** εἶναι ἐφ᾽ ἑαυτῆς **to be** by oneself, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.23}}; εἶναι ἐπὶ ὀνόματος **to bear** a name, {{Refs|[prev. author] 39.21}}; εἶναι ἐπὶ τοῖς πράγμασιν **to be engaged** in.., {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.12}}; εἶναι ἐπί τινα **to be** against him, {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.33}}; εἶναι ἐφ᾽ ἑξήκοντα στάδια **to reach** sixty stadia, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.6.11}}; εἶναι ἐπὶ τὰς ἁφάς **pass** through the points of contact, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Biographus Perg. “Con.” 4.1}}; εἶναι ἐπί τινι, see above 3 b.
**C.IV.6** εἶναι πρός τινος **to be** in one's favour, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.10, 29, etc.}}; **to suit**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.11, etc.}}; εἶναι πρός τινι **engaged** in, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 84c, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 5.31}}; πρὸς τοῖς ἰδίοις **mind** one's own affairs, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1309a6, “Ath.” 16.3; εἶναι πρὸς τὸ κωλύειν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.26.3}}; πρὸς τὸ πονεῖν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Teles p.46 H.; εἶναι περί τι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.5.7, etc.}}
**C.IV.7** εἶναι παρά τινι or τινα, = παρειναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 6.2.15, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.140.ά}} (assuming variant).
**C.IV.8** εἶναι ὑπό τινα or τινι **to be** subject to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.2.17 (assuming variant), 6.2.4.}}
**C.IV.9** περὶ τούτων ἐστίν that **is** the question, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 30.}}
**C.IV.10** εἶναι ἀπό.., in Geometry texts, **to be constructed** upon, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ σφαίρας καὶ κυλίνδρου” 2.9, “Con.Sph.” 7.}}
**D** ἐστί is very frequently omitted, mostly in the _present_ _indicative_ before certain predicates, as ἀνάγκη, ἄξιον, δυνατόν, εἰκός, ἕτοιμον, οἷόν τε, ῥᾴδιον, χρεών, etc., and after the _neuter_ of Verbals in -τέος, and such forms as θαυμαστὸν ὅσον: less frequently with other persons and moods, εἰμί omitted, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 92, “Aj.” 813}}; εἶ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.206}}; ἐσμέν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 634}}; ἐστέ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.463}}; εἰσί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 499 (Lyric poetry), “IG” 2.778 B}}; _subjunctive_ ᾖ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.376, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 659, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.32}}; _optative_ εἴη, {{Refs|“IG” 22.1183.12}}; _imperfect_ ἦν, {{Refs|[prev. work]2.778 B}}; _future_ ἔσονται, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.394.}}
**E** the Inf. frequently seems redundant,
**E.1** in phrases implying power or will to do a thing, ἑκὼν εἶναι (see. ἑκών) κατὰ δύναμιν εἶναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 2.32; εἰς δύναμιν εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 300c}}; τὸ ἐπ᾽ ἐκείνοις εἶναι, **quantum in illis esset**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.48, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.5.9, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.58; τὸ ἐπὶ σφᾶς εἶναι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.28; τὸ κατὰ τοῦτον εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.6.9; κατὰ τοῦτο εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 317a}}; τὸ τήμερον, τὸ νῦν εἶναι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Cra.” 396e, “La.” 201c, Theopomp. Comedy texts 98, Decrees cited in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 31.2, etc.}}
**E.2** after Verbs of naming or choosing, σοφιστὴν ὀνομάζουσι τὸν ἄνδρα εἶναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 311e; σύμμαχόν μιν εἵλοντο εἶναι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.134}}; of giving, δῶκε ξεινήϊον εἶναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.20.}}
**F** _imperfect_ ἦνissts. used where other languages take the _present_,
**F.1** after ἄρα, to express a fact which **is and has always been** the same, δέρμα δὲ ἀνθρώπου.. ἦν ἄρα σχεδὸν δερμάτων πάντων λαμπρότατον human skin then **it appears is**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.64; Κύπρις οὐκ ἄρ᾽ ἦν θεός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 359}}; ὡς ἄρ᾽ ἦσθ᾽ ἐμὸς πατὴρ ὀρθῶς{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. work] 1169; ἦ πολύμοχθον ἄρ᾽ ἦν γένος.. ἁμερίων [prev. author] “IA” 1330; ἦ στωμύλος ἦσθα 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.79}}; so also when there is reference to a past thought, τουτὶ τί ἦ; what **is** this? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 157, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 387c: so in the Aristotelian formula τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι (“APo.” 82b38, +others)}}, used to express **the essential nature** of a thing, where τί ἦν (for ἐστί) takes the place of the _dative_ in such phrases as τὸ ἀγαθῷ εἶναι, τὸ μεγέθει εἶναι, {{Refs|“APr.” 67b12, “de An.” 429b10.}}
**G** ἐγώ εἰμι, in {{LXX|LXX, pleonastic for ἐγώ, ἐγώ εἰμι οὐχ ἥμαρτον LXX.Judg.11.27, compare 6.18; also ἔσται πᾶς ἀποκτενεῖ με LXX.Gen.4.14.}}

2259:
** ἡνίκᾰ** [ῐ],
_Doric dialect_ **ἁνίκα** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.24, +others, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.41}}; _Aeolic dialect_ **ἄνικα** {{Refs|[prev. author] 29.33:}} — _adverb_ of Time, _relative_ to τηνίκα, **at the time when**, usually with past tenses of _indicative_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.198 (nowhere else in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus), 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus [same place], 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1144, +others, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.73, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 607, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.4, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 509e, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.78, etc.}}; ἡνίκ᾽ ἐχρῆν δύνειν, νῦν ἄρχεται ἡδύνεσθαι Timo17: with _present_ _indicative_, ἡνίκαπερ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.8.9; μετὰ τὴν ζωὴν.. ἡνίκ᾽ οὐκ εἰσίν 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Morte - Papiri Ercolanesi” 36}}; rarely **while**, ἡ. ἦν ἔτ᾽ ἐν φάει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 726.}}
**2** ἡνίκ᾽ ἄν, like{ὅταν}, with Subj., of _future_ time, **whenever**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 880, “OT” 1492}}: to denote repeated occurrence in _present_ time, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 310, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1179, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.4, etc.}}; ἄν should be restored in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 304.7}}; ἄν goes with Verb in ἡνίκ᾽ ἂν ὠφέλει **when** it would have been to his advantage, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 29.16}}; but ἡνίκ᾽ ἂν ἀνέβη ἡ νεφέλη, of repeated occurrence, {{LXX|LXX.Exo.40.36(30).}}
**3** with _optative_ in indirect speech, or to denote an uncertain or repeated occurrence in past time, **whenever**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 705 (Lyric poetry): also in indirect speech, of future time, ἡνίκα.. ἀπείη}} **when** he should have been absent, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tr.” 164}}; but ἡνίκ᾽ ἂν ἡμεῖς μὴ δυναίμεθα, with implied protasis, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.31; and so ἡνίκ᾽ καταλαμβάνοιμι, εἰ τυγχάνοιμι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 11.14.}}
**4** with _genitive_, ἡ. τοῦ χρόνου **at which point** of time, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 12.35.}}
**5** ἡ. χρὴ φλεβοτομεῖν **in which case**.., {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus “Synopsis” 6.13.}}
**6** after Verbs of knowing or remembering, οὐ μνημονεύεις.. ἡνίκα..; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1273; οἶδ᾽ ἡνίκ᾽ Αἴας εἷλκε Κασάνδραν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 70, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 815.}}

2261:
** ἤπῐος**, α, ον,
also ος, ον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 407, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 53, etc.}}
**1** of persons, **gentle, kind**, πατὴρ ὣς ἤπιος αἰεί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.770, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.47, 234}}; of a monarch, ἀγανὸς καὶ ἤ{{Refs 8th c.BC+|. [prev. work] 230, 5.8. compare 14.139; ἡνίοχος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.281: with _dative_ _person_, ἐθέλω δέ τοι ἤπιος εἶναι 8.40, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.337, etc.; ἤ. ἀνθρώποισι καὶ ἀθανάτοισι θεοῖσι 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 407; ἠπιώτερος τοῦ πατρός 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.92.ζ}}; of the gods, σωτῆρας.. ἠπίους θ᾽ ἡμῖν μολεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 738; θεὸς ἀνθρώποισιν -ώτατος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 861; compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 879 (Lyric poetry); ἐχίδνης οὐδὲν -ωτέρα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 310}}; οὐδέ πω ἤπιος **appeased**, {{NT|[prev. author] “Med.” 133: later in Prose, NT.1Thes.2.7, NT.2Tim.2.24.}}
**2** of feelings, words, etc., εἴ μοι κρείων Ἀγαμέμνων ἤπια εἰδείη had **kindly feeling** towards me, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.73; ὁμῶς δέ τοι ἤπια οἶδε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.405, compare 15.557; ἤ. δήνεα οἶδε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.361}}; μῦθος ἤ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.327}}; ὀργαί, φρένες, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 53, “fragment” 362.6; πρὸς τὸ -ώτερον καταστῆσαί τινα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.59.}}
**3** of heat and cold, **mild, less intense**, τὸ πνῖγος -ώτερον γέγονεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 279b, compare “Ti.” 85a (_comparative_)}}; ἠπιώτεραι αἱ θέρμαι, of a fever, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 7.1}}; τὰ τοῦ πυρετοῦ ἤπια{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 5.73; αἰθέριον πῦρ ἤ. ὄν 4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 8.57}}; of river-currents, -ώτερα ῥεύματα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Meno Medicus “Iatrika” 16.26.}}
**II** _active_, **soothing, assuaging**, φάρμακα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.218, 11.515}}; opposed to ἰσχυρά, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.130, compare 7.142 (_comparative_)}}; ἀκέσματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 482; φύλλα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 698 (Lyric poetry)}}; ποτήματα **soft** drinks, opposed to φαρμακώδη καὶ δριμέα, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.44}}: _superlative_, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.44 W.}}
**II.2** ἤπιον ἦμαρ with _infinitive_, a day **favourable** for beginning a thing, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 787.}}
**III** _adverb_ **-ίως**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.105, 143, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1439 (Lyric poetry); ἠ. ἀμείψασθαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.60}}; χρήσετ᾽ αὐτή σοι τότ᾽ ἠ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 495}}: _comparative_ -ωτέρως, ἔχειν πρός τινα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 56.44; -ώτερον καὶ κηδεμονικώτερον 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Pietate - Herkulanische Studien” 65}}: _superlative_ **-ωτάτως**{{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

2263:
** ἤρεμ-ος**, ον,
later form for ἠρεμαῖος, **quiet**, ἤ. καὶ ἡσύχιος βίος {{NT+5th c.BC+|NT.1Tim.2.2, compare “OGI” 519.10 (3rd c.AD), 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in Platonis Parmenidem commentarii” p.536 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus; ἠ. πούς 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Trag.” 207; ἤρεμον ἑαυτὸν παρέχειν “IPE” 12.40.24 (Olbia, 2nd-3rd c.AD)}}; -ώτερος ἐπισπασμός **gentler** traction, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.73.}}
**2** τὸ ἤ. **smoothness**, of pigments, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Lapidibus” 62.}}
**3** _adverb_ -μως **quietly**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aspasius Philosophus “in Ethica Nicomachea commentaria - pars” 120.13}}. (Cf. Lithuanian _rìmti_ 'grow still', Gothic _rimis_ (= ἡσυχία), Sanskrit _ramṇāti_ 'set at rest'.)

2270:
** ἡσῠχ-άζω**,
_future_ **-άσω**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.84, “Anthologia Graeca” 5.132 (1st c.AD(?):}} Maecius Epigrammaticus), **-άσομαι** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Gall.” 1}}: _aorist_ ἡσύχᾰσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.12}}: (ἥσυχος):—**keep quiet, be at rest**, σὺ δ᾽ ἡσύχαζε {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 329, compare 346}}; ἡ ἀπορία τοῦ μὴ ἡσυχάζειν the difficulty of **finding rest**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.49; οἱ πολέμιοι ἡσύχαζον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.4.16; ἀνάγκη τὸ ἡσυχάζον ἑστάναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 162e; τοὺς [νόμους] οὐκ ἐῶν ἡσυχάζειν ἐν τιμωρίαις 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Abd.” 19}}; ἡ. πρὸς μίαν θύρην, of a lover, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 5.166 (3rd c.BC: Asclepiades Epigrammaticus); ὁ διαλεκτικὸς ἡσυχάσει 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 2.239}}: frequently in _participle_, ἡσυχάζων προσμενῶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 620, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 134}}; ὥστε μὴ ἡσυχάσασα αὐξηθῆναι **by resting from war**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.12}}; ἡσυχαζουσῶν τῶν νεῶν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 49; μόλις ἡσυχάσαντες [prev. author] 8.86; ἡσυχάζουσαν ἔχων τὴν διάνοιαν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.24}}; τὸ ἡσυχάζον τῆς νυκτός **the dead** of night, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.83}}; ἡ. ἀπό τινος **keep away** from.., {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 5.132 (1st c.AD(?): Maecius Epigrammaticus)}}: with _dative_, **suspend work on,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|PFay. 117.23 (2nd c.AD)}}; ἀλλ᾽ ἡσύχαζε only **be tranquil, calm thyself**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 98, “IA” 973.}}
**b** ὁ -άζων, with or without λόγος, a fallacy, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.8 (_plural_), 2nd c.AD: Gellius, Aulus Grammaticus Latinus 1.2.4 (_plural_).}}
**II** _transitive_, **bring to rest**, ἡσυχάσας τὼ δύο εἴδη, τὸ τρίτον δὲ κινήσας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 572a.}}
**II.b** _absolutely_, **impose silence**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 69.6.}}
**II.2** **leave unspoken**, ἃ χρὴ λέγειν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.254, compare 2.268; τὰς ἀπειλάς 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 7.7.3}}.
**III** _passive_ in _impersonal_ sense, ἡσυχάζεται ἐπὶ τῆς γῆς **there is quiet**, {{LXX|LXX.Job.37.17.}}

2271:
** ἡσῠχ-ία**,
_Ionic dialect_ -ιη, _Doric dialect_ **ἁσ-**(?), ἡ, **rest, quiet**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.22, etc.; personified in 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 8.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1321 (Lyric poetry); ἁ. φιλεῖ συμπόσιον 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 9.48: with _genitive_ object, ἡ. τῆς πολιορκίης}} **rest from.. **, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.135; τῆς ἡδονῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 583e; τοῦ λυπεῖσθαι ibid; περί τι [prev. work]c; ἡ ἀπὸ τῆς εἰρήνης ἡ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 5.25: in _plural_, αἱ ἡ. σήπουσι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 153c.}}
**2** **silence, stillness**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 77 (_anapaest meter_)}}; especially of the Pythagoreans, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Vit.Auct.” 3.}}
**3** with Preps., δι᾽ ἡσυχίης εἶναι keep **quiet**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.206}}; ἐν τῇ ἡσυχία, opposed to ἐν τῷ πολέμῳ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.12}}; ἐν ἡ. ἔχειν τι to keep it **quiet**, not speak of it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.92.γ}}; ἐν ἡ. ἔχειν σφέας αὐτούς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 93; ἐν ἡ. διατριβειν 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 2.5.2; ἐφ᾽ ἡσυχίας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1517; μένειν ἐπὶ ἡσυχία 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 1.13.2}}; κατ᾽ ἡσυχίην πολλήν quite at one's **ease**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.9, compare 7.208, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.12}}; καθ᾽ ἡσυχίαν at **leisure**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1224, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.48, etc.}}; opposed to διὰ σπουδῆς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.2.28}}; μετὰ.. ἡσυχίας **quietly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 205 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**4** with Verbs,
**4.a** ἡσυχίαν ἄγειν keep **quiet**, be **at peace** or **at rest**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.66, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 38a, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.2, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.1, etc.; περὶ μὲν τῶν ἄλλων ἡ. ἦγον, ὑπὲρ δέ.. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 10.49}}; κινήσεων **from** movements, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 89e}}; keep **silent**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.92, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 143 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 321: _plural_, τὰς ἡ. ἄγειν or ἔχειν, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 3.114a, 11.493f.}}
**4.b** ἡσυχίαν ἔχειν, = {ἡ. ἄγειν}, but generally implying less continuance, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.45, 7.150, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.18, “HG” 3.2.27; ἡ. ἔχειν πρός τινα 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 28.7}}; keep **silent**, τὰ δεινὰ ἡ. ἑκτέον **about** them, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 58.60.}}
**II** **solitude, a sequestered place,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|hymnus ad Mercurium 356, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.21.}}

2273:
** ἤτοι**:
**I** ={ἦτοι}, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.446, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.3, etc.}}
**II** ={ἤ τοι}, see at {ἤ} (A).

2274:
** ἡσσ-άομαι**,
_Attic dialect_ **ἡττ-**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 936, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.57}}: _future_ ἡσσηθήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 727, 976, ἡττ- 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 20.32, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.42:}}_future_ _middle_ ἡττήσομαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|in _passive_ sense, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 28.9, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.3.23}}: _aorist_ ἡσσήθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 917, etc.}}: _perfect_ ἥσσημαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1242, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 697}}: _pluperfect_ ἥττητο {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.160}}: _Ionic dialect_ **ἑσσόομαι**, _participle_ ἑσσούμενος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.82:}}_imperfect_ ἑσσοῦτο (without augment) {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.166, 8.75}}: _aorist_ ἑσσώθην {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.169, etc.}}: _perfect_ ἕσσωμαι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] 8.130 (and variant in 7.9.β), 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 8.19}}: (ἥσσων):—**to be less** or **weaker than, inferior to**, with _genitive_ _person_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 697}}: with _genitive_ _person_ et _participle_, ἡττᾶσθαί τινος εὖ ποιοῦντος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.2.23, compare “Cyr.” 5.4.32; ἡττᾶσθαί τινός τινι [prev. work] 8.2.13; ἔν τινι in a thing, [prev. work] 3.3.42}}, etc.: with _genitive_ _of things_, τὸ μὴ δίκαιον τῆς δίκης -ώμενον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1117: with _neuter_}} _adjective_ in _accusative_, ὃ ἡττῷτο wherein he **had proved inferior**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.5.}}
**2** as a real _passive_, **to be defeated, discomfited**, ὑπό τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.106, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.28, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.39}}; ὑπ᾽ ἔρωτος, ὑπ᾽ ἔχθρας, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 233c, “Plt.” 305c, etc.; πρὸς τἀφροδίσια [prev. author] “Lg.” 650a: with _genitive_ _person_, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1252, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 70, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.57, etc.: with _genitive_ _of things_, τοῦ κόπου γὰρ ἕσσωμαι 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus [same place]: with _dative_ modi, ἑσσωθῆναι μάχῃ ὑπό τινων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.46, etc.; τοῖς ὅλοις 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.64, etc.; also with _accusative_, μάχην 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.47, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.320; ἀγῶνα 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 63.9: with _dative_, τῷ θυμῷ}} **to be broken** in spirit, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.130; ἑσσωθέντες τῇ γνώμῃ πρὸς Κύρου [prev. author] 9.122; ἡ. περί τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 239b: _absolutely_, οἱ ἡσσώμενοι, opposed to οἱ κρατοῦντες, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 516, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.9.β}}; τὴν γνώμην αὐτῶν οὐχ ἡσσῆσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.72.}}
**3** as law-term, **to be cast in a suit**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1242, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 482, etc.; ἡττ. ἐν τοῖς δικαστηρίοις 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.417}}; δίκην, παραγραφήν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 880c, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 45.51.}}
**4** **give way, yield**, with _genitive_ _person_, οἱ φύσαντες ἡσσῶνται τέκνων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 936; εἰ παθών γε σοῦ τάδ᾽ ἡσσηθήσομαι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 976}}; **give way, be a slave to** passion and the like, νηδύος ἡσσημένος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “fragment” 282.5; τοῦ παρόντος δεινοῦ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.37; τῶν φόβων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 635d; ἡδονῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 5.1; ὕπνου [prev. author] “Cyr.” 1.5.11}}; [χρημάτων] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 28.9; τῆς τούτων παρασκευῆς [prev. work] 11; θνητοῦ κάλλους 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 10.60; πικροῦ ἔρωτος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 727}}: with _genitive_ _person_, **to be in love with.. **, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.771f}}; of other things, ἡττ. τοῦ ὕδατος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.2.5}}; τοῦ δικαίου{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 5.4.31; τῆς ἀληθείας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.273; τὸ δίκαιον ἡττ. τοῦ φθόνου 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.75.}}
**5** with _dative_, **to be overcome by.. **, ἀκοῆς ἡδονῇ ἡσσώμενοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.38, compare 7.25; ὕπνῳ 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 13.22; τοῖς δικαίοις 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Mi.” 16.}}
**II** later in _active_ **ἡττάω**, **overcome**, τινα {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Cornutus Philosophus “de Natura Deorum” 9, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.22.6, +others}}: _aorist 1_ ἥττησα, τὰς ψυχὰς τῶν ὑπεναντίων {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.75.3; ταῖς ψυχαῖς τοὺς ὑπεναντίους [prev. author] 3.18.5, cf. Heraclitus Paradoxographus “de incredibilibus - Mythographi Graeci” 16}}; **defeat**, τοὺς Λακεδαιμονίους ἀπὸ κράτους ἡττηκότες {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 15.87.}}

2276:
** ἥσσων**,
**ἧσσον**, _genitive_ ονος; _Attic dialect_ **ἥττων**, _Ionic dialect_ **ἥσσων** (not ἕσσων) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus (see. below), 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 50, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τῶν ἐν κεφαλη—ͅ τρωμάτων” 2: formed from ἦκα (properly ἠσσ-, compare ἤκιστος), but in sense _comparative_ of κακός, μικρός}}:
**I** with _genitive_ _person_, **inferior**; especially in force, **weaker**, αἴθ᾽ ὅσον ἥ. εἰμὶ τόσον σέο φέρτερος εἴην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.722}}; of horses, {{Refs|23.322, +others}}; ῥώμῃ ἥσσονες τῶν Περσέων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8 113, compare 9.62; γυναικῶν ἥσσονες 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 680; Κύπριδος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 631}}; ἔς τι in a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.102: with}}_infinitive_ modi, ἥσς. τινὸς θέειν **not so good** at running, {{Refs|[prev. work] 105}}; οὐδενὸς ἥσς. γνῶναι **second** to none in judging, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.60}}; ἱππεύειν ἥττ. τῶν ἡλίκων **inferior** to them in riding, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.15.}}
**I.2** _absolutely_, οἱ ἥσς. **the weaker party**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 203, 489}}; οὐχ ἥσσους γενέσθαι to have the best of it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.72}}; τὸ λαμβάνειν τὰ τῶν ἡττ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.6.32: with _dative_ modi, ἥσσονες ναυμαχίῃ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.86: with _accusative_ modi, τὸν νοῦν ἥσς. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1023, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.4}}; of things, τὸν ἥττω λόγον κρείττω ποιεῖν 'to make **the worse** appear the better cause', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 18b, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 114: _plural_, οἱ ἥττους λόγοι [prev. work] 1042, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.15; τὸ ἧσς. ἀδικία νέμεις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 379 (Lyric poetry).}}
**I.2.b** **less, fewer**, ἵνα πλείω μὲν ἀκούωμεν, ἥττονα δὲ λέγωμεν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Zeno Citieus Stoicus 1.68.}}
**II** with _genitive_ _of things_, **giving way** or **yielding to** a thing, **a slave to.. **, τοῦ τῆσδ᾽ ἔρωτος εἰς ἅπανθ᾽ ἥσς. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 489; τῶν αἰσχρῶν [prev. author] “Ant.” 747; ὀργῆς [prev. author] “fragment” 929; γάμων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1354; κέρδους 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 363; ἡδονῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 353c; γαστρὸς ἢ οἴνου ἢ ἀφροδισίων ἢ πόνου ἢ ὕπνου 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.5.1; χρημάτων 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus [same place], 4th c.BC: Theopompus Historicus 121}}: generally, **unable to resist**, τοῦ πεπρωμένου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1660; νόσων καὶ γήρως 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.78}}; οἱ ἥττους τῶν πόνων [ἵπποι] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitum magistro” 1.3, 2.78.}}
**III** _neuter_ ἧσσον, ἧττον, as _adverb_, **less**, ὀλίγον δέ τί μ᾽ ἧσς. ἐτίμα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.365, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 264 (_anapaest meter_); ἧσσόν τι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.75; ἧσς. ἑτέρων [prev. author] 1.84}}; ὁμοίως τε τρωθεὶς καὶ ἧσς. Hp.[same place]: mostly with Verbs, but also with _adjectives_, ἀριστοκρατίαι.. αἱ μὲν ἧττ., αἱ δὲ μᾶλλον μόνιμοι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1307a14, compare “Mete.” 340b8}}: with a _comparative_, ἧττ. ἀκριβέστερον {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pr.” 957b8; ἧττ. εὐληπτοτέραν 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 3.43 codices: with _negative_, οὐχ ἧσς., οὐδ᾽ ἧσς}}., not a whit **less, just as much**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 181, 708, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.8}}; οὐδὲν ἧσς., μηδὲν ἧσς., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 276, 1329}}; for τὸ μᾶλλον καὶ ἧττ., see at {μάλα}.

2278:
** ἠχ-έω**,
_Aeolic dialect_ and _Doric dialect_ **ἀχέω** [ᾱ]:
**I** _intransitive_, **sound, ring, peal**, ἠχεῖ δὲ κάρη.. Ὀλύμπου {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 42; ὅταν ἀχήσῃ πολιὸς βυθός 2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus “fragment” 1.4; ἀχοῦσι προσπόλων χέρες 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 72 (Lyric poetry)}}; of metal, ἠχέεσκε ὁ χαλκὸς τῆς ἀσπίδος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.200; τὰ χαλκία πληγέντα μακρὸν ἠχεῖ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 329a, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 66.4}}; of the grasshopper, **chirp**, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 39, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 16.96}}; of the ears, **tingle**, ἠχήσει τὰ ὦτα {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.3.11}}; διὰ τί ἠχεῖ ἢ διὰ τί ἐμφαίνετα; _impersonal_, of **anecho**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Anthologia Graeca” 0.98a27.}}
**I.2** **suffer from noises in the ears**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Herodotus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 10.40.3.}}
**II** with _accusative_ _cognate_, ἀχεῖν (ἰαχεῖν codices) ὕμνον **to let** it **sound**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 869 (Lyric poetry); κωκυτόν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 866; γόους [prev. author] “fragment” 523; ὕμνους 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 883 (Lyric poetry)}}; χαλκέον ἄχει **sound** the cymbal! {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.36}}; ἐφεξῆς ἠχοῦντα αὐτά (i.e. τὰ φωνήεντα) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 71:}} —_middle_, ἀχεῖσθαί τινα **to sound** his praises, uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 75.19:}} — _passive_, ἠχεῖται κτύπος a sound **is made**, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1500. (Cf. {ἠχή})}}]

2279:
** ἦχος**, ὁ,
later form of ἠχή, {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Audibilibus” 804a30, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 27.57, NT.Heb.12.19, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Tactica - Griechische Kriegsschriftsteller” 35.3, etc.}}; τεττίγων λιγὺν ἦ. {{Refs|Callias Epigrammaticus “Aet.Oxy.” 2079.29 (ἧχον Papyrus)}}; παγᾶς {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus “fragment” 1.12; αὐλοῦ [prev. author] 2.98; οἱ τῶν πριόνων ἦχοι 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 290.24}}; of the **sound** of words, opposed to sense, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.258S.; ἦχοι καὶ ψόφοι [prev. work]1.150S.; τῆς φωνῆς ὁ ἦ. ἐν ταῖς ἀκοαῖς παραμένει 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nigr.” 7; γραμμάτων 4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 71}}; ἦ. ἐν ὠσί, or _absolutely_, ἦχοι, ἦχος, **ringing in the ears**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Κωακαὶ προγνώσιες” 189, 190, “Prorrh.” 1.18, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Sensu” 19; ἦχοι ὤτων 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων ὀξέων παθῶν” 1.5.}}
**2** **echo**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 899b30.}}
**3** Grammars, **breathing**, ἦχοι ὁ μὲν δασύς, ὁ δὲ ψιλός {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 73.}}
**4** **voice**, τὸν ἦ. εὔτονον καὶ λαμπρὸν ἀποτελεῖ {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 5.17}}. (**ἦχος**, τό, is found in {{LXX+NT|LXX.Jer.28(51).16, uncertain in NT.Luke.21.25.)}}

2281:
** θάλασσα** [θᾰ],
_Attic dialect_**θᾰλᾰμ-ττα** {{Refs|“IG” 12.57}} (but θάλασσα 22.236 {{Refs 4th c.BC+|(4th c.BC))}}, ἡ: —**sea**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.294, etc.}}: frequently of the Mediterranean **sea**, ἥδε ἡ θ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.1, 185, 4.39, etc.}}; ἡ παρ᾽ ἡμῖν θ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 113a; ἡ θ. ἡ καθ᾽ ἡμᾶς 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.3.9}}; ἡ ἐντὸς καὶ κ. ἡ. λεγομένη θ. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 2.5.18}}; ἡ ἔσω θ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 393b29}}; ἡ ἔξω θ., of the Ocean, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Mete.” 350a22}}; ἡ Ἀτλαντικὴ θ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Mu.” 392b22}}; ἡ μεγάλη θ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 73}}; of a **salt lake**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 351a9; ἐς θάλασσαν τὴν τοῦ Εὐξείνου πόντου 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.33; πέλαγος θαλάσσης 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.608}}; κατὰ θάλασσαν by **sea**, opposed to πεζῇ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.63}}; opposed to κατὰ γῆς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.28 codices}}; κατά τε γῆν καὶ κατὰ θ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 241a; χέρσον καὶ θ. ἐκπερῶν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 240}}; τῆς θ. ἀνθεκτέα ἐστί one must engage in **maritime affairs**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.93}}; οἱ περὶ τὴν θ. **sea**-faring men, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 598b24, compare “Pol.” 1291b20; θ. καὶ πῦρ καὶ γυνὴ—τρίτον κακόν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 231, compare 264: _metaphorically_, κακῶν θ}} **a sea** of troubles, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 758 (Lyric poetry)}}; ὁ Κρὴς τὴν θ. (i.e. ἀγνοεῖ), of pretended ignorance, {{Refs|10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}}
**2** **sea-water**, ἔστω ἐν χαλκῷ ἡ θ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Κωακαὶ προγνώσιες” 427, compare 3rd c.BC: Diphilus Siphnius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 3.121d, 4th c.BC: Moschio Tragicus [prev. work] 5.208a, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 16.5.4, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.83.}}
**3** **well of salt water**, said to be produced by a stroke of Poseidon's trident, in the Acropolis at Athens, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.55; θ. Ἐρεχθηΐς 2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus 3.14.1.}}
**4** **channel**, {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.18.32.}}
**5** χαλκῆ θ. **laver**,{{LXX|LXX.2Ki.8.8.}}
**6** θ. κοίλη wooden **theatre**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Grammaticus “fragment” 208 (= “Comica Adespota” 864).}} —For the _Laconian dialect_ form σάλασσα, see at {θαλασσομέδων}.

2282:
** θάλπ-ω**,
_future_ **-ψω**{{Refs 4th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Fragmenta” 258, 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 2.4}}: _future_ _middle_ in _passive_ sense θάλψομαι {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.42}}:—**heat, soften by heat**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.179, +others}}:—_passive_, ἐτήκετο κασσίτερος ὣς.. θαλφθείς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 864, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 697}}: _metaphorically_, **to be softened, deceived**, αἴ κε μὴ θαλφθῇ λόγοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 210.}}
**II** **heat, warm**, without any notion of **softening**, καῦμ᾽ ἔθαλπε (i.e. ἡμᾶς) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 417}}; θερμὴ ἡμᾶς ἀκτὶς θ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1092}}; **keep warm**, χλανιδίων ἐρειπίοις θάλπουσα καὶ ψύχουσα {{Refs|“Tragica Adespota” 7: proverbial, θ. τὸν δίφρον}}, of an idle life, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 1.37; θ. τὰς κοχώνας [prev. author] 7.48; τὴν βαίτην θάλπουσαν εὖ [prev. work] 129}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ παθῶν” 4}}; θάλπεσθαι τοῦ θέρους **to be warm** in summer, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.1.11; τῷ πυρὶ θάλψομαι 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.42: _metaphorically_, ἔτι ἁλίῳ θάλπεσθαι}} **to be alive**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 4.14.}}
**II.2** **warm at the fire, dry**, θάλπεται ῥάκη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 38, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 183 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II.3** **hatch**, ᾠά {{Refs|“Geoponica” 14.1.4}}: so _absolutely_, **sit**, {{Refs|[prev. work]3}}; θ. ἐπὶ τῶν νοσσῶν, ἐπὶ τῶν ᾠῶν, {{LXX|LXX.Deu.22.6.}}
**III** _metaphorically_, of passion, **heat, inflame**, ἣ Διὸς θάλπει κέαρ ἔρωτι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 590, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 474 (_passive_)}}; ἔθαλψεν ἄτης σπασμός {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tr.” 1082}}:—_passive_, ἱμέρου βέλει τεθάλφθαι πρός τινος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 650}}; θάλπῃ (_2nd pers._ _singular_) ἀνηκέστῳ πυρί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 888; εἴ σευ θάλπεταί τι τῶν ἔνδον 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 2.81.}}
**III.2** **comfort**, ὕπνος.. θάλπει κέαρ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus “fragment” 3.11, compare “fragment” 16.2, “Comica Adespota” 5.16 D}}.; **cherish, foster**, ἄλλον θάλπε φίλον {{NT+3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 14.38; ὡς ἐὰν τροφὸς θάλπῃ τὰ ἑαυτῆς τέκνα NT.1Thes.2.7; τὴν ἑαυτοῦ σάρκα NT.Eph.5.29; τὸ ἀσθενοῦν 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 2.4; θ. καὶ τρέφειν “PMasp.” 6 B 132 (6th c.AD)}}; τὴν πόλιν θ. **tend** it **with fostering care**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“OGI” 194.5 (Egypt, 1st c.BC).}}
**III.3** ἐμὲ οὐδὲν θ. ἡ δόξα I **care** nothing for glory, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 2.2; ἐμὲ οὐδὲν θ. κέρδος 5th c.AD: Aristaenetus Rhetor 1.24.}}
**IV** _intransitive_, **to be full of heat, vigorous**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 879a33}}; θάλψαι τρεῖς ποίας **to live** three summers, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 7.731 (1st c.AD: Leonidas Alexandrinus Epigrammaticus).}}

2284:
** θαμβ-έω**,
**to be astounded**, οἱ δὲ ἰδόντες θάμβησαν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.77, compare 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 5.84; οἱ δ᾽ ἀνὰ θυμὸν ἐθάμβεον 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.638, etc.; καὐτὸς τεθάμβηκ᾽ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1246; ἐθάμβησεν δὲ πᾶς.. ὅμιλος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1205.}}
**2** with _accusative_, **to be astonished at**, θάμβησαν δ᾽ ὄρνιθας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.155, compare 16.178; τὸν ἐθάμβεον Ἄρτεμίς τε καὶ Ἀθάνα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.50; τέρας δ᾽ ἐθάμβουν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 570 (Lyric poetry): in late Prose, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aemilius Paulus” 34.}}
**II** later also causal, **alarm**, {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.22.5; τοὺς ὀφθαλμούς τινος “PMag.Par.” 1.237}}:—_passive_, **to be astounded,** {{NT|NT.Mark.1.27, “POxy.” 654.7, “PMag. Leid.W.” 11.39; τεθαμβημένος}} **astounded**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Brutus” 20; διά τινος [prev. author] “Caes.” [45].}}

2285:
** θάμβ-ος**, εος, τό,
also ὁ {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 237, LXX.Eccl.12.5 (_plural_)}}: (τέθηπα):— **amazement**, θ. δ᾽ ἔχεν εἰσορόωντας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.79; θ. δ᾽ ἕλε πάντας ἰδόντας 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.372; θάμβει δυσφόρῳ τερπνῷ τε μιχθείς 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.55; θάμβει ἐκπλαγέντες 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 291, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 781 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.31, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 254c: _plural_, 1st c.AD: Onosander (Onasander) Tacticus 41.2.}}
**2** in objective sense, θάμβοι **terrors** in the way, {{Refs|LXX [same place]}}; **object of wonder,** {{Refs|epigram Gr. 1068 (from Gerasa).}}

2286:
** θᾰνάσῐμος** [νᾰ], ον,
(θάνατος) **deadly, fatal**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 2.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 610e, etc.; τύχαι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1276; πέσημα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1033; χείρωμα [prev. author] “OT” 560; πέπλος [prev. author] “Tr.” 758; φάρμακα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 616, 3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 103.31, compare 4th c.AD: Metrodorus Epigrammaticus 53, etc.}}; θηρία θ., of **poisonous** reptiles, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.56.4: θανάσιμα, τά}}, **poisons,** {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Mark.16.18, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.108, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 14.154.}} _adverb_ -μως, τύπτειν to strike **with deadly blow**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.3.4}}: _neuter_ _plural_ as _adverb_, ἀσπίδες -μα δάκνουσαι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.87.}}
**2** **belonging to death**, θ. αἷμα the **life**-blood, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1019 (Lyric poetry)}}; μέλψασα θ. γόον having sung her **death**-song,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1445; θ. ἐκπνοαί 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1438.}}
**II** of persons, **near death**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 819; θ. ἤδη ὄντα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 408b}}; **liable to the death-penalty,** {{Refs|Abh.Berl.Akad. 1925(5).21 (from Cyrene).}}
**II.2** **dead**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 517; θ. βεβηκότα [prev. author] “OT” 959.}}

2288:
** θάνᾰτος** [θᾰ], ὁ,
(θνῄσκω) **death**, whether natural or violent, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc.}}; τῶν ὑπαλευάμενος θάνατον **the death threatened** by them, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.275; ὣς θάνον οἰκτίστῳ θανάτῳ 11.412}}; θάνατόνδε **to death**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.693, 22.297}}; θανάτου τέλος, μοῖρα, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 906 (Lyric poetry), “Pers.” 917 (_anapaest meter_), etc.}}; θανάτου πέρι καὶ ζωᾶς for life and **death**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 9.29; θ. ἢ βίον φέρει 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 802; θάνατος μὲν τάδ᾽ ἀκούειν [prev. author] “OC” 529; θανάτῳ ἴσον πάθος [prev. author] “Aj.” 215; ἐν ἀγχόναις θάνατον λαβεῖν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 201}}; πόλεώς ἐστι θ., ἀνάστατον γενέσθαι it is its **death**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 61}}; γῆρας ζῶν θ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Secundus Gnomologus “Sententiae - FPG” 12}}; θάνατον ἀποθνῄσκειν, τελευτᾶν, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Crassus” 25, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.76.}}
**2** in Law, **death-penalty**, θάνατον καταγνῶναί τινος to pass **sentence of death** on one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.81}}; θανάτου δίκῃ κρίνεσθαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 57; θανάτου κρίνειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.14, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.14.6; περὶ θανάτου διώκειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.3.6}}; πρὸς τοὺς ἐχθροὺς.. ἀγωνίσασθαι περὶ θ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.47}}; θ. τῆς ζημίας ἐπικειμένης the penalty is **death**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.50}}; elliptically, παιδίον κεκος μημένον τὴν ἐπὶ θανάτῳ (i.e. στολήν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.109; τὴν ἐπὶ θ. προσαγαγεῖν τινα 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 44}}; but δῆσαί τινα τὴν ἐπὶ θανάτῳ (i.e. δέσιν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.119}}; τὴν ἐπὶ θανάτῳ ἔξοδον ποιεῖσθαι to go to **execution**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.223; ἐπὶ θάνατον ἄγεσθαι [prev. author] 3.14}}; τοῖς Ἀθηναίοις ἐπιτρέψαι περὶ σφῶν αὐτῶν πλὴν θανάτου for any penalty short of **death**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.54; εὐθύνας εἶναι πλὴν φυγῆς καὶ θανάτου καὶ ἀτιμίας “IG” 12.39.73}}; εἰργόμενον θανάτου καὶ τοῦ ἀνάπηρον ποιῆσαι short **of death** or maiming, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.183.}}
**3** _plural_, θάνατοι **kinds of death**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.341}}; **the deaths** of several persons, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1200, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 628 (both Lyric poetry)}}; _poetry_, of one person, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 53, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 496, “El.” 206 (all Lyric poetry); οὐχ ἑνός, οὐδὲ δυοῖν ἄξια θανάτοιν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 908e; πολλῶν θ., οὐχ ἑνὸς ἄξιος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.21, compare 19.16, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 483, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.24; δεύτερος θ.}}***_apocalyptic_** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2.11, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.942f}}; especially of **violent death**, θ. αὐθένται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1572 (Lyric poetry), compare “Th.” 879 (Lyric poetry); εἰς θανάτους ἰέναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 399b.}}
**II** as _proper name_, Θάνατος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“Death”, Ὕπνῳ.. κασιγνήτῳ Θανάτοιο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.231, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 854, “Ph.” 797, etc.; μόνος θεῶν γὰρ Θ. οὐ δώρων ἐρᾷ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 161}}; ὃν [ἰὸν] τέκετο Θ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 834; character in 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus Alcaeus}}
**III** **corpse**, θ. ἀτύμβευτος {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.439 (1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Crinagoras Epigrammaticus).}}

2289:
** θᾰνᾰτ-όω**,
_future_ **-ώσω**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1053 (_anapaest meter_)}}, etc.: _perfect_ τεθανάτωκα {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.359S.}}:—_passive_, _future_ **-ωθήσομαι**{{LXX|LXX.1Ki.14.45}}: _future_ _middle_ in _passive_sense θανατώσοιτο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.31}}: _aorist 1_ ἐθανατώθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “An.” 2.6.4, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 865d}}: _perfect_ τεθανάτωμαι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 23.4.14}}:—**put to death**, τινα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.113, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Pr.” [same place]}}; especially of the public executioner, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 872c, etc.: _metaphorically_, τεθανατωκέναι τὰς Ἀθήνας (i.e. τοὺς ῥήτορας)}} Phld.[same place]:— _passive_, **to be made dead,** {{NT|NT.Rom.7.4}}; ὁ -ωθείς the **murdered** man, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 865d.}}
**2** _passive_, of flesh, **to be mortified**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 26}}:—_metaphorically_ in _active_, **mortify**, τὰς πράξεις τοῦ σώματος {{NT|NT.Rom.8.13.}}
**II** **condemn to death by sentence** of law, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.3.11, NT.Matt.26.60}}:— _passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.6.4}}; οἱ τεθανατωμένοι those **condemned to death**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus [prev. cited]}}
**III** **to be fatal, cause death**, ὄφεις -οῦντες {{LXX|LXX.Num.21.6}}; μυῖαι -οῦσαι{{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.Eccl.10.1; νόσος 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.247 (-ῶσαν, -ώσασαν codices).}}

2292:
** θαρσ-έω**,
_Attic dialect_ **θαρρέω** (compare _proper name_ Θαρρίας {{Refs|“IG” 12.847), _Aeolic dialect_}}_participle_ θέρσεισα (variant{θαρσ-}) {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 28.3}}: (θάρσος):—**to be of good courage**, τεθαρσήκασι λαοί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.420, etc.}}; ἄνευ νοῦ, σὺν νῷ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 88b}}; in bad sense, **to be over-bold**, ὕβρει θ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.65: μάτην θ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 189d:}} — Constr.:
**1** _absolutely_, Il.[same place], etc.; θάρσει **fear not!** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4.184, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 732, etc.; θαρσεῖτε [prev. work] 600, 910}}; θάρσει, θυμέ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Sopater Comicus 14; θάρρει 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 328, +others:—in Epitaphs, θάρσει.. οὐδεὶς ἀθάνατος “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 5200b (from Ptolemais), etc.:}}_participle_ in an _adverbial_ sense, θαρσήσας μάλα εἰπέ **with good courage**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.85, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 666; κόμπασον θαρσῶν [prev. author] “Ag.” 1671, compare “Pr.” 916, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 491; θαρσέοντες πλούτου πέρι ἐρίζετε 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.49; πῖθι θαρρῶν 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 232; λέγε τοίνυν θαρρῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 243e; θαρρῶν πλείονα ἔθυεν ἢ ὀκνῶν ηὔχετο 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 11.2}}; τὸ τεθαρρηκός **confidence**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Fabius Maximus” 26; τὸ θαρροῦν τῆς ὄψεως [prev. author] “Cat.Mi.” 44}}: in _aorist_, **pluck up courage**, καὶ τότε δὴ θάρσησε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.92.}}
**2** c._accusative_, θάρσει τόνδε γ᾽ ἄεθλον **fear not about** this contest, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.197}}; later, **feelconfidence against, have no fear of**, πάντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.50; θ. γέροντος χεῖρα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 993, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 649; θάνατον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 88b; τὸ τοιοῦτον σῶμα.. οἱ μὲν ἐχθροὶ θαρροῦσιν.. [prev. author] “Phdr.” 239d; θ. τὸ ἀποκρίνασθαι [prev. author] “Euthd.” 275c; οὔτε Φίλιππος ἐθάρρει τούτους οὔτε οὗτοι Φίλιππον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.7; χωρίον 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Heroicus” 1.3: c._accusative_ _cognate_, ἠλίθιον θάρρος θ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 95c}}; αἰσχρὰ θάρρη θ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Prt.” 360b; ταὐτά τισι θ. καὶ φοβεῖσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.9}}; **venture**, θ. τὰς μάχας {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 3.2.20}}:—_passive_, **to be risked**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Philostrati majoris imagines” 1.17.}}
**2.b** with _accusative_ _person_, also, **to have confidence in**, τινα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.5.42, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 51.11.}}
**2.c** θ. τινί τι **entrust** to.., {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Marinus Biographus “Procl.” 9.}}
**3** with _dative_, **have confidence in**, τεθαρσηκότες τοῖσι ὄρνισι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.76; ἑαυτῷ 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.69c (assuming variant); τοῖς χρήμασι “PGoodsp.Cair.” 15.19 (4th c.AD).}}
**4** with Preps., θ. περί.. **to be confident** about.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 793, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 574b}}; ὑπὲρ ἑαυτῶν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 566b; διά τι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.55; ἐπί τινι [prev. author] 6.60; πρός τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 350b}}; πρὸς ἐμαυτόν in myself, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 1060.}}
**5** with _infinitive_, **believe confidently that.. **, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 668; also θ. ὅτι.. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.81, etc.; θ. τὸ ἐξελέγξειν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.3.}}
**5.b** with _infinitive_, **make bold, venture**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.8.6, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 22, 2nd c.AD(?): Antoninus Liberalis Mythographus 19.2.}}
**II** _transitive_, **inspire with confidence**, [λόγοι] οἵ με θαρσοῖεν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 19.1.9.}}

2293:
** θαρσ-έω**,
_Attic dialect_ **θαρρέω** (compare _proper name_ Θαρρίας {{Refs|“IG” 12.847), _Aeolic dialect_}}_participle_ θέρσεισα (variant{θαρσ-}) {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 28.3}}: (θάρσος):—**to be of good courage**, τεθαρσήκασι λαοί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.420, etc.}}; ἄνευ νοῦ, σὺν νῷ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 88b}}; in bad sense, **to be over-bold**, ὕβρει θ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.65: μάτην θ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 189d:}} — Constr.:
**1** _absolutely_, Il.[same place], etc.; θάρσει **fear not!** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4.184, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 732, etc.; θαρσεῖτε [prev. work] 600, 910}}; θάρσει, θυμέ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Sopater Comicus 14; θάρρει 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 328, +others:—in Epitaphs, θάρσει.. οὐδεὶς ἀθάνατος “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 5200b (from Ptolemais), etc.:}}_participle_ in an _adverbial_ sense, θαρσήσας μάλα εἰπέ **with good courage**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.85, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 666; κόμπασον θαρσῶν [prev. author] “Ag.” 1671, compare “Pr.” 916, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 491; θαρσέοντες πλούτου πέρι ἐρίζετε 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.49; πῖθι θαρρῶν 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 232; λέγε τοίνυν θαρρῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 243e; θαρρῶν πλείονα ἔθυεν ἢ ὀκνῶν ηὔχετο 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 11.2}}; τὸ τεθαρρηκός **confidence**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Fabius Maximus” 26; τὸ θαρροῦν τῆς ὄψεως [prev. author] “Cat.Mi.” 44}}: in _aorist_, **pluck up courage**, καὶ τότε δὴ θάρσησε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.92.}}
**2** c._accusative_, θάρσει τόνδε γ᾽ ἄεθλον **fear not about** this contest, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.197}}; later, **feelconfidence against, have no fear of**, πάντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.50; θ. γέροντος χεῖρα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 993, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 649; θάνατον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 88b; τὸ τοιοῦτον σῶμα.. οἱ μὲν ἐχθροὶ θαρροῦσιν.. [prev. author] “Phdr.” 239d; θ. τὸ ἀποκρίνασθαι [prev. author] “Euthd.” 275c; οὔτε Φίλιππος ἐθάρρει τούτους οὔτε οὗτοι Φίλιππον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.7; χωρίον 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Heroicus” 1.3: c._accusative_ _cognate_, ἠλίθιον θάρρος θ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 95c}}; αἰσχρὰ θάρρη θ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Prt.” 360b; ταὐτά τισι θ. καὶ φοβεῖσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.9}}; **venture**, θ. τὰς μάχας {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 3.2.20}}:—_passive_, **to be risked**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Philostrati majoris imagines” 1.17.}}
**2.b** with _accusative_ _person_, also, **to have confidence in**, τινα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.5.42, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 51.11.}}
**2.c** θ. τινί τι **entrust** to.., {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Marinus Biographus “Procl.” 9.}}
**3** with _dative_, **have confidence in**, τεθαρσηκότες τοῖσι ὄρνισι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.76; ἑαυτῷ 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.69c (assuming variant); τοῖς χρήμασι “PGoodsp.Cair.” 15.19 (4th c.AD).}}
**4** with Preps., θ. περί.. **to be confident** about.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 793, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 574b}}; ὑπὲρ ἑαυτῶν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 566b; διά τι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.55; ἐπί τινι [prev. author] 6.60; πρός τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 350b}}; πρὸς ἐμαυτόν in myself, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 1060.}}
**5** with _infinitive_, **believe confidently that.. **, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 668; also θ. ὅτι.. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.81, etc.; θ. τὸ ἐξελέγξειν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.3.}}
**5.b** with _infinitive_, **make bold, venture**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.8.6, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 22, 2nd c.AD(?): Antoninus Liberalis Mythographus 19.2.}}
**II** _transitive_, **inspire with confidence**, [λόγοι] οἵ με θαρσοῖεν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 19.1.9.}}

2294:
** θάρσος**,
_Attic dialect_ **θάρρος**, _Aeolic dialect_ **θέρσος** (which see), εος, τό, (θρασύς) **courage**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.126}}; θ. τινός **courage to do** a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 91, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 48}}: with _genitive_, **courage against.. **, πολεμίων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 647b; πρὸς τοὺς πολεμίους 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.2.15}}; θ. ἴσχε take **courage!** {{NT+8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 807; θ. ἔχειν περί τινος [prev. author] “El.” 412; φρεσὶ θ. ἀέξειν 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 96; αἴρειν πρός τι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1598; λαβεῖν NT.Act.28.15; but θ. ἔλαβέ τινας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.92; θ. ἐμπνέειν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.381; ἐνὶ φρεσὶ θεῖναι 3.76}}; τῷ δ᾽ ἐνὶ θυμῷ θῆκε.. θ{{Refs 8th c.BC+|. 1.321; ἐν κραδίῃ βάλλειν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.547}}; παρασχεῖν, ἐμποιεῖν τινι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.68, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.5.17}}; θ. ἐγγίγνεται, ἐμπίπτει τινί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 4.2.15, “HG” 7.1.31; ἐμφύσεται [prev. author] “Cyr.” 5.2.32; οὔτ᾽ ἐλπίδος γὰρ οὔτε του δόξης ὁρῶ θ. παρ᾽ ἡμῖν ὡς.. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 371: _plural_, φόβοι καὶ θάρρη 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1107a33, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 360b.}}
**2** **that which gives courage**, ὀλολυγμόν.., θάρσος φίλοις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 270, compare 184: _plural_, θάρση}} **grounds of confidence**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1281 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II** rarely in bad sense, ={θράσος}, **audacity**, θάρσος ἄητον ἔχουσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.395}}; μυίης θάρσος ἐνὶ στήθεσσιν ἐνῆκεν the **reckless persistence** of a fly, {{Refs|17.570}}.—On the different of θάρσος and θράσος, see at {θράσος}.

2295:
** θαῦμα**, ατος, τό,
_Ionic dialect_ **θῶμα** (compare θαυμάζω): (see. θεάομαι):
**I** of objects, **wonder, marvel**, in _Epic dialect_ always in _singular_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.99, etc.}}; θαῦμ᾽ ἐτέτυκτο πελώριον, of Polyphemus, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.190; θαῦμα βροτοῖσι, of a beautiful woman, 11.287}}; ἄσπετόν τι θ., of Hercules, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 961 (Lyric poetry), etc.: frequently with}}_infinitive_, θ. ἰδέσθαι **a wonder** to behold, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.725, etc.; θ. ἰδεῖν “hymnus ad Venerem” 205, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 318; θ. ἰδεῖν εὐκοσμίας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 693; θ. ἀκοῦσαι 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.26; θ. ἀνέλπιστον μαθεῖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 673, etc.}}; θαῦμ᾽ ὅτι.. **strange** that.., {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.2}}; οὐ θαῦμά [ἐστι] no **wonder**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.50}}; so καὶ θ. γ᾽ οὐδέν and no **wonder**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 99; τὸ μὴ πείθεσθαι θ. οὐδέν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 498d, etc.}}; τί τοῦτο θ.; {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 439; ἦ μάλα θ. κύων ὅδε κεῖται 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.306; θῶμα ποιεῖσθαί τι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.68; τί τινος [prev. author] 9.58}}; τινος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. author] 7.99; περί τινος [prev. author] 3.23: after 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus in _plural_, θαύματ᾽ ἐμοὶ κλύειν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1166 (variant θραύματ᾽) θαύματ᾽ ἀνθρώποις ὁρᾶν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1142}}; θαυμάτων κρείσσονα or πέρα things more than **wondrous**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ba.” 667, “Hec.” 714.}}
**I.2** in _plural_, also, **puppetshow, toy theatre**, θ. δεικνύναι, ἐπιδεικνύναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 514b, “Lg.” 658c}}; ἐν θ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 6.4, compare 27.7, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.28}}; **mountebank-gambols**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 2.1, compare 7.3 (_sing._)}}; ἐν τοῖς θ. ὑπεκρίνετο μίμους in the **jugglers' booths**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 10.452f}}; of menageries, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.213}}; of mechanical devices, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mechanica” 848a11: _metaphorically_, ἔνιοι θ. ποιοῦσιν ἐν φιλοσοφίᾳ 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.99S.}}: _singular_, **puppet**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 644d}}; **trick**, τὸ τῆς σοφιστικῆς δυνάμεως θ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Sph.” 233a.}}
**II** **wonder, astonishment**, θ. μ᾽ ἔχει ὡς.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.326, etc.}}; ἔσχον θ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 897; θ. δ᾽ ὄμμασιν πάρα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 407; θ. μ᾽ ὑπέρχεται 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 928}}; μ᾽ ἐλάμβανε θ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 511}}; θαύματος ἄξιος worthy of **wonder**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 906, etc.}}; ἐν θώματι εἶναι to be **astonished**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.68, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.14}}; ἐν θώματι ἔχεσθαι, ἐνέχεσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.135, 7.128}}; ἐν θ. ἐνέχεσθαί τινος **at** a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 9.37; ἐν θαύματι ποιεῖσθαι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 14; διὰ θαύματος σχεῖν 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 2.2.7: _plural_, θαυμάτων ἐπάξια 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 716, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 967a.}}

2296:
** θαυμ-άζω**,
_Ionic dialect_ **θωμ-**, _Attic dialect_ _future_ θαυμάσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 476, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 157, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 129c}}, _Epic dialect_ θαυμάσσομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.467; also θαυμάσω 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ φύσιος παιδίου” 29, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.823f, etc. (in 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.2.12 θαυμάζουσι is restored for -σουσι, θαυμάσετε}} is variant for{-σαιτε}, {{Refs|[prev. author] “HG” 5.1.14)}}: _aorist_ ἐθαύμασα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 772 (Lyric poetry)}}, etc., _Epic dialect_ θαύμασα {{Refs|“hymnus ad Mercurium” 414}}: _perfect_ τεθαύμακα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.4.2, etc.}}:—_middle_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “ὅτι ὁ ἄριστος ἰατρὸς καὶ φιλόσοφος” 2 (variant), 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 12.30:}}_aorist 1_ ἐθαυμασάμην variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-4th c.BC: Aesopus Fabularum Scriptor 92}}; οὐκ ἂν θαυμας ώμεθα (to be read -σαίμεθα) {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in Platonis Parmenidem commentarii” p.750S.}}; θαυμάσαιτο variant in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 3.5.1}}:—_passive_, _future_ **-ασθήσομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.105, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.41}}: _aorist_ ἐθαυμάσθην {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.12}}: _perfect_ τεθαύμασμαι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.82.1.}}
**1** _absolutely_, **wonder, marvel**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.394, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 282e, etc.}}
**2** with _accusative_, **marvel at**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.631, etc.; πτόλεμόν τε μάχην τε 13.11; τύχη θαυμάσαι μὲν ἀξία 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 777, compare “OC” 1152, “El.” 393:}} —_passive_, ὡς τέρας θ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.28}}; μὴ παρὼν -άζεται I **wonder** why he is not present, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 289.}}
**2.b** **honour, admire, worship**, once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (but compare θαυμαίνω), οὔτε τι θαυμάζειν.. οὔτ᾽ ἀγάασθαι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.203; frequently later, as 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.80, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 772 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1093, etc.; θ. τύμβον πατρός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 519; μηδὲ τὸν πλοῦτον μηδὲ τὴν δόξαν τὴν τούτων θαυμάζετε, ἀλλ᾽ ὑμᾶς αὐτούς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.210}}; μηδὲν θ., Latin _nil admirari_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.44b}}; technically, of the **attendance** of small birds on the owl, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 609a15}}; θ. πρόσωπον **to show respect to** a person, i.e. **comply with** their request, {{LXX|LXX.Gen.19.21}}; θ. τινά τινος **for** a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.36; θ. τινὰ ἐπὶ σοφίᾳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 161c, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.4.2; ἀπὸ τοῦ σώματος τὸν νεανίσκον 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Romulus” 7}}:— _passive_, **to be admired**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.204; ὑπό τινος [prev. author] 3.82; ἔν τινι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.39; τῶν προγεγενημένων μᾶλλον -θησόμεθα 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.105}}; τοὺς ὁμοίως τεθαυμασμένους [ποιητάς] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ ποιημάτων” 5.31; διά τι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.59: with _genitive_, τῆς ῥώμης 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 7.42; χάρις δ᾽ ἀφ᾽ ἡμῶν ὀλομένων -άζεται 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 703}}; τὰ εἰκότα θ. **to receive** proper **marks of respect**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.38; θ. τινί [prev. author] 7.63.}}
**2.c** **say with astonishment**, ἵνα μηδεὶς.. εἶτα τότ᾽ οὐκ ἔλεγες ταῦτα..; θαυμάζῃ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.25.}}
**3** with _genitive_, **wonder at, marvel at**, τούτου (conjecture for τοῦτο) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.23}}: with _participle_, ὃ δ᾽ ἐθαύμασά σου λέγοντος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 329c, compare “Cri.” 50c; θ. τῶν προθέντων αὖθις λέγειν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.38}}; θ. τί τινος **to wonder** at a thing in a person, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1041; ὃ θ. τοῦ ἑταίρου 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 161b, compare “R.” 376a: with double _genitive_, θ. τούτου τῆς διανοίας 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.44:—these phrases are used in _Attic dialect_ as a civil mode of expressing dissent.}}
**4** rarely with _dative_ _of things_, **to wonder at**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.85.}}
**5** followed by Preps., **τὰ** -όμενα περί τινος {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 80c; θ. περί τινος τί τῇ τέχνῃ συμβάλλεται 3rd c.BC(?): Sosipater Comicus 1.37; ἐπί σου θαυμάζω, πῶς δύνῃ.. 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 23.5.12; θαυμάσονται ἐπ᾽ αὐτῇ LXX.Lev.26.32.}}
**6** frequently followed by an _interrogative_ sentence, θαυμάζομεν οἷον ἐτύχθη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.320; θ. ὅστις ἔσται ὁ ἀντερῶν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.38; θαυμάζοντες τί ἔσοιτο ἡ πολιτεία 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.3.17; θ. ὡς οὔπω πάρεισιν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.90, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.20, etc.}}; θ. ὅτι I **wonder at the fact that.. **, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 489a; πολλάκις τεθαύμακα ὅπως.. “Comica Adespota” 22.46D.}}; but more commonly, θ. εἰ.. I **wonder if.. **, as a more polite way of saying I **wonder that.. **, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.155, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1140, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 97a; ἐὰν.. λέγω, μηδὲν θαυμάσῃς [prev. author] “Smp.” 215a; ὃ καὶ θαυμάζω, εἰ.. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.86}}; θαύμαζον ἀκούων, εἰ σὺ μὴ εἴης.., Latin _mirum ni.. _, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1292 (hexameters).}} —This construction is frequently combined with one or other of the {θαῦμα}.
**6.b** with _accusative_, θαύμαζ᾽ Ἀχιλῆα, ὅσσος ἔην οἷός τε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.629}}; Τηλέμαχον θαύμαζον, ὃ θαρσαλέως ἀγόρευε they **marvelled at** Telemachus, that he spake so boldly, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.382}}; τὸ δὲ θαυμάζεσκον (_Ionic dialect_ _imperfect_), ὡς.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|19.229; θ. σοῦ γλῶσσαν, ὡς θρασύστομος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1399, etc.}}: sometimes without a connective, ἀλλὰ τὸ θαυμάζω· ἴδον.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.655; σοῦ.. θαυμάσας ἔχω τόδε· χρῆν γὰρ.. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1362}}: sometimes with _infinitive_, θαυμάζομεν Ἕκτορα δῖον, αἰχμητὴν ἔμεναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.601.}}
**6.c** with _genitive_, θ. τινός, ἥντινα γνώμην ἔχων κτλ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.5; θ. τῶν.. ἐχόντων ὅπως οὐ λέγουσιν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.3; θ. αὐτοῦ τί τολμήσει λέγειν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.66; θαυμάζω τινὸς ὅτι.. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.1}}; θ. τῶν δυναστευόντων εἰ ἡγοῦνται I **wonder at** men in power supposing, {{Refs|[prev. work]170}}; ὑμῶν θ. εἰ μὴ βοηθήσετε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.3.53; also θ. αὐτοῦ.. τοῦτο, ὡς.. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 89a.}}
**7** with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, πενθεῖν οὔ σε θ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 268, cf. Alcaeus1130}}: after a _genitive_, θαυμάζω δέ σου.. κυρεῖν λέγουσαν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1199.}}

2297:
** θαυμάσι-ος** [ᾰ], α, ον,
_Ionic dialect_ **θωμ-**, rarely ος, ον {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Im.” 19: (see. θαῦμα)}}:— **wonderful, marvellous**, ὄσσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Mercurium” 443; χάρις 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 584 (nisi _neuter_ _plural_); [ὁδὸς] θωμασιωτέρη 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.21}}; θωμάσια **wonders, marvels**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 35}}: _superlative_ **-ώτατα** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 6.47; θαυμάσια ἐργάζεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 220a}}; ἧττον θαυμαστά, καίπερ ὄντα θαυμάσια less **admired**, though **admirable**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.974d}}: with _infinitive_, τέρας θ. προσιδέσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.26}}; οὐ θ. [ἐστι] with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 468; ἔστιν δὲ.. τοῦτο.. θ., ὅπως.. [prev. author] “Pl.” 340}}; θ. τοῦ κάλλους **marvellous** for beauty, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.3.9; πρὸς τὴν τόλμαν -ώτατε 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.152: with _interrogative_, θαυμάσιον ὅσον}} **exceedingly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 217a; θαυμάσι᾽ ἡλίκα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.24}}; τὸ -ώτατον what is **most wonderful**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.63.}}
**2** _adverb_ -ίως **wonderfully**, i.e. **exceedingly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nu.” 1240: frequently with ὡς, θ. ὡς ἄθλιος}} **marvellously** wretched, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 471a}}; θ. ἂν ὡς ηὐλαβούμην I should be **wonderfully** cautious, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 29.1.}}
**3** **disposed to wonder**, in _adverb_, ὧν οὐ -ίως γ᾽ ἔχουσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἱερῆς νούσου” 1.}}
**II** **admirable, excellent**, with slight irony, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 242a, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.113: frequently ὦ θαυμάσιε 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 435c, +others}}; ὦ -ώτατε ἄνθρωπε, in scorn, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.1.27.}}
**III** θ. καὶ ἄλογον **strange** and absurd, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 496a}}; θαυμάσια.. ἐργαζομένους behaving **in an extraordinary manner**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ap.” 35a.}}

2298:
** θαυμ-αστός**,
_Ionic dialect_ **θωμ-**, ή, όν, **wonderful, marvellous**, first in _neuter_ as _adverb_, θαυμαστὸν γανόωντα {{Refs|“hymnus ad Cererem” 10}}; ἔργα μεγάλα καὶ θ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1 “Prooem.”; θ. καρπός [prev. author] 9.122}}; θ. λόχος γυναικῶν, of the Furies, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 46; οὐδὲν τούτων θαυμαστὸν ἐμοί 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 191, etc.; ὃ πάντων -ότατον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 220a}}; θ. πλέγμα, in Medicine texts, the **rete mirabile**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 5.196: with _accusative_, θαυμαστὴ τὸ κάλλος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 110c; πᾶσαν ἀρετήν [prev. author] “Lg.” 945e: with _genitive_, τῆς εὐσταθείας 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Publicola” 14; τῆς ἐπιεικείας [prev. author] “Per.” 39: with _dative_, πλήθει [prev. author] “Caes.” 6; πλέοσι ἐσόμεθα θωμαστότεροι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.122; πρός τι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.980d: followed by an _interrogative_, εἰ, etc., θαυμαστὸν ὅσον..}}, Latin _mirum quantum_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 150d, etc.; θαυμαστὸν ἡλίκον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.122; θαυμαστά γ᾽, εἰ.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.3}}; οὐδὲν θ., εἰ.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 279a, “R.” 390a; οὐ δὴ θ., εἰ.. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.23}}. _adverb_ **-τῶς**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 633b; θαυμαστῶς ὡς σφόδρα [prev. author] “R.” 331a}}: _neuter_ _plural_ as _adverb_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Smp.” 192b; θαυμαστὰ ὡς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 960, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 943.}}
**II** **admirable, excellent**, πατήρ, υἱός, ὄλβος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.71, 4.241, “N.” 9.45}}; ἁνὴρ γὰρ οὐ στενακτὸς.., ἀλλ᾽ εἴ τις βροτῶν θ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1665; iron., πράξας μὲν εὖ θ. ἂν γένοιτ᾽ ἀνήρ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 212}}; **strange, absurd**, θ. καὶ γελοῖα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 154b; θαυμαστὰ δρῶντες [prev. work] 151a}}; θαυμαστὰ ἐργάζεται behaves in **an extraordinary way**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Smp.” 213d, compare θαυμάσιος 111; θαυμαστὸν ποιεῖς, ὅς.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.7.13; ὦ θαυμαστέ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 265a; ὦ θαυμαστότατοι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.7.10.}}
**III** **to be worshipped**, οὐδείς μ᾽ ἀρέσκει νυκτὶ θαυμαστὸς θεῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 106.}}

2299:
** θεά**, ἡ,
in later _Epic dialect_ **θεή** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Dianam” 119}}, _dative_ θεῇ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.549 codices (8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus has _dative_ _plural_ θεῆς, θεῇσι, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.158, 8.305)}}, _Laconian dialect_ **σιά** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1263 (Lyric poetry): _feminine_ of θεός (which see)}} in _Epic dialect_, Trag.(with imitations in Comedy texts, as {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 81, 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 64), _Attic dialect_ in set phrases (see. below)}} and later Prose: —**goddess**, opposed to γυνή, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.315}}: with another _substantive_, θ. μήτηρ {{Refs|1.280; θεαὶ νύμφαι 24.615}}; Παλλὰς θ. [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1184; θεοὶ θεαί τε 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 87 (Lyric poetry); μὰ θεούς, μὰ θεάς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 219c; μὰ τοὺς θεοὺς καὶ τὰς θεάς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.67; τοῖς δώδεκα θεοῖς καὶ ταῖς σεμναῖς θεαῖς “IG” 22.112.9, compare 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 206.2, 4th c.BC: Anaxandrides Comicus 2; τῷ θεῷ καὶ τῇ θεᾷ “IG” 12.76.39: in dual, of Demeter and Persephone, μεγάλα θεά 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 683 (in earlier _Attic dialect_ τὼ θεώ, see at {θεός})}}]; αἱ θεαί {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 22.661.28 (3rd c.BC)}}; αἱ σεμναὶ θ. the Erinyes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 458, etc.}}; δειναί, ἀνώνυμοι θ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 1270, “IT” 944.}}
**II** name of third τόπος, Vett.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Val. 69.12, 4th c.AD: Paulus Alexandrinus Astrologus “L.” 3, compare “Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 8(4).144. [?~X-, but sometimes monosyllable in Trag., as 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 978.]}}
